I. INTRODUCTION
The purpose of agricultural statistics is to contribute to human betterment.The purpose
of this bulletin is, to complement and support local skills and initiative in improving the
agricultural statistical services in developing countries. In particular it tries to provide a link
between specialist statisticians on the one hand and agricultural and other field workers on
the other, providing the former with a guide to the field problems and organisation which
they must take into account in their plans, and the latter with an idea of the setting within
which their own work lies.
In the years since the end of the last world war, a great deal of effort has gone into
attacking the various statistical problems concerned with agriculture in developing countries.
Though in recent years the reports of many of these pieces of work have contained
reasonably clear accounts of the methods used, know-how seems to spread rather slowly.
The justification for the present publication is this slowness of spread. It tries to bring
together these experiences and is, in effect, a third—but completely rewritten—edition of a
bulletin of which the first two appeared in the 1950s.1
The approach has been to draw on the lessons from a large number of studies, together
with more general experience from both developed and developing countries, to provide
proposals which seem of fairly general application. These are set within an overall approach
which has the aim of getting the best guidance from the available sources of men and money
in the agricultural statistics service. Plans which would result in good data for a few parts of
the agricultural field and great gaps in the rest do not seem likely to provide the best overall
guidance.
No two developing countries are quite alike in the situation which they present to their
agricultural statisticians,but there are features common to many. Many have only the results
of rather small and infrequent sample censuses to provide a description of the main structure
of their agriculture. Annual data are usually available only for export crops. Their
agricultural systems are often complex and non-standardised or the land areas involved are
huge—or both.
Local people are either not able or not inclined to complete questionnaires, so that all
information must be collected by enumerators. Suitable people for training as enumerators
are often scarce and so, too, are funds to pay them. Distances are often large and travelling
conditions difficult, which in turn makes the supervision ofenquiries onerous.
Agricultural statistics in developing countries raise problems of statistical technique and
of data processing, but these are not the most difficult problems to deal with. There is
relevant experience under both headings from developed countries which can be applied
reasonably directly. "Human" problems raise many more difficulties since experience
elsewhere can give only very general guidance. Indeed, most of the problems discussed in this
bulletin are human problems of one kind or another. Many stem from the difficulty of
communication, between one man and another up and down a chain of command or
between departments which must maintain liaison on a statistical subject. Others come from
difficulties in building up effective and economical organisations and getting people to work
within them. To give some examples of these non-statistical considerations,voting offunds
by a government for some special, large-sized, statistical project may well-depend on the
success with which the directors of the statistical services have made clbar to their political
chiefs the contribution which the data could make to national development. Routine annual
statistical enquiries may raise no unusual budgetary problems but they may stand or fall by
