multi-stage, multi-phase, stratified random sample enquiry, may fail because it asks more of
its operators than they can be moved by loyalty to the service—or patriotism, money, or
tough supervision—to give.
It has been said that the establishment of an acceptable statistical system requires
ingenuity, hard work, intelligent employment of limited resources, keen awareness of the
data needs of other professional disciplines, astuteness in the political world,and probably a
reasonable amount of good fortune—and this is a list worth bearing in mind.
Turning now to the application of agricultural statistics. In many developing countries
governments are the chief users—sometimes with such singlemindedness that they forget the
interest which others are developing. Typically, governments use statistics for various forms
of national planning—general development planning, import saving, project appraisal,
infra-structure studies, regional planning, agricultural extension programme building, etc.
Outside government service there is a great variety of demands. Those of the statutory
organisations and large private companies are often quite similar to those of governments.
For instance, a seller of fertiliser operating on a country-wide basis is interested in
comparisons between different regions in the levels offarm income,in demand prospects for
farm products, and probably also in agricultural extension work aimed at improving farming
practice. Other users may have rather specific needs. For example, dealers and transport
agents who want to form a view of how much of various commodities they will have to
handle may need forecasts of prospective crop production some weeks before harvest.
Finally there are the economic historians and research workers who are seeking statistics
either for purely academic purposes or for research studies which will later play their part in
planning and policy formulation For instance, statistical time series are valuable raw
materials used by agricultural economists to measure effects of factors which influence the
amounts of agricultural products grown or the demand for them. A relationship of rather special interest to a bulletin like this is that between these
agricultural economists and the agricultural statistician. The relationship seems now to be
much closer than it was, say, 10 or 15 years ago. Moreover, the distinction between such
agricultural statistics as crop areas, livestock numbers, and farm product prices, on the one
hand, and the records from "farm management" enquiries on the other, are not so sharply
separated now, and rightly so. They are valuable supplements to each other. Generally
speaking, agricultural statisticians and agricultural economists have a joint role in defining
problems; however,it typically falls to the economist, often speaking for the policy-maker,
to define the information required, and to the statistician to identify and obtain the
statistics which will provide it, but obviously feed-back between the two is essential.
During the ten years since the previous versions were published, the problems, the
availability of senior staff, and the sampling techniques,appear not to have changed greatly.
Among other features, there seems to have been a striking increase in the general
appreciation of the usefulness of agricultural statistics. More field staff seem to be under the
direct control of statisticians with lessened need to borrow staff from the field divisions of
the agricultural departments, and there is much more activity with farm input-output and
family expenditure studies. Finally, a number of departments have fairly ready access to
computers. The sections which follow fall broadly into three parts. First there is a review of the main
statistical needs, the general approach to statistical work which is used here,staffing and an
outline of the practical application of sampling methods. It is assumed that specialist
statistical advice will be available to those who are likely to use this manual and
consequently basic statistical techniques are not described in any detail. Some readers may
feel that these more generalised chapters could have been omitted without loss. However,
whereas in the 1950's those working with agricultural statistics in developing countries


