II. THE DATA REQUIRED
From the documents consulted in the course of preparing this manual it is clear that
much more discussion is needed about the agricultural statistics which would be effectively
serviceable in developing countries.Practice in temperate countries is still all too often taken
as the pattern, and much too little attention seems to be given to the effect ofsampling and
recording errors on the usefulness of the figures for the purpose which they are supposed to
serve. However,it is sufficient here to give an indication of the types of statistics which must
be provided for and hence to indicate the collection techniques which must be explained.
Statistics are required for planning the future and assessing the past—and the latter
contributes to the former. In development planning,statistics are needed not only when the
plan is being developed and various budgets are being evaluated but after it is put into
operation in order to keep track of progress. The planners who might use them include
governments, statutory organisations,operators of private businesses ancillary to agriculture,
and farm firms. If the latter are small they may not undertake any very explicit planning
themselves as individuals but a service may be provided for them either by their co-operative
society or by a government agricultural extension service.
Ideally one would seek for these and other purposes a comprehensive description in
quantitative terms of the agricultural sector set within a framework of national accounts.
This would require information on the structure of the industry—the size and organisation of
production units, details ofland use,numbers oflivestock, production offarm products,the
quantities sold and the prices realised,inputs tin quantity and value hence leading to figures
for the income of the sector, and the incomes of the businesses within it. Movements of
produce towards users, the distribution of the end use to export and local consumption,
would need also to be covered. In order to provide for forecasting it would be necessary to
have an appraisal of the availability of resources (for example, labour) and of response
relationships—for instance, what change in the output of a farm would follow the departure
of one of its workers? Some of these relationships might be estimated from an analysis of
time series, others by direct observation. Input-output relationships for fertilisers might very
well come best from observations on actual farms.
Where such a matrix of data is available it reinforces a user's confidence in the reliance he
can place on a specific item.In a full statistical coverage it would be possible for statistics of
subject A to reinforce those of subject B. Where the coverage is thin,however,and those on
A are lacking, one is reduced to getting what guidance one can on that subject from
inferences based on the statistics of subject B. This is typically the state of a great many
developing countries. Usually there are figures on the main structure of the agricultural
sector derived from sample censuses at ten year intervals. Data for the years between may be
available for a much smaller range of items, e.g. estimates of the areas of one or two
important cash crops derived from enquiries using much smaller samples than the decennial
census. Between sampling and recording errors it is unlikely that a year-to-year trend can be
determined with any certainty. Firm data may be available for certain marketing
statistics—for example the quantity of a crop sold for export. These are often valuable,but,
more often than not, they represent only a part of the crop, e.g. the citrus exports from a
peasant area which also supplies local stallholders. Furthermore, tIley are not usually
publicly available until after the period concerned so they are of little use to the general business public.
