In so far as much of the developing country's agriculture is directed to food production,
and in so far as shortage of food is often a cause ofanxiety,forecasts offood supply would
often be valuable. There are circumstances where it might be possible to obtain forecasts, (for instance, where food supply practically depends on a single crop) but more often the
statistical problems are much greater. Subsistence supply is a very difficult flung to estimate
and still more difficult to forecast. The distribution of output between various outlets may
change from year to year and, generally speaking, the problems are such as to make
thoroughly effective forecasting of food supply too costly for practical achievement. In
developed countries a great deal of useful guidance on the working ofthe agricultural sector
is obtained by analysis of a time series, but for this purpose reliable estimates, at least
annually, are needed of prices and certain consumer income variables.
Without the possibility of obtaining estimates of variables and trends, it is very difficult
to be at all rigorous in making forecasts,and it becomes necessary to seek direct pointers to
change rather than to depend on comparisons over time. Thus,statistics of new areas planted
to perennial crops, of new farmers taking up a crop, of new cultural practices, and similar
rather crude indicators, may yet be valuable. If good statistics of inputs and derived
relationships bearing on producer response are not available, it is important to include farm
management type records in the statistical programmes. The insight they can give into what
really happens on a farm, the availability of resources (especially labour), the level of
operational achievement,the practical limitations on production,etc., may be invaluable as a
basis for making forecasts. This is quite apart from their value for individual farm planning
and building of extension programmes. To sum up, the chapters which follow provide means of dealing with five broad groups of
needs: (a) Basic statistics indicating the structural features of the agricultural sector. These
might include statistics of land use, numbers and sizes of farms; production of main crops
and forecasts of production ofimportant cash crops;numbers oflivestock where these are in
commercial production; numbers or areas under tree crops with the ages and the productive
state of the trees; farm population, distinguishing hired workers from family workers and
those living on the farm from those supported by agriculture; and land tenure. For the
broader features of this group,it would suffice if the figures were available at intervals often
years, but crop acreages and production and livestock numbers should be available at annual
intervals. (b) Output and input data in physical terms, sales of main products through various
channels and flows of main inputs into agriculture. It is desirable that these statistics
should permit intersector and interregional movements to be assessed. They include
prices of inputs and outputs at various stages from the farm gate to retail or export
point; indicators of the structure of the marketing system,1 internal as well as for export
products,including some indication of the services offered.
(c) Records relating to individual farm businesses and farm and non farm households.
Most of the urban consumer family budgets enquiries will be outside the regular agricultural
statistical service. Farm business records should include receipts, expenditures, sales and
purchases in physical terms; labour in use with an indication of seasonal usage; capital
formation; and degree of self sufficiency. Farm household expenditure statistics should
contribute to this and focus the enquirer's attention on the sources of income and the
activities offamily workers.
(d) Other general indicators of development. These might include data on the presence
of new techniques, on farmers'attitudes to new knowledge and to policies and programmes,
but it may be extremely difficult to obtain by orthodox agricultural statistics in developing
countries some of the indicators that are possible in the developed countries.
(e) The statistical organisation, its size and aims.It is highly important that attention be
paid to building up an increasingly effective statistical organisation in addition to collecting a
