III. THE STATISTICAL ENVIRONMENT
The pattern of statistical activities undertaken at any stage in a country's development
depends largely on the statistical environment at that time. The main core of this manual is
concerned with the application of fairly straightforward sampling methods to obtain
basically rather simple items—for example, statistics of the areas of the principal crops
planted and their production, this representing a compromise between the needs and
possibilities of the present situation. Before going on to the practical details a brief review of
the main features of the statistical environment will be useful.
There are few short cuts to getting agricultural statistics in any country. One day aerial
survey and observation from satellites may provide feasible solutions to a number of
problems, but not yet. Typically, there are two approaches open to the agricultural
statistician for obtaining his basic data. A postal enquiry can be sent to the farmer(or other
informant) or an enumerator can visit him. The first approach is economical but it will not
work if the informant cannot read or write, or if•he does not customarily think in terms
which conform to statistical practice, or if he is un-cooperative. Further, the more
complicated the enquiry, the smaller the proportion offorms likely to be returned.There is
also the probability that those who reply under these conditions are different in some
important respect from those who do not. Consequently any statistics derived from this
biased return must be,to some degree,suspect.
If enumerators are used, they must be paid and provided with transport and subsistence
allowances for the time spent in the field. Only rarely can an enquiry be so designed that the
enumerator can identify the farm or field in which he is interested without making contact
with the farmer who operates it. Even where he can identify his site without such assistance
only part of the data needed may be obtainable without the cooperation of the farmer.For
example, it is possible to obtain information about the areas of a designated sample unit
which are under various crops simply by walking round it and making visual observations or
taking measurements. However, if any information is required about the size of the farm of
which this is a part, or about its livestock or the equipment used or other items which may
not be on open view,recourse must be had to a farmer contact.
Key features of many developing countries are their very large size, the diversity of
ecological systems within them, the illiteracy of many of the farmers and the small, often
fragmented holdings possibly farmed on a system of shifting cultivation. But much of the
experience in large scale agricultural statistical work has been gained in countries which have
temperate, fairly 'standardised', agricultural practices and largely European based social
systems, or in India where at least the agriculture tends to be non-shifting and where
mapping in many areas is good. A number of developing countries are physically small,for
example some islands, but seem to make up for this by the unusual complexity of their
agricultural or social system and the extreme urgency in solution offarm income problems. Resources in funds and staff for statistical work are short by any standards, and
particularly so with regard to the areas to be handled.
The agencies concerned with collecting agricultural statistics may be the Central Statistics
Department, the Agricultural Department with a special statistics and economics section,
possibly semi-governmental development agencies which have to engage in statistical work as
a preliminary to their development work,various kinds of non-governmental agencies such as
universities within the country, and visiting research teams. Within the government
organisation the responsibility for statistical work is usually clearly accorded to one or other
of the departments but occasionally responsibility is divided—for example,large scale census


