enquiries may be carried out by the Statistics Department, but annual enquiries by the Agricultural Department.
Though there is always nominal liaison between the departments concerned it is not
unusual to find very little effective contact. Sometimes this arises from friction between
personalities or incompatability of departmental organisations, more often from sheer
pressure of work which precludes anyone deviating from his formal commitment. In
consequence, departments may not be providing the support they might give to one another
by realistic sifting of the questions to be included in enquiries, by the cooperative use of
their resources from time to time, by the early circulation of data in various forms of
consolidation, etc. They may,in fact, be working over very similar areas and asking much
the same questions of much the same people.
The original informant in statistical enquiries in the agricultural sector is usually
concerned either with producing agricultural products or with trading in them or in
requisites. Typically, agricultural production units can be classified into three groups:
i) expertly managed enterprises with formal records and often paid managerial staff. They
may be privately owned,for example many sugar plantations, or they may be more or less state enterprises.
"modern" agriculture which may be peasant or family farmer operated,but is distinctly market orientated.
iii) traditional agriculture which may be subsistence or it may have a market for a
proportion ofits products,either locally,or for export.
The group i) may raise difficulties over cooperation but sampling and recording problems
are usually confined to ii)and iii).
In the context of marketing statistics the big traders and statutory marketing
organisations correspond to i) of the previous paragraph and the smaller middleman and
local traders to ii) and iii). The traders and marketing organisations can usually be easily
located and, provided they are prepared to cooperate, there should be no difficulty in
getting information from them. The other groups raise many queries of contact and
communication,just as do the "modern" and traditional farms of the previous groups.
Many developing countries have statistical ordinances which require firms and individuals
to provide information for certain statistical purposes. Often they guarantee they will be
treated in confidence. Without such legal backing information may hot be obtainable,and,
even with it, there are limits to the amount or type of material which informants will supply.
Generally speaking, statutory ordinances do not often enforce the provision offinancial and
income data for purely statistical purposes. This information may be required by
governments for taxation purposes but there are usually strict provisions restraining the
transfer of information so obtained to any other department.Private agencies attempting to
collect statistics must depend on good will and good public relations to smooth their ways.
One might expect that informants would be more ready to supply information if they were
interested in the results. In fact,in few countries is this much comfort to the statistician and
scarcely ever in developing countries. In the latter, the government and certain statutory
organisations are probably the main users of statistical material, but they do not themselves
contribute data to the statistics service, not being original producers. Associations offarmers
may be interested in statistics, and individuals and firms may corporately agree that
statistical material needs improving but individually they may not help towards achieving this
end. Firms may show resistance or apathy to statistical enquiries, either from statistical
illiteracy or from the exact opposite. Both apply in developing countries. Most individual
farmers and the smaller marketing firms in developing countries are statistically illiterate; the
informant may see no value in figures as such and could not envisage making any use of the
results of enquiries. In the case of resistance arising from statistical sophistication, a firm
may already be obtaining all the information it needs from private sources and see no point
in cooperating with a general enquiry which would have the result of making its competitors
as well informed as itself. Again, a comparatively large firm, viewed either nationally or
regionally, may hesitate to provide statistical material through fear that, in whatever form
the summarised data for the country or region are published, details of their firm which
would be useful to competitors may be identifiable.


