Most agencies, governmental or otherwise, who set about making statistical studies of
developing countries now seem to have access to expert statistical advice that can deal with
the design of enquiries. Usually experts are available in the relevant departments as
consultants, but if not, the basic technical material is available from reference books. This
does not necessarily hold for the field organisation and this accounts for the particular emphasis of this manual. In the last few years computers have been installed in the statistics departments of many
of the larger developing countries and access to mechanised handling of data is available for
big enquiries.It might usually be wise to be cautious over access for smaller statistical studies
in agriculture and it might be unwise to depend on availability of computer servicing to a
timetable unless it is known that the agricultural statistics concerned had a high priority for
computer time. It may be expected at the planning stage of an enquiry that computer
facilities will be available and(partly perhaps for prestige reasons)the organisers are tempted
to depend on these facilities. When the work comes to be undertaken, however, the
timetable results in exasperating delays. There is always some promise of new staff and new facilities becoming available to ease
the central problem of too few resources for the job at hand. In most developing countries
this potential comfort is a mirage. Even if more resources do become available demands are
continually being put on the statistics department for data for planning purposes and the
ratio ofinputs to expected output scarcely increases. Many developing countries have very few statistics for the period before about 1950,
except for quantities of produce exported or imported. They have the results of sample
censuses for 1950 and 1960, but usually the sampling fraction was small and the error
attaching to the figures was fairly high, say 10% or more. In some instances it was not
practicable to complete the enquiry in a single year and different regions were surveyed at
different times over a cycle of three or four years.There may have been follow-up studies to
provide material on the main items of interest for years between the decennial censuses but
often these are patchy and confined to selected sectors of the agricultural economy.In fact,
there may be little beyond the acreages on the estate, or large private holdings, and the
quantities marketed through statutory channels. The scope of the response to these conditions lies in one or other of the following
directions. Enquiries might confine themselves to only a few of the possible items about
which statistics could be collected,or they might deal with only part of the total agricultural
producing population. This might be done either by sampling more or less evenly across the
whole country or by planned omission of Certain regions from the statistical coverage, for
example by some form of cyclical arrangement whereby the enquiry is carried out in region
A in one year, in region B in the next year, and so on, returning after a period to the first
region. Economy might also be achieved by including in the one enquiry(which unavoidably
must demand heavy expenditure on manpower and transport), a rather large number of
questions so that it serves a multi-purpose objective. All these approaches have been used in developing countries and a brief mention of the
pros and cons may be useful at this point. Undoubtedly some reduction in the cost of a
particular enquiry can be made by including fewer items in it,but this device may save much
less than a proportionate length of time. The same farms may have to be visited and the
same land surveyed whether one crop or ten is included in the enquiry.It may be essential to
record the total of a group of crops as well as the figure for an individual one in order to get
a cross check on the field operation. Usually, significant economies can be made only by
omitting records for items which are heavy users of recording labour—subsistence crops
grown in small patches is an obvious example. The recording of a selection of farms by recognised sampling procedure is a most
powerful tool contributing both economy and precision in agricultural statistical work.
Efforts are sometimes made to cut costs by omission of parts of a country, not under a
formal sampling procedure, but because operations would be too difficult there or because
there is little interest in the locality. The general feeling of statisticians reviewing the African


