situation recently was that such piecemeal omission should be avoided wherever possible, even a rough coverage being better than none. There are many difficulties in the kind of enquiry which deals with one region each year until the whole country is covered, unless some kind of continuing local organisation is present. For instance,the sum total of agricultural activity as a whole is never known for any specified date so there is no "bench-mark" for comparative purposes. Again, such studies tend to require field teams to be moved from place to place over large distances and this invites wastage of staff who do not like to be away from their home districts for a matter of years. It may cause difficulty, too, with the language and contact between enumerator and informant. The general impression of multi-purpose enquiries is that though they may appear to economise in overhead costs as represented by travel and subsistence they are not efficient. It is difficult to control the length and complexity of the questionnaire and it tends to become overburdened with questions. This has its obvious discouragement to informants who may not be prepared to be patient to the end of a long enquiry. Further, different
aspects of the field work tend to require a different range of expertise.The kind of man who
is good at getting details from a farmer's wife may not be good at doing compass and chain
surveys of crop areas. The multi-purpose survey requires each man in a small field team to be expert in all kinds of field approaches,or a big team has to operate, which raises another set
of problems.
In practice, agricultural statistics methods that will work in developing countries seem to
fall into two broad categories. The one consists of collecting the main structural and
commodity data from family farms, whether of a modern or traditional type, and for this
purpose sampling techniques are very suitable and are universally applied;the other group of
activities consists of three sections,for none of which has the sampling approach as much to
offer as it has in the basic family-farm pattern. One of these sections consists of statistics on
acreage and similar material for the large-scale private and socialised production enterprises,
where postal enquiry is possible provided that the informants will cooperate. There is little
need for sampling and there is little trouble in field recording. The second section consists of
aspects of marketing and prices and the like, where a good deal of information may be
obtained as by-products of records made for other purposes as,in effect,statistics collected
in the previous section are by-products of the farm records of large farming enterprises. Sampling procedures have a part to play in marketing statistics, but less than in the production side. The third section includes large scale land use enquiries. Here, too, in
principle sampling may be applied but often there is as much interest in the location of the
various kinds of land use as in their total in statistical terms,and,consequently,the focus of attention may be as much on mapping as on statistics. The two are capable of being brought together in computer derived maps but not generally from the kind ofstatistics which can be obtained in the majority of developing countries. These sections in which sampling plays rather a small part will be dealt with towards the end of this manual. The bulk of the
chapters which immediately follow deal with the collection of statistical material from the
family farm.
It is worth noting that the sampling technique is used not solely because the magnitude of
the problem forces selection but because obtaining a complete census is less attractive than it
appears at first sight. Recording errors are unavoidable in all census work. Even if it were possible to ensure a complete record the individual figures would rarely be accurate and the
total would be inaccurate unless the errors were compensating, those of over estimation
being equalled by those of under estimation. Since, within limits, recording errors can be
reduced by the careful operation and supervision that is possible in sampling enquiries they
would contain fewer errors than those of a complete record attempted without proportionately greater resources. There are not many instances in statistical practice in
developing countries where a complete record is obtained. The two exceptions are
population censuses and certain enquiries which are made expressly to provide a record of the total universe from which the sample is subsequently to be drawn. These are of the


