nature of 'pre-enumeration' lenquiries (discussed below)and usually consist of records of rather simple items, for example they might include a list ofhouseholds in a village without
any details of their land or even the number of people in each.
Notwithstanding the extent to which sampling is used and the success which has attended
it in agriculture in developing countries there are one or two features which might be
mentioned at this point as they bear on certain choices to be made in the designing of
enquiries.2
Three points might usefully be made here about the results of sampling studies, even
when well-planned and executed. First, sample enquiries are usually designed to achieve a
given level of precision for broad regional totals, for example, maximum errors might be
specified for the national and provincial totals. If only these items are, in fact,required no
problem arises. However, increasingly interest is centring on smaller regions and individual
localities, and the results of sampling enquiries may be inefficient for this purpose for two
reasons. By the nature of the design no observations at all may have been taken in the
locality of interest;the selection may have passed over that area and chosen an adjacent one.
Even if the area was included appreciably larger errors attach to totals for the smaller
geographical units than to the provincial and national totals for which standards of precision
were built into the enquiries. The confidence which can be attached to the figures for
individual localities may be unsatisfactory for the purposes required,but an enquiry capable
of giving the required precision for all local detail may be prohibitively costly. Attention
should be given to the amount of'grain',in the sense in which the term is used in describing
a photographic picture, that is acceptable in statistical work. Changes from time to time in
census area boundaries, the use of different areas for special surveys from those used by
perhaps a census, and the omission of certain small gedgraphical areas from recording under
the normal sampling procedure, may mean that comparable agricultural statistics can be
assembled only for rather broad geographical areas. These may in fact result in too coarse a
grain to give a clear detailed picture. A much better result might have been obtainable had
more care been taken in choosing unit boundaries and definitions in the first place.
The two further aspects of the significance of error attaching to samples enquiry results
should also be given some weight. Notwithstanding the economy and efficiency of sampling
designs it is possible that the minimum error obtainable with the resources available may still
be too large to serve the purpose desired, particularly since the error as calculated in most
statistical enquiries does not comprise the total error to which observations may be subject.
To take a rather gross case, if the weighing equipment which is used for estimating the
production of a cash crop in a country consistently gives a reading 50% above the true one
the sampling error may be a modest one but a substantial discrepancy can be expected
between the estimated crop and the quantity which will be sold. If the error in the equipment is different at different years in the survey, or if the staff, possibly trained by
different supervisors, apply their instruments in somewhat different ways in two successive
enquiries even the comparison between two years would be totally unreliable.
In conclusion the continuing nature of statistical work and the need to take the long view
should be stressed. Each enquiry naturally attracts its own special attention. However, it
would probably be wise to think of agricultural statistical work in developing countries as
rather like the growth of a firm, where it is important to service the investment and to
obtain orders and capital to permit continued growth and development.In a manufacturing
firm the bulk of the capital may be represented by equipment. In a statistical organisation
investment is represented by staff who have been trained, by contacts which have been
made, and by sampling designs and field operating procedures which have been tested. A
firm would wish to maintain in good working order all the productive equipment and
