IV. APPLICATION OF SAMPLING TO AGRICULTURE IN DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
Introduction
Most of the sampling enquiries in the agricultural sector in developing countries have conformed to one basic pattern. Typically the whole national agricultural industry is divided into sections—possibly regions, possibly 'type offarming'areas.This is called 'stratification'. Within each of these a sample of units, of a size which can be readily handled in the particular circumstances, is selected at random. These 'first stage' sampling units might be
administrative districts or villages. Typically, it is convenient for certain stages of the work to use first stage units which are too big to be used for all the detailed recording work.For this detailed work second stage units are chosen at random from within the first stage units;
perhaps individual farmers from within villages. Sometimes a sample of fields is chosen from
those operated by the sample farmers,thus representing a third stage.Such a design is called a stratified, multistage, random sample design. The choice of the proportion of the whole
which it is desirable to include in the sample at each stage depends on various considerations,
including the variation between units at different stages and the effect on the costs of
carrying out the enquiry of different sampling fractions at different stages. Generally
speaking, for a given fraction of the agricultural sector actually recorded, a design which is
based on recording a few farmers in each of many first stage units is likely to be more costly
than one which uses many farmers in each of a few first stage units, because the former
requires more time to be spent in travelling per farmer recorded.
Once the records have been made, the process of deriving regional and national totals
from them—a process variously termed 'raising' or'blowing up'—consists essentially of saying
that if the sampling fraction was 1/n and the total for the sample is t then the total for the
whole is nt. In practice, the computation may not be done in this way, and ancillary data
may be brought in, but the statistician is dependent on the data used in the design for the
derivation of the totals which are usually the end products desired from enquiries of this
kinTdh.e actual work of recording may occupy very different periods of time in different
types of enquiry. It may require only one visit of an hour or two to each unit finally chosen,
or practically continuous contact for months, or a series of visits. For convenience each of
these visits, in which the enumerator usually moves from sample unit to sample unit across
his area, is called a "passage". 'Pre-enumeration' visits may be needed to collect data for
planning the enquiry and 'post-enumeration'visits may be made to check details in the main record.
In outline, this is the typical pattern of sampling studies in developing countries. The
concept of the stratified random sample has been well tried in practice; the theoretical
aspects and the computation of results from such enquiries are dealt with in the statistical
text books. The discussion below centres on operating the method in the field under
conditions in developing countries.
Frames
Whether a complete or a sample enquiry is to be undertaken it is necessary to know the
scope of that sector of agriculture which is to be included. A comprehensive list of one kind
or another is essential—it might be a list of farmers for an acreage estimating enquiry or of


16 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
ginneries for a cotton buying enquiry—but a list is essential. In sampling work such a list is
customarily known as a 'frame'. It is usually necessary to take over for use in agricultural
enquiries a list which has been prepared for some other purpose,consequently it tends to be
either out-of-date,or incomplete,or both.
Most frames fall into one oftwo groups:
a) those made up of human or business units or
b) those consisting of areas ofland(or their equivalent on maps).
Both tend to have advantages and disadvantages.
Amongst the commonly available human frames are lists of villages,or offarmers selling
produce to centralised marketing agencies—complete lists of farmers are rarely obtainable
directly in developing countries. One advantage of frames in this group is that they lead
directly to agricultural activity, at least to the extent that those on the list are farmers.
Further, they tend to lead there by a route which is familiar to the informants. For example,
where the list of villages is derived from the ordinary administrative activities of the local
and central government, it will seem quite natural to a farmer for an enquirer to come to
him through the local administrative headquarters. Again, practically all kinds of
information which can reasonably be sought about farming can be obtained through the
farmer if he is prepared to be cooperative. This means that a frame which is keyed to the
farmer is suitable for enquiries on all kinds of subjects.In contrast,area frames are elegantly
suitable for certain kinds of estimation where the cooperation of the farmer is not required,
but if the statistical service is later to take in other kinds ofinformation which can only be
obtained from him an entirely new approach, with its staff training, would be necessary.
A typical frame would consist of the map sheets for the country concerned.The means of
using them to obtain a sample is described in some detail later in the chapter, but essentially
consists of choosing, by an appropriate method, sample segments from sample map sheets.
When the appropriate areas are visited on the ground, the necessary information can be
collected. Clearly such a frame remains perpetually up-to-date.Its disadvantages include the
fact that there is no simple and reliable means of telling without visiting all the areas whether
or not they include agricultural activities. In a number of developing countries there are large
areas of undeveloped land without farming and it may not be at all easy to devise a scheme
which will retain the advantages of this frame but avoid staff visiting many areas without
agricultural interest. Again, though the technique can be readily adapted to directing
attention to farmers, it loses some ofits economy in the process. Moreover,viewed from the
standpoint of the farmer, particularly an illiterate farmer,the approach is a strange one.The
enumerator turns up with a map and measuring equipment,in the process oflocating on the
ground the selected map segment and thence proceeds to a contact with the producers.This
is in total contrast with the lines of responsibility and authority that are well-known in local
administration and may well raise fears of compulsory purchase ofland.
The process of location on the ground is not by any means straightforward in cases where
the maps are on a fairly small scale. It is probably impracticable to operate these schemes
with anything less than about a 1:25,000 map.There is the obvious difficulty oflocating the
field equivalent of the chosen map segment and, to judge by Japanese experience, there is
some appreciable risk that field staff will, where a margin of uncertainty prevails, move
segments to areas where recording needs less effort.
In principle, it is, of course,possible to use one type offrame for one part of an enquiry,
and the other type for another, and some rather complex techniques ofoverlapping frames
have been devised.Though it is quite practical to use two frames it may involve difficulties in
accurately drawing a boundary line between the areas being handled by each.
Of the frames actually used in developing countries in recent years the records of previous
population censuses have been amongst the commonest lists used for agricultural enquiries.
It is unusual for an agricultural and population census to be combined and, since the
population census has a more general application,it is usually undertaken first. The records,
at least down to village level, are usually good. The household heads may have changed with
passing years but this can usually be dealt with by other means. Perhaps the commonest
disadvantage is that the smallest unit recognised as an entity for population census purposes


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 17
which may differ considerably in size from unit to unit. It is, therefore,usually essential to
provide for the grouping of these units within a reasonably standard "primary sampling
unit". The provisions must also be made for a substantial expenditure of time in locating the
primary units on the ground. A unit may still exist in complete conformity with the census
record of a few years back, but there may be disagreement on the part of the inhabitants
about its boundaries. There is,however, a special risk here in that, whereas originally the
whole area might have been covered by a complete population census, a use of the list as a
frame may mean that only a single village or district in a locality may be included in the
agricultural enquiries. If the inhabitants of only that district are consulted about boundaries
they may indicate positions which are different from those corresponding to the records of
the census. It means that the authorities on all sides of a chosen sample unit may have to be
consulted before its boundaries are cleared. Otherwise an error might be introduced if it
were assumed for purposes of'raising' that the relation between the unit as recorded and the
country as a whole was equal to the ratio between the census record and the country total.
Rather cruder problems have arisen from time to time; for instance the original field
records for a census have, on occasion, been sent for tabulation outside the country
concerned and have not been returned;blocks offield records have been lost in moving;and
necessary identification data may have been lost though the record books were retained.
Among other fairly comprehensive and serviceable frames which have been used from
time to time are various kinds of tax lists and more general administrative lists. Typically,
local administration provides for some kinds of hierarchy of unitsleading perhaps from the
whole country to provinces and sub-provinces, then to districts and sub-districts, possibly to
parishes. The smallest unit may consist ofcommunities of a few hundred people.Generally,
such lists are reasonably complete and reliable down to the lowest or perhaps the next but
one level. Here, too, it may not be possible to locate the boundaries of the lowest
administrative unit without local enquiry. Tax lists were formerly much used in default of
other frames and they have the advantage of providing a line of contact which is generally
familiar to most of the people concerned.The disadvantages include the odium attaching to
taxation, the fact that widows and old men and other minorities are probably excluded from
the list although they may be still farming, that there are multiple taxpayer families, and
that the final units are often ofvery unequal size.
It is best, when starting out on agricultural statistical work in developing countries, to
begin with the clear realisation that there is no hope of finding good frames ready made.
They have to be built up by painstaking work.Such lists as are available from various sources
should be taken, and an organised effort made to check and develop them either completely
so that there is a record of the whole of the relevant universe,or partially by methods that
are outlined below. This approach, namely that a frame is something to be built and kept in
continual repair, representing a valuable capital asset, is essential for good statistical work.
As a pointer to what might be possible in particular situations, some less usual bases for
sampling might be noted which have been used in developing countries in recent years.
In Libya a study of tomato production was based on the lists obtained from processing
plants of the holdings having contracts with them.Incidentally,the sample size was based on
the quantity contracted by the various holdings. Sheikships were used in an enquiry into
durra production in the Gedaraf area in the Sudan.In Malawi those selling cotton at buying
points were interviewed, a card completed giving the size of holding,labour used,and other
crops grown, and these cards provided the frame for a sample enquiry. A tea survey in
Uganda used as a frame the list of those to whom tea seedlings had been issued in a
development programme. The lists of areas for which agricultural instructors are responsible
have been used as a frame for a crop estimating study in Ceylon and a number of other
countries. In principle this ought to have the advantage that each such unit should represent
about the same volume of agricultural activity.
Care must always be taken that the checking offrames is not carried out in too narrow a
manner. Thus it may be necessary to make a systematic and broad-ranging check of
exceptions to the content of any frame, and in doing so it might be well to explore the
matter with a wide range of experts in agriculture in the neighbourhood,not least those with


