the frames referred to above,except the area frames.In Iran for example,some large private
farms which were not in villages had to be listed separately and brought in to the coverage of
the enquiry. Absentee landlords who farm by hired labour would be missed if sampling is,in
effect, from a resident farmers list in whatever way it may be built up.If they have tenants
in occupation and these are treated for the purposes of the enquiry as farm operators then
the land would be correctly represented. In general it is probably worthwhile in preparing
frames to make out a list of all large farms and their locations even if many can be later
deleted as already covered in the standard frame being used.
The approaches outlined in the previous paragraphs should result in a serviceable frame
being available. It may be that, initially, only the skeleton is actually prepared but the steps
for filling in the details should be clear for completion during pre-enumeration work. The process of designing an enquiry requires the organiser to weigh the facilities he has
available to carry out the enquiry, including the amount of staff time available, the
supervision and other skills, the amount of transport and the facilities for tabulation. The planning process first requires a general review of the kinds of procedure which are open(for
instance random or systematic sampling, simple or stratified sampling), the hierarchy of
sampling units that the frame involves, whether equal or unequal sampling fractions are to be
used, and what method is to be used for raising the sample material to national level. It is
against this general background that choice of procedures will be made and steps taken to
develop strata and select samples.
Stratification
In statistical terms the aim in choosing a basis for stratification is to get as big a variation
as possible between the mean values for the various strata of the item being recorded.This is
helpful because this inter-strata variation can be 'analysed out' of the total variation shown
by the individual records when assessing the 'error' attaching to the estimate for the country
as a whole. Thus in a sample scheme to estimate maize area per farm,and hence total maize
area, suppose that the country is divided into climatic zones and a random sample offarms
drawn from within each. If maize area per farm is very different in some zones from others
the variation in the mean values of maize area per farm between strata will be large. When
the source of variation is removed from the total variation it will represent a valuable
reduction in error.'
Obviously, if several items are being recorded one basis of stratification may not be
equally effective in error control for each. For example, the area per farm of a particular
crop may differ considerably between climatic zones, but equipment per farm may show
little inter-zone variation but a considerable variation between strata based on size offarm.
Happily no bias is introduced if stratification procedure proves not to be successful in this
aim, only a smaller control of variance will be achieved than was hoped. The commonest
bases of stratification in the experience of developing countries appears to be (a)
administrative units,(b)ecological/agricultural zones,(c)village or farm sizes. These are not
intended to be in order of preference since often they are used in combination. The
advantage of stratification is usually quite clear, e.g. a Lesotho enquiry found that
stratification by administrative units gave a gain in precision of 12% and stratification by
ecological zones a gain of 25%.
In principle, one would expect that the best gain from stratification would come if the
strata could be some kind of ecological or agricultural zone. These are usually well known,in
