second—perhaps more important—fairly general qualitative information may be perfectly
adequate as the basis of a stratification *scheme which will achieve very useful improvements in precision.
Design
Some examples of sampling procedures which have been used in developing countries
may illustrate techniques better than a general exposition. A very straightforward example is
that of an enquiry in Turkey for which the frame consisted of cards, one for each village,
which had been prepared for the 1960 census. Each card showed the total population, the
geographical location,and the main economic activities. For technical reasons villages greater
than 5,000 in population were dealt with by a separate procedure and the cards were
removed. The remaining cards were then sorted into nine strata representing the agricultural
regions. Within each of these nine strata the cards were sorted by economic activities. Within
these, villages were arranged by province as an administrative convenience to get
neighbouring groups as close together as possible in the records. Within each province these
were distributed in order of population. Finally it had been estimated that the required
sampling units would include about 39 persons, so the number of sampling units to be
assigned to each village was obtained by dividing the population in 1960 by 39. The
accumulated numbers of sampling units were listed for the whole of each province and the


