required proportion selected systematically with a random start.
In a small farm survey in Malawi all villages in an area were assigned to one or other of 58
equal strata according to the size of village and ecological zones. One village was then chosen
from each stratum and 21 families from each village for final interviews.
In a study of date production in U.A.R. the frame was built up by pre-enumeration
enquiries which took the form of the preparation of sketch maps of trees in the orchard
areas of the villages concerned. These individual plantations—which varied considerably in
size from owner to owner—were combined to give units of similar size—actually of 250-350
bearing trees. This standardisation of size was essential to improve the efficiency of
sampling. One-fifth of these units were then chosen at random for the final survey. In an enquiry in the same area on citrus production much the same procedure was followed except that the villages were divided into those above and those below a certain area of citrus production. In the Sudan a frame consisting of sheikships was divided into three strata on the
following bases. One consisted of those near Gedareftown of,in the main,big holdings with
some degree of mechanisation and a high degree of durra production.This,in fact,included
nearly 340 sheikships with 18,000 cultivators. From this, 18 sheikships were chosen and 6
cultivators per sheikship. The second stratum was made up ofsheikships in the hilly area and
the third of those which remained. A similar sampling procedure was followed in each.The
working procedure consisted of preparing cumulative lists of cultivators from the sheikships
and drawing a sample from this list. In a Kenya enquiry, districts were stratified by agricultural divisions and within these
divisions smaller administrative units were selected at random—perhaps the simplest of
stratification procedure. Some parts, however, had records which permitted sampling
systematically from within land consolidation registers. An example of an even simpler
procedure was provided by a groundnut enquiry in Gezira,Sudan,which consisted ofsimple random sampling of cultivating tenants from the block records of the operating agencies.
This was a case where the volume of production to be handled was heavy and niceties of
design had to give way to pressure of time. Moreover,the ground layout was such as to make
agricultural production fairly consistent over the whole area and the gain for stratification
might therefore be expected to be slight.(One might contrast this kind of situation with its
complete records and fairly standardised system of production with the very difficult problems set by non-static agriculture—e.g. the problem of estimating the crops of a
semi-nomadic people who move to a good soil at the cultivation seasons, and then live in
encampments which are largely hidden in high bush. It is very difficult to devise an estimating technique which will handle this kind of situation). These outlines in the preceding few paragraphs are concerned with human based frames.
A fairly typical type of enquiry using area frames is that which disposes a 2.25 acre sample
unit, of square shape, per half square mile grid on maps. The location within each half
square mile unit is determined by random coordinates. This area of slightly over 2 acres in a number of enquiries of this kind would embrace perhaps 10 to 15 separate plots of crops.It usually means that maps appreciably larger in scale than 1:10,000 are needed for satisfactory
operation of this kind ofenquiry,even iffacilities are available for enlarging the maps. The possibility has been explored of using 1:25,000 maps with the assistance of aerial photographs to divide up the ground into segments which are clearly identifiable by reference to roads, streams and other physical and generally immovable features. These are then coded on the map and a sample drawn from them by any suitable procedures. The chosen 1:25,000 sectors are then expanded photographically to twice this size and sent to the field staff concerned for sketching in the plots found upon them and identification of the crops being grown. Cluster sampling might be included in this general group,too.In this procedure groups of points are located according to a formal sampling procedure and steps taken to identify the crop on the land where each point falls. The number of points falling on the several crops then indicates the proportion of the total area which is occupied by the various crops. There is some experience of using clusters within about 1.5 to 2 hectares for this purpose. The


