advantages of points over grid is that only identification at these few selected points is
needed, and recording of areas within selected sample quadrats is avoided. The purpose
of grouping points in a moderately confined space rather than scattering them more or less
at random across the whole of an area, is that the travelling costs are appreciably reduced,
even though the precision, for a given number of points examined,may be somewhat lower.
It is, of course, entirely possible, as mentioned under the discussion of frames, that a
different sampling procedure might be used for one part of the country from that used for
another.
It is, in principle, desirable to provide for an 'interpenetrating'sampling procedure when
drawing up the design of a large enquiry. A typical procedure might be as follows. Suppose
that primary sample units are to be selected within each of a number of administrative
districts. When drawn each unit will be assigned in respect of district A to group Al or A2;
for district B to groups Bl,B2; district C,groups Cl,C2; and so on. When organising field
work, one team will be assigned to samples Al and Bl, another team B2 and C2, and
another team Cl and A2.Thus,in each district there are two estimates of the situation from
two independent observers. This provides a means of making some check on the accuracy of
the individual observers. It also gives the opportunity to halve the size of the enquiry after
the design stage has been reached without upsetting the balance of the enquiry. Such
curtailment of scope is not desirable but is occasionally forced on the organiser by difficulty
over staff or problems in field operations.
Arrangementsfor drawing samples
The physical arrangements for drawing samples vary with the situation almost infinitely.
A simple case has been noted above where the records were available in card form which
could be sorted physically into the respective strata and sub-strata. A somewhat similar case
may arise where record books from censuses are available. Care must be taken in the
planning stage to find out what is the actual physical location of records and arrangements
made for decentralising the sampling at lower stages in the hierarchy. It is probably worth
while, unless the cost would be prohibitive,to get the process of selecting sample villages and
sample farmers completed in a round of visits in advance ofthose necessary for making the
actual observations. This means sending field staff to the appropriate lower stage units where
relative centralisation of records ceases. Local information can then be obtained to supply
the frame for that particular stage of the enquiry and the instructions for sampling can be
carried out and farmers identified. This allows instructions for dealing with the selection of
reserve sample units to be carried out, uncomplicated by instructions relating to actual
enumeration. Each of these may present sufficient problems for the field staff to occupy all
their attention and the attention of the supervisors without tangling the two.
It is usually especially important at this stage to give attention to the non-resident
farmers, to small farmers, to joint operators (for example, brothers in matrilineal societies)
and to districts on the edges of towns.




