V. STAFF
Introduction
In very broad terms the agencies with which we may be concerned in agricultural
statistical work include: a) government central statistics departments, b)government agricultural departments, especially those sections of them concerned with
agricultural economics, and with agricultural extension,
c) private research agencies, principally academic.
The work which has to be dealt with, again in broad terms,includes
a) the planning of enquiries, b)the field management, c) field supervision,
d)tabulation and editing work on current records,
e) the tabulation and summarisation of final records.1 The kinds of staff concerned in this work include a) highly trained professional statisticians and statistician-economists, with interests spread
across all kinds ofstatistical work,without any necessary specialisation in agriculture,
b) working statisticians and economists with experience of the general management of
statistical enquiries, c) extension staff of agricultural departments with special skills in agricultural fields and
with particular local knowledge, d)field supervisory staff of a kind trained within the department,
e) various categories of field enumerators and recorders.
Again in very broad terms, there are four ways in which organisations, particularly
government departments, can obtain the staff they require. They may
a) establish a permanent body of staff which they will move from place to place and from
project to project as required, b)borrow staff from other departments for a limited period; generally this means that a
section with statistical responsibilities borrows the service of extension staff,
c) appoint staff for a particular enquiry and discharge them when it is over,
d)train staff for a particular project, and perhaps in some other basic skills, use them
intermittently but pay them a retaining fee to keep them in touch. The majority of the discussion which follows assumes that, wherever possible, staff will
be engaged on a permanent basis and will increase in value as they gather experience to the
steady improvement of agricultural statistics.
However there are occasions where other expedients must be resorted to. The use of
agricultural extension staff for statistical enquiries seems less common now than a decade
ago. It is only to be expected that the extension department, with its own programme
expected of it, will be reluctant to divert staff to time-consuming field studies. It generally
seems easier to arrange for liaison and joint workings in the early days after setting up of an
extension service and possibly it is in these conditions that circumstances are likely to press
the statisticians into wanting to use them. Generally,it is probably best to,think in terms of
their helping with problems of stratification, local identification of agricultural zones and
