work. These, however, are all expedients. Statistical work, at almost all levels, should be regarded as a career in itself or at least an important stage in progress to a career, and
permanency should be the aim. Furthermore, the aim of an organisation setting out to develop sound statistical work should be to get men with good training on to the established staff, bringing into establishment men who have proved themselves in temporary work,and gradually building up a reliable, well-founded body of statistical expertise. It is as important
to have this available at the lower level of the service as at the higher. The amount of staff required for the various stages of study can only be determined by calculation of the work to be done in the light of the field conditions in which it has to be done.Some of this will emerge in the course of the details of techniques in later chapters but two points might be made of a general nature here. •
First there is the very great importance of designing enquiries so that they are immune to
variations in senior staff. Sometimes it is unavoidable that one staff member should be
primarily responsible for organising and executing but,quite often. more risks of disruption
if he is not available are incurred than are necessary. One safeguarding action is to make sure
that a careful diary of the whole project is maintained with a complete record of instructions
and actions undertaken. Another is to arrange, wherever possible, that the Director takes
into his active confidence one or two other Senior staff members, if not those directly
involved with the working of the enquiry then those which are directly involved in the
department's responsibility for this kind of work. If the main Director leaves,it is then still
possible to keep the momentum of an organisation going until new arrangements can be
made. To deal with a section which has "run-down" because ofloss of drive is almost worse
than having to build up a section where none existed before. These risks occur at all levels
but the farther up the scale of responsibility one goes the greater the risks and the greater
the opportunities for safeguarding them to some degree.
The second point that is worth emphasising at this stage is the paramount need to provide
for sufficient office staff for editorial scrutiny, current tabulation, and final tabulation.
Attention tends to centre on the field work because the risks of hold-ups from local
obstruction, transport problems, and so on force themselves upon the planners' attention.
However,if there is not the capacity to handle the processing of any data as soon as it comes
in, then it is impossible to keep up to date with the checking of errors. This has two grave
consequences. One is that when tabulation is finally undertaken at some later stage,a large
number of forms or questionnaires will have to be either arbitrarily edited or abandoned
because of inaccuracies or uncertainties.Perhaps more important is the fact that if errors are
not followed up straight away the valuable stimulus for field staff provided by the
knowledge that someone cares about the material they send in is lost.
Scarcely less important than the need to keep abreast of current office work is the need
to provide for adequate tabulation facilities for getting the material into publishable form in
a reasonable time. If a great delay ensues between the conduct of the enquiry and the
availability of the results, not only is useful guidance not available but the goodwill is
foregone at least amongst the agencies and firms who might find the data useful.
The calculation of requirements for these various stages will depend in part on what
facilities are available for handling tabulations, whether they have to be done on desk
