In principle, the staff who are concerned with the field recordings up to field supervisor
level might operate either as centrally based teams which can be sent where needed within
the whole country, or they may be based in their own home localities. Naturally,
locally-based people are more liable, unless there is a substantial body ofenquiries going on,
to be used intermittently, but in many countries now there is so much work in hand that it
is possible to think of staff members in permanent employment on statistical work living in
their home areas. Among the advantages of using people in their home bases are
a) travelling costs should be reduced
b)there is no language problem and no problem in introducing the staff to the community,
c) they are well placed to know if people are omitted from local frames or fields from
local farmers' records
d)if they have some skills they should be in a position to help people in the locality in the
course of non-survey activities and thus increase the public goodwill.
On the other hand
a) such arrangements are inflexible: well-trained staff may need to be used more fully than
is feasible in their own locality
b)if the field worker has any feature attached to him which makes him disliked in the
community then this will affect his statistical work c) it is difficult to maintain discipline amongst reporters based in their own area since if
a man is dismissed he is there in the path of the successor and,even if he is not actively obstructionist, the new man has difficulties to overcome.
The decision in a particular case must rest on a balance of advantages and disadvantages
and the best approach may be to concentrate on guarding against the more acute sources of
embarrassment. For example it may be possible to get the advantages of local knowledge
without the disadvantages of local obstructionism by appointing someone from a locality
at a little distance from the one in which he has to work.This could also have the advantage
that he is not in the village at times when he is not required for actual observational work.
It is at such times that sources of friction tend to be built up.
Selection
With rising academic standards in all countries, it is likely to be possible to have fairly
objective means of assessing whether an applicant is educationally of sufficient standard for
the work required However,if there is any doubt about this a test which includes:
an interview (to get some idea of whether the man is in the least suitable for the post);
dictation (to see whether he can take down a simple passage and record reasonably
effectively); a short essay on some familiar subject (to indicate whether he can marshal his ideas
sensibly); an arthimetic test suited to the kind of information he is likely to have to handle,
giving particular weight not only to the correctness of the answer but on the
methodical way in which this problem is attacked and worked through.
memory tests (to show that he can make a reasonable attempt at renjembering the
kinds ofthings which are to be seen around a farm homestead,for example).


