It should be remembered that suitable tests of these kinds are probably readily available from educational departments in the country concerned.
Once employed,staff may have to turn a hand to a range of types of work but it might be
worth distinguishing, broadly, two groups, namely the repetitive enquiry in which a team
moves from one village to another over quite a considerable area, and the arrangement in
which the enumerator stays within a village for some considerable time, perhaps even for a
full year. Only rarely can any kind of field statistical work be undertaken without contact
with human beings, and reasonable tact and respect for the social courtesies of the
communities concerned are essential characteristics. For the travelling,enumerator good
health and a certain persevering toughness,intellectual honesty and a capacity for hard work
are needed if he is to make a good showing, month in and month out, under conditions
which are often rather arduous. Resident enumeration staff and, to some extent, the
supervisory staff of such enquiries, have a special need for that tact and innate decency
which can allow them to stay on and be reasonably welcome in a community when
operating a life which is quite different from that of the local people. They need to be as
unobtrusive as is consistant with getting the necessary attention and,perhaps above all, they
need to avoid taking advantage of their apparent leisure to disrupt the household and
farming operations of the people with whom they have to deal. It would also be useful,since
repeated enquiries of similar character are tedious for the informant if the enumerator could
have the skill to encourage informants to talk about their social activities and their agricultural work and be able to summarise from this flow of natural conversation the items
which are required for the purpose of the enquiry.
Occasionally, it is possible to use staff members of an established organisation (e.g. a
marketing board or a settlement agency, etc.) for field work in collecting agricultural
statistical material. It may be better for this purpose to try to use a member who is known
to informants but is not locally resident—for example,somebody who operates on a regional
or sub-regional basis and send him round to visit a number of places, even if it occupies an
appreciable amount of time, rather than to use a much smaller proportion of the time of
each of a lot of staff members lower down in the organisation's hierarchy. Whether this is
feasible will probably depend on the interest of the organisation in the results of the work.
Training
Training may be required at a number of levels. A statistician may need training in the
special skills of census work,a man with a good agricultural degree or economics degree may
require training in the main skills of a statistician, staff may need training for supervisory
duties in the field or in the office;local field staff may need basic training;etc. Any or all of
these people may need follow up in-service training to take account of modifications in the
practice either of an organisation as a whole, or of details of a particular enquiry which are
being made because of initial experience. In all cases, in the conditions of developing
countries, special care should be taken to search out opportunities for getting the best
increase in the skills required for the minimum disruption of organisation and expenditure of
funds. Thus a serviceable member of a statistics organisation,at fairly senior level, might get
a very useful increase in his own self-esteem and competence to work in a developing
organisation if he could have perhaps a one-month specialist course in some aspect of his
work at a college or department somewhere within the geographical region where he is to
work. This, with a little attention all round,could be achieved with little more than the cost
of his maintenance and short-distance travel expenditure. At that particular stage in his
working life it might be quite pointless to give him the opportunity of a one- or two-years
course, with only partially suitable emphasis,in some country on the other side of the world
(though later he might benefit from such a course). Similarly it may be a good deal more
effective to bring in field staff to some local centre that they can reach for a day, to work
them hard for that day and send them back again to their base, than to go through the
escalating costs of arranging residential courses of some length. Of course, these costs may
have to be met during the basic training for staff who have not previously had any
experience in the work required, but even this may merit close scrutiny.


