There seems to be at least some evidence that interviewers and enumerators who get a
very short training make no more errors than those who get one of two or three times the
length. If this is so, the explanation may be that in the longer course they get enough
attention to get somewhat above themselves,or that they get information which is not really
relevant to their job and merely confuses them, or that the trainers in the longer sessions
spend time on material which is of no direct constructive benefit to the job in hand.There is
also a financial point which affects particularly temporary staff. If a period of residential
training is provided, even a week, with expenses paid, there is some temptation for staff to
take it and drop out soon afterwards so that the costs of maintaining a trained staff with this
system may be unnecessarily high. The more elaborate the training planned the more
temptation there is to have fairly large numbers of trainees at the sessions and therefore to
have them coming in from a considerable distance. This is an accumulating set of
embarrassments.
For the purpose of this manual, training of staff above the supervisory level can be
omitted and the following notes relate primarily to training of basic field staff and of clerical
and supervisory staff. It must be remembered that the concern is with adults and,
notwithstanding selection tests, there will be bright boys,average boys, dull boys and bright
ones with blind spots,in any group who are brought together for the purpose. Consequently,
it should be the aim to teach only what the students want to know for their own purposes
and the instructor who is vividly appreciative of the actual field job to be done will get far
better results than a man—or a succession of men—who have mainly academic acquaintance
with particular facets of the whole training. The old saying that"you should tell them what
you are going to tell them, tell them, and tell them what you have told them",should in
most cases ensure that no more is included in any sessions than they have a reasonable
chance of absorbing.
The length of training needed is difficult to appraise because different studies have
managed quite reasonably well with very different periods for training their field staff. In
part, the amount needed depends on the amount of supervisory time that can be deployed
for day-to-day control of the actual enquiry. One might say that the basic skills of using the
selection methods required in a stratified, randomised, sample enquiry, locating the
necessary sample units on the map, preparing local frames, making contact with farmers,
cross-checking the details of agricultural information provided by informants, measuring
fields and weighing crops, etc., might be taught in, perhaps, a week to those who already
have a good agricultural college type of knowledge of the industry. Others with much less
specialised knowledge might require a month or more. However, enquiry into the tea
industry in Uganda illustrates the difficulties of generalising. In this case the enumerators
were chosen after an interview and an examination in arithmetic and general knowledge,and
were sent to a Farm Institute for two weeks training. The first week included the way to
enter information on records, how to measure plots, and use scales, the way to approach
informants, and how to deal with their usual objections; the second week was spent in trial
interviews in the morning and analysis of the results in the afternoons. In two weeks this
basic training was completed and a number of the group were chosen to act as senior
enumerators in charge of checking. The staff then immediately went on to a fairly
complicated enquiry in the field. It is essential to emphasise, however, that when the length of training is very sharply cut
it is essential to provide for steady and patient supervision in the earlier stages of the
enquiry. It is one of the losses in a very brief period ofinstruction,especially with minimal
opportunity to follow the results of the students trying out the practice in the field, that one
does not know in which particular direction they have failed to grasp the basic principles. If
these gaps are discovered early, either by the field supervisors or by the editors of the forms
when they come into the office, no great loss occurs, but if the period of training,the scope
of supervision and the promptness of editorial work are clipped, then disaster is almost
certain.
For those working in the field training should include at least the fundamentals of the
following:a) basic arithmetic operations.


