One of the pieces of experience that it is important to obtain from editing completed
forms and to store for future reference, is an assessment of which parts of an enquiry and
what kinds of question the enumerators find most difficult to handle so that these items can
be looked at specially in a future enquiry.
The training for supervisors should have a somewhat different emphasis from the training
for enumerators. Clearly they should be fully conversant with all the procedures which the
enumerator has to carry out and should be able to do them at least as efficiently as the
enumerator. They should be able to identify departures from proper practice and to give the
necessary instruction and advice for improvements. However, in addition to all this it is
important to give the supervisor something rather more than he actually needs for carrying
out hisjob and this is particularly true if a continuing and developing body of statistical staff
is to be constructed. It should be made clear to him that each of the levels in the sharing of
responsibility—the statistician, the supervisor, the enumerator—has an essential part to play
and these parts should be clearly explained in such terms as are suited to the man being dealt
with. His part in the whole should be made clear to him. He is not merely a postbox as might
at first sight seem to be the case in some types of organisation; his role is a critical one in
achieving high standards of statistical work. It is absolutely essential to stress the need for
precision and economy of operation, and to indicate the general commonsense in the whole
pattern of the statistical work. Where it is possible to give him the opportunity of a little
extra training, with the status which this brings even if it is not absolutely essential for the
work he is doing,such an opportunity should not be lost.
Staff management and control
There are two sides to this. On the one,the staff have rights and duties and,on the other,
the organisations have rights and duties, and both have valid places. The situation with
regard to salary and allowance,terms of appointment,etc.,should be made quite clear to the
staff on appointment,and it is highly desirable that the status and training of staffshould be
recognised quite clearly in the salary they get. Responsibility and extra training should be
reflected in extra pay. It is also highly important that the contact between the head of a
statistics organisation and the staff at various levels and in various geographical locations
should be effective and not merely formally operative. Some countries have found it very
well worthwhile to arrange meetings at regular intervals of all the regional supervisory staff
in the field, and the cost of these meetings was explicitly provided for in the budget.They
have been regarded as having a value in contact which no indirect communication can
supply. Again, time should be taken by some representative of the central organisation to
visit each locality regularly so that a contact may be maintained and queries thrashed out.
The file for that area should be taken out with the visitor and letters rectived and carbons of
letters despatched checked one against the other to make sure that no action has been
omitted which should have been taken and no important letters have gone astray in other


