directions. This has sensible working value and tends to avoid an impression that queries sent
up the line get lost if they are inconvenient. Where corrections and revisions of statistical
material have to be made at any stage then it is extremely important that the opportunity
provided by meetings of this kind should be taken for consultation on this subject.
Reconciling or amending statistics by correspondence is slow and unsatisfactory at best.
Given that communication in both directions is effective and that the pay and conditions
of staff are defensible—it is too much to be hoped that everyone will be satisfied of
course—then the central organisation must ensure that it has disciplinary control throughout
the whole regional and local set-up. If a mistake has occurred it is essential that it is not
passed over and, further, that it can be followed up to the point where it originated. This
does not necessarily mean that every arithmetical error or minor variation in the application
of a procedure would be the subject of a reference back down the line, but it does mean that
an opportunity should be taken to correct any general misuse or misapplication of
instructions. A clear distinction should be drawn in this between genuine errors and
'cooking' results. It is not unreasonable to dismiss instantly on 'cooking' being found, but
leniency with genuine errors,at least until in-service training has been used to the full, is
desirable. The arrangement for payment of temporary enumerators and interviewers need some
attention. It is a good policy, if it can be arranged,that an interviewer is not paid at the end
of the week until his supervisor has checked his records. This has two advantages—one,that
he will be encouraged to get his material through to the supervisor on time and the other
that there is a strong incentive to be accurate enough to satisfy the supervisor. Faults of a
grave nature can be met by stoppage of pay. More generally,if it is necessary for temporary
enumerators to be paid off before their work is checked it is important to make sure that
there is some lien on them to do repeats. This may be feasible enough in a case,for instance,
of primary school teachers who are already in government employment or students who are
attached to a college where some discipline can be applied.
The position of enumerators on long-term enquiries which keeps them in the locality for
some weeks or months is a matter of particular delicacy. It must usually be necessary for
him or his supervisor to convince the farmers with whom he is dealing, and the farmers'
wives, that the enquiry is a sensible one and to convince neighbours who might otherwise
chaff the farmer for working with the government and silence the barrack-room lawyers in
the locality. By the nature of his work he is likely to be in rather frequent touch with the
farmers'family and he could easily be at the home when the farmer is away at the farm. This
is liable to produce misunderstanding, if nothing more. It is for the enumerator, under the
supervisor, to ensure that he does not get too involved with the informants,that he does not
get into the habit oftalking to the farmer except in the express purpose of the enquiry,or of
accepting entertainment at the farm home, or talking to young female members of the
family. The social consequences of getting too involved are obvious enough but it is also
found that enumerators who become much involved with their informants are more liable to
guess what to put down than to press in an objective way for the exact quantitative detail
required.
A problem of particular difficulty faces the supervisor when his enumerator reports a
refusal by a farmer to cooperate in spite of all available explanations and tactful pressures. In
quite a lot of instances it is not difficult for a supervisor, or a more senior member of the
statistics organisation, to go along to the farmer concerned, talk to him as man to man,
making much of the farmer's importance,allowing him kudos from the attention of a senior
visitor. The exact details of such approaches vary and they may very well be successful in
getting sufficient cooperation for the purpose of the point immediately in hand. There is a
grave temptation to deal with the problems of this kind in this sort of way, it solves a
problem and it tends to give the senior staff member concerned a sense of satisfaction in
handling other people. However, it puts the enumerator who failed in a very difficult
position. It is frustrating to him personally because he was not able to use the same approach
and it spoils his status in the locality by being overriden in this way. Moreover,it is liable to
produce some friction between the people who are prepared to play with the enumerator


