VI. FIELD ORGANISATION
Introduction
When the shape of the enquiry begins to emerge a skeleton timetable should be prepared,
running from the initial planning and outstanding work on the frame through selection of
field staff, their training, allotment to bases, publicity for the enquiry and the various stages
of its actual conduct. Obviously the length and formality of such a programme will vary with
the job to be done and some parts—training, for example—may be non-existent if it
represents a further stage in an already established programme.
Public relations
Keeping the importance of statistical work before senior members of government,other
departments, statutory organisations, and private firms has already been stressed. In general,
care should be taken to avoid springing the news of any major development in statistical
work on those who might be directly or indirectly involved. There is much to be said for the
"drip-drip" method whereby the idea is introduced in an exploratory manner well before the
plans are in any way finalised and a note taken of the response that the idea evokes. This
may give very useful guidance on certain aspects of planning and may show the direction in
which further information should lay its stress. It is important that all levels of a statistical
department should foster good relations with other departments and agencies. These other
contacts may include agricultural, labour, and commerce and industry departments, and
general administration together with statutory organisations for marketing agricultural
products, and firms trading in farm products and in requisites. When the plans have
crystallised to a reasonable degree information about what is planned should
be circulated to those concerned. It may be that the proposals will have to be pushed ahead
against the opposition or indifference ofsome ofthose concerned. This will generally not be
any the more powerful for opponents having known of the plans at a fairly early stage. On
the other hand, most people will react violently at having been presented with a fait
accompli.
It is possible in some environments to present statistical work as having an independent or
neutral image, with some gain in goodwill. This is particularly true ofstudies carried out by
non-governmental agencies, such as universities and philanthropic organisations. In many
developing countries it is practically impossible to convey this picture of independence and
no great weight should be attached to achieving it.
Before field work actually begins key officials should be provided with a project outline.
They may not in fact read beyond the opening paragraphs—so it is important that the main
pattern and aims should be clearly stated right at the beginning—but it is a courtesy and it
prevents their being placed in the embarrassing position of having it first mentioned to them
more or less publicly,and having to cover up the fact that they knew nothing about it.
Those directly involved in the operations of an enquiry should receive a letter at a
suitable time before the work starts setting out the aim, making clear what value can be
looked for from the information so gathered, and stressing the fact that the personal affairs
of the individual providing the information will not be divulged to anyone,including other
departments of government. Typically such letters should go to civic leaders in the
neighbourhoods concerned, chiefs, church leaders, etc. In a number of population census
enquiries the local churches have been asked to read prepared statements from their pulpits
in the weeks before the enquiry. The statements have been graduated to bring interest to a
head immediately before the study has begun. There may be occasions where village


34 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
meetings can be called at which such letters can be read. Though the method does not seem
to have been used recently there are instances of useful information being actually collected
at village meetings. They have the advantage that everything seems to be above board and
therefore acceptable to the community and the information can be cross-checked by the
comments of neighbours. Obviously one needs certain social conditions and rapport between
the statistical staff and the local people before this kind of data gathering meeting is possible, but opportunities for information meetings are much wider.
Considerable care should be given to making contact with those from whom information
is sought and in convincing them that there will be no disadvantage to them in becoming
involved in the enquiry and indeed they will be contributing to the development of the
country. Any honest argument which has local force should be used to introduce the study.
National planning may be accepted and may form a sufficient argument; there may be
problems about getting crops moved at harvest and it may make sense to make forecasts of
the crop in order that better preparation can be made for transport; or where,for instance,a
programme of subsidising a crop improvement measure such as crop spraying is in force,it
can be argued that crop yield estimation must be carried out in order to form a basis for organising such assistance.
The people to be convinced may extend beyond the actual farm operators. Often enough
the farmer's wife is the person who answers many of the questions and she must be
convinced of the sense of cooperating, too. In any enquiry that is to last more than a single
interview it is important also to make sure that neighbours and others will not undermine
the preparedness of the informant to continue with the study. It is worth taking a good deal
of trouble to start off these farmer contacts on the right foot. In a large scale,but relatively
straightforward, enquiry such as a crop area estimating survey, the enumerators must make
all contacts. It is essential that their training should have stressed the importance of their
contact with the public and that they should do it with patience and courtesy. Where studies
are to continue for longer perhaps, involving several "passages" through the country at
different periods in the crop year, or even continuous residence of the enumerator in the
locality, much more is at stake. On the other hand this usually means that there is a little
more leisure in organising the enquiry and the supervisory staff are more ready to hand.
There is a lot to be said for each association between enumerator and informant being
started rather formally by a senior member of the survey staff.
Not only should the occasion be taken to make a good general impression but the
authority for making the enquiry should be clearly set out. In the more complicated
enquiries it will almost certainly be voluntary and this, and the confidential nature of the
information, must be stressed. Again, no promise of any kind should be made about the
farmer getting any return for this cooperation and this should be underlined. Generally,the
most that can be offered is a certificate that the farmer has cooperated.
Some of the problems that come from resident enumerators with a different working day
from farmers have been indicated in the training section above. It is extremely important for
supervisors to keep their ear to the ground over these matters and to step in before any major friction can build up.
The last few paragraphs have been concerned with the relationship between the statistical
field staff and the general public. It is equally important to maintain good relations with the
field staff of other government departments. One element may be merely a matter of
maintaining good relations which will allow mutual help and cooperation in the future. A
somewhat different feature may be the desirability of keeping the nature of cooperation
between two departments on a comparable basis in different localities. Where,for example,a
statistics department and the extension staff of an agricultural department are jointly
working on an enquiry the compromise under which the work is split may be left to local
initiative. This could mean quite considerable variation in procedures and a good deal of
variation in the soundness of the data from district to district. It may require some tact on
the part of the organising officers to keep this problem within bounds.
It should be borne in mind that the work of a statistician tends to look to the public to
be a matter of "all take and no give". It is bad policy to leave a neighbourhood where a good


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 35
deal of the informants' time has been occupied without formally conveying the thanks of
the organisation to them. Whether this is best done by a letter to the local chief or a visit
from the local supervisor is for decision in the light of local conditions,but one should not
just disappear without a "thank-you".There may be scope,too,for feeding back some of the
results of the enquiry in carefully edited form. In many communities there will be people
who can appreciate the relevance of some of the results. Even if they cannot, the recipient is
likely to feel more sympathetic to the enquirer if he is evidently remembered.The local staff
of a cooperating department might also value summaries of the result, perhaps comparing
their neighbourhood with an adjacent one. If this is going to be done it really means
choosing for use certain figures that can be obtained quickly and easily in the course of
tabulation. If one waits till the complete report is published and extracts made, the local
interest will almost certainly have passed.
Where no statutory authority for the enquiry exists cooperation depends wholly on good
public relations and this may encourage courtesy by the organising departments.If statutory
authority exists this may be sufficient to obtain cooperation for this particular enquiry but
it should not be forgotten that other work may be required later for which no specific
statutory authority can be produced and the public regard for a department and its work
depends somewhat on the sum total of the public's relations with it.
Definitions and language
In any study a number of definitions will have to be laid down.Some of these may be of
international relevance and the 1970 World Census of Agriculture could usefully be
consulted as a guide. For example, formal definitions will be needed for dwelling unit
(usually a private building in which people sleep); household (a group of people who eat
together); holding (all land under the economic control of one farmer); farmer or farm
operator (a person or persons chiefly concerned with operation of a holding—but there may
be a question regarding who, in fact, makes the decisions): parcel of land (land forming
part of a holding surrounded on all sides by land of other farmers,public land,or communal
land); field (a continuous piece of land within a parcel covered by one category of land use,
such as one crop or one crop mixture);etc;etc. A minimum size may have to be established
for some of these units, for example 25 square yards has been used in some instances for a
field in contrast to% or 1 acre in other types of agriculture.
Units must be decided upon and quite clearly provided for in the forms issued to
enumerators. Where items of measuring equipment calibrated in different units(for instance
measuring wheels recording in feet and decitnals of feet or feet and inches) are in use,care
must be taken that the right forms go with the right instruments. Certain local units may
have to be used either because this is the natural form in which the material appears(a
bunch of bananas, head of millet) or because some customarily used unit of measure will
arise naturally in information obtained from farmers. It is in the highest degree important
that these should be avoided in all recording work where this is possible or the conversion
from the local unit to those for incorporation in the final tabulation should be done under
strictly standardised conditions, on the basis of test weighings or measurements. For
example, an Ethiopian team found that a particular area measure, for which the same term
was used over a considerable geographical region, in fact ranged in size from 33 to 76
hectares. When the team asked presumably knowledgeable people about the hectarage of this
unit, the standard reply was "40 hectares". Perhaps the classical example of this problem is
provided by cigarette tins as measures of volume in West African markets. Here,though, the
volume of tins has varied both with the brand and with the degree of fill, a combined
adjustment permitting rather subtle variations in pricing of produce.
A list should be provided to enumerators of all the local names for the crops which it is
intended to include in the enquiry. In some livestock enquiries a similar glossary may be
needed to take account of stock of different ages. A considerable range of names is often to
be found for the same products but care should be exercised to make sure that, if there are
varietal differences which are significant in respect of the utilisation of the crop, these are
provided for separately in the list.


