When it comes to tabulating the figures there should be no doubt regarding the state in
which the crop, or other products, was weighed. For example, paddy may be the natural
product in one stage in the process from field to mouth,and rice another.Provision should
be made for the entry to be in terms ofthe product as it stands at the appropriate recording
stage. Conversion can then be made as appropriate in the office. Other possible occasions for
confusion are shelled and unshelled maize,shelled and unshelled groundnuts,fresh or partly
dried root crops,(such as yams),etc.
The peculiarities of a local language may not raise any problems in large scale enquiries of
a fairly simple nature where the enumerator can obtain most of his information by
observation. However, as enquiries increase in scope some risks of misunderstanding on
purely language grounds may arise. It might well be worthwhile if a questionnaire is written
by a statistician in a local language to have it translated by someone else, preferably
someone who is not a statistician, back into,say English. Similarly if it is drafted initially in
English then translated into a local language,it might be valuable to get it translated back by
a non-statistician. It should be remembered that in many societies there may be only very general terms for
aspects of time. An attempt to translate "minutes" or "hours" into a local language is
likely to produce a meaning similar in precision to that in the expression "wait a minute!".
"Time states" may be difficult to handle, too."Being married" may in some languages imply
the ceremony and not the state of being married It may be necessary to refer to "two
people who were married last year" if the state of marriage is to be indicated. The idea of a
wife, in any event, may not be entirely clear. For instance in some societies there may be
differences in a wife's rights over land depending on whether a formal marriage has taken
place (e.g. a bride price has been paid)or whether the marriage is temporary. But full status
may be claimed even if notjustified—for example if an adult woman is not married,she may
be liable to tax and may fear that the enquiry will bring this to light. When one goes into less
factual enquiries the problems are considerably greater. For example, in an enquiry which
included questions about a family's hopes for their sons who were at school, quite a number
included in their answers some reference to his being killed in a road accident or other
unhappy feature. This became explicable when it was appreciated that in that particular
language 'hope'and 'expect' were covered by the same word.
Documentation
A large number of documents providing information about an enquiry, providing the
place in which information is recorded or providing summary sheets to which it may be
transferred, may be needed in the course of an enquiry. Various report forms are needed
too. Receipts and other records in connection with financial transactions, refunds on
expenditure for petrol and repairs, etc., must be provided for. An indicative check list for
this is provided in the Appendices. These cover the majority of the main headings under
which forms will arise. One or two particular features may be worth noting separately.
Provision should be made for transmission of documents in such a way that losses are
minimised and the stage of processing ofeach one can clearly be assessed. That is, when they
move from staff member to staff member along a chain of responsibility there should be a
receipt and passing-on indication somewhere in a record which does not move with the
documents themselves. Losses have made regrettable gaps in a number of enquiries and if
this occurs near the end of a crop season it may be impossible to re-do the work even if staff
time were otherwise available. Arrangements should be made to move the first few schedules completed by an
enumerator (say, half a dozen) through to the supervisor and thence to headquarters rather
quickly so that any major oddity in the carrying out of instructions can be noted and
corrected straight away. At this stage travelling by headquarters staff is likely to be valuable,
if it does not completely denude the headquarters of essential people to whom ultimate
reference can be made. Subsequent batches can be despatched in rather larger quantity but
not too many should be placed at risk in any one consignment.
District progress sheets should be displayed in district statistical offices and kept regularly


