displays of wall charts at headquarters relating to up-country activities unless this is the
point at which some action has to be initiated on each of them.
The problem of getting revised instructions to field staff, when this is occasioned by
experience in the course of an enquiry is a difficult one. As has been mentioned under
training, if at all possible it would be best to arrange for senior supervisory staff to take such
instructions to the people who have to implement them, explaining the background and
making clear the need. A stream of revisions of this kind coming out of headquarters by post
is calculated to create a wholly perfunctory—if not mutinous—attitude on the part of field
staff. It is not unreasonable if some of these instructions get 'lost' at a rather late stage in their distribution!
The provision of extra copies offorms to field staff needs explicit attention. They should
be instructed to indent for them at least two weeks ahead of requirements(more if postal
