discussion here will be confined to about half a dozen points of emphasis.
In big surveys a detailed listing of the equipment required will probably have been made
in the preparation of the application for the necessary vote offunds.This may not have gone
into specific detail for the smaller items,however,and where a body of material has already
been built up and enquiries are being paid for out of a regular vote, early routine listing of
equipment requirements may not occur. It is important to provide at an early stage for a
review of what is likely to be required and what is likely to be available, and get an
indication of the delivery dates of anything which has to be purchased. This may be
something in the nature of a reconnaissance on the ground which might point to certain
critical areas and the need for a scrutiny of the importance of certain items which it might
be difficult to get. A list should be maintained of the work that is in progress and planned so
that the total demand for particular pieces ofequipment at particular times is not excessive.
This may mean making enquiries outside the statistical department if it is planned to use
equipment from other sections who may have their own plans for it. These reviews are
intended to provide "early warning" of likely difficulties. A detailed listing should be made early on of what equipment is going to be required,
how much, and when. In order to avoid omissions it may be well worthwhile to approach
this from several different directions. For instance, the various field records to be made
could be listed and the relevant equipment noted,tabulations to be made and documents to
be produced at the end with an indication of their size (it may be that the forms required
in the summarising stages cannot be produced on the equipment in the department
concerned but will have to go out to other departments or commercial printers which can
handle larger sheets). Again, a list should be made of the staff and their duties and the
equipment which will be needed for this purpose. The position of the staff in each stage of


