the enquiry should be listed too, and this may throw up matters of tents or bedding
required, equipment for meals, and so on. All draft instruction sheets should also be
scrutinised and the equipment required indicated. In all these processes equipment includes
not only machinery of a capital demanding kind but also expendable items such as paper
clips, staples and stationery. Particularly where special purchases may be needed the resulting requirements list should
be scrutinised with great care to see whether the same results could be attained more cheaply
or readily (the modern term for this is 'cost effectiveness'). Even if equipment is actually
available it is pointless to use it if there is a serviceable substitute procedure which will make
the whole arrangement somewhat less vulnerable to competition for equipment or cash. For
instance,if planimeters are envisaged for measuring the area of fields from sketch maps in the
office but they are difficult to obtain, is it possible to draw out the sketches on squared
paper and count the squares instead of using planimeters? Will the contribution to error be of
any significance in the total error? The desired equipment could at this stage be divided into
categories,e.g. what the statistics agency owns or will control absolutely, what must be drawn
from another agency, either commercial or government. For this latter group the maximum
limits for the period when it is wanted should be calculated and the agency concerned
consulted to see whether this is going to be a feasible arrangement throughout the possible
period of demand. Ideally a positive promise is required from the supplying agency but some
discretion may have to be used ifflexibility in the period of demand is to be allowed for. An
attempt to get a cast iron promise may merely result in a flat refusal.
In respect of the department's own equipment a very careful scrutiny of its service record
should be made. If equipment has not been overhauled recently then this should be done in
advance of a heavy demand on it. For items like transport equipment it is important to
check the past maintenance records to show what proportion of equipment is normally
serviceable at any particular time. It is not necessarily impossible to work with a high
proportion of the equipment out of action at any one time, provided this is allowed for in
the original plans. Account should be taken of the seasonal conditions in all planning of this
kind. This may seem too obvious to mention but the triumph of hope over experience has
led to many enquiries having to be abandoned with inadequate samples.
Resources needed
Variations in the details of the design of the enquiry can lead to variations in the cost of
carrying it out and in the precision of the results finally obtained,as is noted above under
sampling. For a given sampling fraction and a given proportion of the agriculture of the
country which is actually recorded costs are likely to be reduced if the units are bunched in
localities, thus reducing the amount of travelling time. Obviously the detailed outcome
depends on the organisation of the field staff and many other details and can only be
worked out with any precision if a pilot enquiry to get the actual quantities concerned has
been made. Consequently part of the preparatory work in any study should include records
of the time taken to undertake certain work and to transfer staff from place to place,
including injury time and time out for sickness and disruption due to local holidays,and the
like. It would be useful if one could give some sort ofindication of the likely manpower needs
of particular kinds of study. Not much can be done in this connection because enquiries
differ so much in their content and in the conditions in which they are undertaken, but
some examples can be quoted. Two survey teams of two or three people carried out 470
interviews, each lasting on average twenty minutes, into farm conditions in 150 villages in
38 days. The actual travelling time between villages was that involved in an enquiry of 20
miles or so diameter. Broadly speaking one supervisor is needed for each five enumerators or
each five people in the office. In another enquiry,involving a large acreage survey,51 teams
took a little over 7,000 man-days to complete it. Of this about 600 man-days was occupied
in travelling from one sample location to the next, about 1100 in interviewing chiefs,
choosing samples, etc., and something over 5,000 in finding informants, identifying plots


