VII. ERRORS AND ERROR CONTROL
Introduction
However excellent may be the design of an enquiry the reliability of the final results
depends on the accuracy of the recording and the processing of the material. No amount of
statistical manipulation will correct for errors in original recording, particularly systematic
errors. Errors tend to be cumulative in the sense that if some occur and are tolerated this
encourages others. Such toleration may be the result either of basic slackness in the control
of an enquiry or it may be the inevitable consequence of the organisation being
over-stretched. It is, therefore, highly important not to start on a bigger piece of work than
there is men, money and equipment to handle. This is, to some extent, a council of
perfection. Nevertheless statistical work of this kind is probably not a case where it is'better to have tried and failed than never to have tried at all'. If an enquiry is clearly proving too
big for the resources available, then it should be trimmed to a scale which can be handled
before a great many poor quality records have been made. For instance, if an
inter-penetrating sample design has been used it can be cut to halfin scope while maintaining
the same rigor of sample design—such sacrifice may be needed only in certain areas. The
overstretching of resources may come not only in quantitative terms but by attempting a type of enquiry that is beyond the capacity of the calibre of staff available. Generally
speaking, multi-subject enquiries need much better staff than such studies as the estimation
of the acreage of a few main crops.
Good staff and proper training are probably the main contributors to sound statistical
material. Interested, alert and conscientious field supervisors are probably the kingpin of the whole organisation.
It is important to look at the totality of errors in an enquiry and to get some idea of
which ones contribute heavily to the uncertainty attached to the final results. It is pointless
to spend a lot of effort in reducing the error in a particular element of the process which contributes no more than plus or minus one per cent to the error of the final result, when
there are other elements which contribute ten per cent. It is not merely that the effort might
be better spent in reducing the ten per cent, but that the outlook which allows such a
concentration is inimical to a sound commonsense approach to practical agricultural
statistics work. Probably the most serviceable approach is first to make all efforts to avoid
error, then to try and assess what remains, and finally to explore the consequences.
It would be useful if the makeup of errors in different surveys were more often available.
Unfortunately, the analyses seem rarely to be published. Obviously an informant may give
wrong information, or the enumerator may make a wrong measurement, but there are also
errors which come in as a rather incidental consequence of the design of the enquiry.
Identification of holdings account for many of the problems, for example, the main core
may leave no difficulty but social practice in the community may mean that there is a
substantial fringe for which it is not clear whether they should be regarded as farmers, or
not. Again, where there are several return visits by enumerators in the course of an enquiry,
the farmers may have changed their customary name in the interval and be difficult to locate
and match with the records of previous passages. This may not be too much of a problem if
the same enumerators come back each time but changes may have to be made through illness
or responsibility for other enquiries, and new men may come back on each occasion. Again, a proportion of the land which ought, strictly speaking, to be included in the enquiry,may
not be found in a first attempt. An experienced Central African worker reckoned that in his
enquiries 90 per cent of the area in the selected sample farms was,in fact,found on the first


