visit. Subsequent visits increased this somewhat,but highly intensified observations might be
needed to bring this up, to, say, 98 per cent.Some interesting pointers have come from the comparison of main survey with post-enumeration test surveys in various countries. In a Middle East study the census showed 47 percent more fields per household than the post
enumeration survey, but the latter showed something like 45 percent more area operated per
household. It is difficult to reach any firm conclusion from this except that the different
conditions of the two enquiries produced markedly different results—In some Japanese
studies where the information was obtained by enumerators from. the farmers, and
subsequently checked by actual observation and measurement, it was found that irrigated
rice was very accurately recorded but upland crops might be under-estimated by upwards of 10 or 15 percent. It was found,too,that crops,such as fruit or rape, which were mainly cash crops were more usually under-estimated than those, such as rice, which were to a substantial extent used for home consumption. There are some indications in other enquiries
that irrigated crops were accurately recorded but that as the enquiry moved out into the
higher land, particularly into mountain areas, field observation in a routine survey gave
under-estimates largely because of the omission of fields attached to sample farmers, fields
which were revealed when a closely supervised post-enumeration test survey was made.
Among the most common errors,again in some Japanese studies, was the transfer of acreage
figures wrongly from a cadastral record to the working tabulation, next came faulty
identification and classification of land. The most important source was often the complete omission from the classification of units which strictly should have been there. No two
surveys are quite alike in the problems presented but the risk of errors in transfer offigures is underlined by these results.
Error control by improved recording The quality of recording turns on the soundness of enumerator training and the rigorousness with which the field supervisors make sure that field staff actually do what they
are supposed to do, watching them carrying out the procedure, and testing from their
records that they have done it in other cases. Typical examples of danger points are 'give and
take' lines to designate the boundaries of irregular fields, and the actual seeking out and
counting of livestock and equipment which is to be entered on returns. In overcoming
problems of continuity from one passage to another the use of maps, even rough sketch
maps, or sketched-in details on rather crude enlargements of small scale maps, or tracings
from air survey photos, may be a considerable help.Some enquiries by their nature put a lot
of responsibility on the enumerator and/or informant where details must be remembered
over days or weeks. Some reference to this is made in the section on farm recording below.
Generally,in sample enquiries the process of substituting another farmer for one who cannot
be found, or who will not cooperate, receives rather less emphasis than it should.The mere
refusal of a farmer to cooperate is not enough to initiate a substitution procedure,neither is
finding a deserted farm, nor the absence of a farmer. In the latter case information can
probably be obtained from someone else. Substitution should not be allowed at the
discretion of the enumerator but specific permission obtained from at least one stage up the line of responsibility.
Control by check procedure
The supervisors of field staff hold a key position here. They should inspect all forms of
questionnaires completed by field enumerators and should look for internal inconsistencies.
A sample should also be checked by regional supervisors before the whole is sent on to the
central office. Typical indications of trouble, and probable need to refer back to
enumerators, are the case where areas for individual crops and for the farm as a whole are
measured and entered but the total for individual crops is greater than the indicated holding.
Supervisors should suspect trouble if there are many zeros or blank items in forms. They
should also be able to check the transfer offigures from one point to another in a form,e.g.
for further computation. These are items which could, in the main, be picked up at


