headquarters level but this delays the return of the forms to enumerators for checking to a
time when they may have moved to new areas. There may be various items which cannot be
detected by any internal checks but which supervisors may query out of their own local
knowledge. There may be a special advantage in scrutinising the data for any product which
is the subject of control, e.g. where planted areas are licensed. Examples have been found
where areas are entered which are appreciably smaller than the true area. Licensing may not
have been enforced in a locality for some time because evasion had become too frequent to
make control possible. In these circumstances,no enumerator wishes to highlight the fact of
this evasion by entering a figure above the one that is nominally the correct one,whether or
not he personally has any responsibility for enforcement.These opportunities for correction
open to supervisors underline the need to get a few completed forms into their hands
quickly so that consultations on prevalent errors can be got under way. There are often
opportunities for useful experience and ideas to be passed on from district to district at this
stage if senior staff can find time to tour.
Procedures for aiding control
As has been noted already, some indication of operator error can be obtained from
inter-penetrating samples provided that the error is not a systematic one common to all. A
call-back system can be instituted in some enquiries by which supervisors can get back to the
farmers who have already provided information and, making some reasonable excuse(about
lost schedules, for example)re-do part of the recording. It would probably be sufficient if it
is taken to the extent of elucidating the number offields which the farmer operates since it
is more than likely that the field measurement will be accurately done, but that the
enumerator's errors will arise from incomplete listing offields. Some countries have arranged
that these call-backs are made by staff who are engaged on more intensive surveys in the
locality, consequently this allows rather more highly skilled staff to be used for the check
procedures. It might be said in passing that the reconciliation offigures obtained by the two
sets of observations may require a certain amount of tact. There may be cases where the
enumerator has good reason for what he has put down and no good purpose is served by
criticising him before hearing his reasons.




