VIII. ESTIMATION OF CROP AREA
Introduction
This chapter is concerned with estimating areas of annual crops. Estimates concerned
with general land use,including potentially cropable land and land unsuitable for agriculture,
raise rather different problems and are dealt with in a separate chapter.So is the estimation
of tree crop areas. The individual crop areas and the aggregated value of these,representing
the total area of the farm, are amongst the most basic of agricultural statistics; they are
usually the first to be collected when statistical work is begun in a country. In some ways
one can regard a survey or census to collect this information as being a typical
general-purpose enquiry.
There appear to be no short cuts to the estimation offield crop acreages. At first sight it
might be hoped that aerial sui-vey would yield such information without the cost of a large
field staff. In practice, so far, aerial surveys are usually made only at long intervals and for
purposes which rank rather higher in the priority list for funds than do agricultural statistics
alone. Typically, they are undertaken as a basis for mapping in a country where maps have
hitherto been inadequate or for the preparation of land utilisation maps and water control
programmes. In order to obtain annual acreage estimates from aerial survey it would be
necessary to repeat a photographic cover, at least in sample form,each year at a time suited
to the particular crops concerned and to use a scale for the photographs substantially
larger—perhaps treble—that normally used for mapping and general land-use purposes. There
seem to be few, if any, cases in developing countries where this has been possible.
Occasionally in certain developed countries either a very pressing need for acreage data
under control programmes of one kind or another, or unusually ready access to air survey
facilities, have allowed the technique to be used fairly freely. Spot checking using light
planes and an incomplete coverage is, however, quite common in many situations in both
developed and developing countries.
It might be noted that even if the air survey technique could be made economically
feasible it is not necessarily as attractive as it might seem at first sight since it cannot be
combined with any enquiry relating to the production or disposal of the crops concerned,or
even their yield. For this purpose contact has to be made with the individual plot, and
usually the individual farmer and, if the field organisation for this has to be maintained, it
may well add little to the cost to extend its coverage to include a basic area estimation.
Occasionally, the existence of records of registered areas or licensed areas may offer a
promise of a short cut. Where a crop is subject to some kind of control so that producers are
required to be licensed, two possible sources of discrepancy should be watched for. One is
the perfectly proper exclusion of certain categories of the crop from the registration.
Sometimes a variety is excluded from the registration but would be included with the main
crop in any ordinary statistical estimation procedure;sometimes small plots for home use are
excluded; sometimes the production in certain localities is exempted from licensing. Usually
this kind of discrepancy can be judged from the regulations governing the registration
through the proportionate extent of the omission may be unknown. The second possible
source of discrepancy is the evasion of the regulations to a greater or less extent.In principle,
it should be possible to derive a correction factor to convert registered acreage to true
acreage by undertaking a survey of the two in a selected sample of localities. This should
not, however, be undertaken lightly. It may be that such a factor can be derived from the
figures of a particular crop when they are gathered together with figureq of various other
crops in a multi-crop survey. The ratio obtained in one year may then be used in subsequent


