years if the matter was of interest and repeat enquiries of the multi-crop scope were not possible. However,if an attempt were made to make a special survey of the actual acreage under the registered crop alone,it would be a matter ofembarrassment to local enforcement administrative officials and it would be difficult to forecast what the statistical and other
outcomes of such a situation would be. Trouble has also been noted over using the land registry records for the area ofindividual fields or farms as an indication of the true area. Here, too, the basic reasons for the discrepancy may be part and parcel of the originating legislation but they may not be very actively in the consciousness ofthose concerned at any given time. Reference has been noted
in the literature to such records which under-estimate true area by up to 15 per cent for
irrigated rice and up to about half that margin for upland crops.
The definition of area for the present purpose is by no means straightforward. For
instance, should it include the paths, boundaries and channels which are intimately associated with the portion of land on which the crop is grown? If only the portion on
which it is grown is to be included, is this to be understood as including the whole of the
land prepared for its reception, or only that part which is actually planted to the crop,i.e.
excluding the unplanted area round the edges? Is it to relate to the area planted? Or is it to
relate to the area harvested? Is it to relate to the superficial area under any of these
definitions of area, or to the area projected at sea level(the latter would be that observed on
a map of the land concerned)? Quite clear principles could be laid down in respect of all
these points of uncertainty;the question is what is feasible under practical conditions.
As to the inclusion of boundary strips, paths, and so on,it is usually difficult to be sure
what definition has been applied in practice whatever may be the instructions to field staff.
There is some indication that under fairly intensive irrigated conditions the minor drains and
channels might amount to from one half of one per cent up to perhaps three per cent of the
total area, perhaps one and a half percent on average (this is an example from Pakistan). Some Japanese figures suggest that the margin might be seven percent for paddy and three percent for upland crops. Obviously if the estimation is under close control it may be possible to pin down these definitions but the field operations may not be entirely straightforward. The matter ofincluding the whole of the land prepared rather than the land actually cropped may not raise grave matters where the agricultural system is a fairly
workmanlike one. One statistician commented that if he were recording labour inputs then
these would relate to the total area dug for the crop and it therefore seemed sensible to
regard this as the area of the crop; this seems reasonable. Where the boundaries of the
prepared land are much less definite, as with the less organised types of shifting cultivation,
there is a real problem which is discussed somewhat more fully later. From time to time the
matter of superficial area versus projected area arises for debate. It seems rarely to be a serious issue in practical working conditions though adjustments are discussed occasionally.
Even in a 25 per cent slope the difference between projected area and actual area is only
about 5 per cent and this is probably among the least of the uncertainties attaching to a good
deal of this work in developing countries. Where estimates have to be undertaken on steeply
sloping land many other problems are usually involved and to concentrate on this particular
issue is likely to divert attention from more important ones. Perhaps the matter of the minimum area to be recorded in estimation procedures might
be included under the general heading of definition. This has to be settled locally in the light
of the purpose of the enquiry and the nature ofcropping practice.Some countries have set a
minimum area of 1/40th of an acre, in other cases as small as 9 square yards for an annual
crop or 81 square yards for perennial crop have been noted. Broadly speaking,it costs as
much in field enumerator's time to deal with a 50 square yard plot as% acre plot,and where
small plots are common some preliminary study of the justification for dealing with them
would be valuable. The omission of some of them in enquiries where the bulk ofthe crop is
in much larger units may be of little consequence. If they are important in a particular
situation, as might be the case for herbs used in "side dishes", they are all grown on such a
microscopic scale that it is very doubtful whether any useful purpose would be served by


