pursuing that particular piece of information. For instance, it might be much better if an
idea is required of the contribution of side dishes to nutrition to attack the problem at the
consumption point.
As might be expected from what has been said before, both population and area frames
have been used in sample enquiries to obtain crop area statistics. In principle either is
suitable (their relative advantages and disadvantages have been discussed already). It might
be said in passing that examples of upward bias in crop acreage estimation using the area
frame method have been reported, apparently arising from the enumerators managing to
move the position of the selected sample units to somewhat flatter ground where they would
otherwise fall on hilly land. Obviously the risks of this depend on the scale of the maps
available and the closeness with which it is possible to supervise the enquiry. Typically,
studies have depended on finding by actual measurement the area relating to the primary
population sampling units—sometimes a village or similar small group, sometimes a farm
family—and deriving the national totals from these records in the usual way. In principle it
would seem that there might be cases where the whole of crop A would be found in a solid
block in one part of the village lands,the whole of crop B in another part,the whole ofcrop
C in a third, and so on. Within each block individual farmers would have their own areas, but
the boundaries might not be readily visible to a visitor. Under these conditions it might be
economical in time not to take a sample of the farmers. To do so would require a visit to
each of the crop areas in the village, the identification of each man's own parcel ofcrop A
and its measurement. This may well be more costly than measuring the larger, but visibly
defined, area, of the village's total of crop A. In fact there seem to be relatively few reports
in the published material of this kind of short-cut having been used.The reasons for this may
not be difficult to envisage. For instance, the crops may be in a more or less compact
locality but still the boundaries may be irregular and, perhaps with belts of trees or bush
breaking up the main block. Again, it is more than likely that other information will be
required in the same visit to the locality, information which can only be obtained from
contact with individual farmers. It will usually be valuable to be able to associate such
information with the crop area data at some stage and for this purpose the area records for
the individual farms will be needed. The possibility of such short cuts should be kept in
nlind, however, though the bulk of the rest of the discussion will be concerned with
estimating procedures based ultimately on the area of the relevant crops on individual farms.
There would be few problems in acreage estimation work if the records did not have to be
made under pressure of time. Sometimes this is imposed by the growth of the crop,
sometimes by the needs of the statistical service to supply data urgently for operational
purposes. Since the basic record required is the area assignable to particular primary
sampling units(as has been noted,usually farmers,)it is essential to locate all the crop grown
by the units concerned. For various reasons, when a farmer is approached he may not take
the enumerator to all his plots or fields. Sometimes this problem can be overcome if the
village head is cooperative and if his personal position in the village is firm. However,he may
be reluctant to take a visiting official three or four miles to the other side of the village land
in order to see two or three odd patches ofland perhaps intended to produce food crops for
a rather despised group of labourers whom the village people employ. Where local customs permit it the list of plots assignable to selected farmers can be usefully checked in a village
meeting. It is quite likely that any omission,accidental or intentional,will come to light if a
good tempered discussion can be sponsored. In some surveys the use ofthis village meeting
was made compulsory on all enumerators,—as for instance the Basutoland (now Lesotho)
1949/50 agricultural census. A further type of cross-check may be obtained by listing the
fields reported by the farmer according to some sort of pattern which is innate in the local
system. For instance,enquiries in the forest area of West Africa have required enumerators to
tabulate the farmers' plots under the year of clearance ofthe original bushland.In a system
of husbandry where land is cleared from bush, cropped for a regular number of years, and
then returned to bush,this cycle provides a reasonable check on the completeness of the list.
A farmer should have an area assignable to each ofthe years ofthe cycle then current. This
kind of check must obviously be keyed to the local practice.


