There are four main methods of obtaining the basic area record for the primary sampling
unit
(a) by locating the individual fields and measuring with measuring equipment,
(b) by preparing a map ofthe areas under the specified crops,
(c) by asking the farmers for the required information,
(d) by some variety of cluster or point sampling.
These methods are described in the following paragraphs,beginning with field measurement
since this seems the most common. The chapter concludes with a few miscellaneous
techniques which may be ofinterest less for themselves than for the possibilities in particular
situations which they suggest.
Field measurement
There are two main groups of considerations under this heading, one concerned with
methods of estimating areas from the basic measurements and the other with the equipment
used to make measurements. There is also a question of whether the calculations from the
measurements should be done in the field or the office.
There are four main methods of calculating areas.
(a) For fields that have at least two sides parallel it is possible to proceed by estimating the
mean length of the two parallel sides and the distance between them and taking the
product of the two.
(b) If there are more than three sides to the field it can be converted to a polygon and
broken down into triangles. The area of the triangles can be determined either by the
half base times height to apex, or the semi-perimeter formulae.These are described in
Appendix E.
(c) These polygonal fields can also be recorded by a traverse of the circumference ofthe
field taking bearings and lengths ofthe several sides.
(d) A variant on this process is the use of the planetable where a map of the plot is built
up by bearing and distance measurements, but not by a traverse round the
circumference.
The rectangulation and triangulation methods require only measurement of distances.For
this process pacing, knotted cords, tapes (steel and linen), survey chains, rods,large scale
versions of 'dividers',and measuring wheels have all been used. For angular measurement the
standard equipment is the prismatic compass with spirit damped card, and, rarely, the
planetable.
Turning now to the working details,any arrangement for field measurement must make it
possible for any observation to be repeated,if required,by the supervisor. If the farmer and
the particular field on his farm are regularly identifiable, it may be sufficient to have these
recorded. This would be the case if farmers are reasonably cooperative and their farms are
compact or conform to some kind of pattern such as being made up of fields cleared from
bush on a short annual cycle. If this possibility of rigorous checking is not provided by the
mere listing of farmer and field, it may be desirable to make the field staff provide a sketch
of the village land and an indication of the paths leading out from it with the location of the
plots actually visited.1 When the field has been located, the first step is to prospect the
situation to see where its boundaries are and what number of sides are needed to provide
reasonable approximation to its area. Ofthe methods noted above for getting area estimates,
the use of rectangles or parallel sided figures seems now rare in area estimation work,except
where fields are laid out on the square at the time of settlement, or, occasionally, where
terraces of regular widths are set out on hillsides. The method for other situations has tended
to leave too much discretion in the hands of the field staff in approximating the boundaries
to the formalised rectangular figure. If a situation does arise where the local land system
suggests this method would be serviceable, then care should be taken that where the plots
are not exactly formal rectangles, parallelograms, or trapeziums, the approximation is not
attempted using too few rectangles.
1. It might be said in passing that the procedure for locating plots must conform rigorously to the
random sampling pattern; attempts to get mean plot areas per village or district made by telling field staff
to measure a certain number of plots, have almost invariably proved to give serious bias.


