The triangulation method is always serviceable; any polygon can be broken down into
triangles, and the areas of these can be calculated,given either all three sides or one side and
a perpendicular upon it. The difficulty here has usually been in getting the field staff to put
themselves in the right position for measuring the perpendicular. It must be remembered
that a line bounding a triangle which crosses a field has no very clear indication on the
ground except as a line of sight. Moreover,the apex may not be clearly visible unless a fellow
team member locates it and stands there. If the crop is low and the distance is small the
problem may not be significant, but in high crops and irregular ground there is an obvious
possibility of trouble. Perhaps the simplest practical way of getting the perpendicular is to
walk along the base with an eye on the position of the apex until it is believed that the right
point on the base has been reached. The enumerator then faces the apex,stretches his hands
out away from his sides, pointing along the line of the base; if he then swings them round
evenly to join in front of him,his fingers should be pointing directly at the apex;if they are
not,he moves accordingly. Apart from siting accurately in high crops the actual walking of the base line and using
measuring equipment along it will possibly cause difficulty in growing crops.The use of the
perpendicular requires even more traversing of the plot than the ordinary measurement of
the three sides. Though the triangulation method seems not often to be used now, it should be kept in
mind as one which needs the minimum of equipment.If pacing or knotted cords are used for
measuring it can be operated with no more than one tape in each locality for checking
purposes. The preparation of traverses of the circumferences of fields using a compass and some
form of linear measuring equipment is a well tried technique and,if the field staff are well
trained, it is likely to be found serviceable and not unduly demanding of resources. The
usual instrument for taking bearings is the prismatic spirit damped compass; and various
forms on the market all seem reasonably satisfactory. The choice of measuring equipment is
a subject of some argument. The chain and steel tape are entirely serviceable, although
chains in bush conditions may get strained and steel tapes in wet conditions need care. In
recent years measuring wheels,(some about two feet in diameter, others about one foot)
have been used.Some users have found them entirely satisfactory,some have discarded them
as introducing too many errors. The chains and tapes need two staff members,or one staff
member and a local helper, whereas, in principle, a wheel can be operated by one person.
This is sometimes a less clear distinction than it appears because some assistance may be
needed,(it may be available out of good nature, or curiosity, merely for the purpose of fmding the way and getting equipment from place to place. Moreover,even with the wheel it
may be necessary to make a trip to the destination corner before starting the measurement if
an assistant cannot be posted there. Lines of sight in developing countries are often far from
clear when doing traverses round plots. To try to crystallise the issues a little, it would
probably be fair to say that if money is short and large numbers of field teams have to be
operated, chains or steel tapes will probably give better results than wheels.They are also to
be preferred if the boundaries to be traversed include bush stems which have to be got over
or round, or ground so uneven that the axle of the wheel moves up and down by, say,6
inches within 6 feet or so of run, or where the surface is crumbly and liable to collapse or
skid under the wheels. Minor roughnesses in method are likely to be more damaging to
accuracy with the wheel than with the other methods. It is important with any of this
equipment to stress its careful use and to keep a very close watch on the way in which the
operator does his work.
The conversion of these field records into an estimate of area raises no problems of
principle. The formulae can be applied with pencil and paper or by computer—and by all
kinds of calculation equipment between. The main practical question is how to avoid errors
of computation and how to get errors of field observation checked most smoothly and
quickly. Generally speaking, it seems to be better so to arrange communications that faulty
field observations can be sent back to the enumerator readily,and to do all the calculation in
the office. If, however, areas need to be calculated in the field, in practice either the


