rectangulation method or the triangulation method using the half base times vertical height
formula is used. The more usual method is to send the field records direct to a central office
for checking and computation. Most commonly the compass and measurement traverse of
the circumference is used although triangulation may be included from time to time.
Typically the first process in the office is to check the closing error of the traverse;this can
be done by computer which will then print out all the observations showing the discrepancy
between the end of the traverse and the opening point as a percentage of the total distance
round it. If these facilities are not available,as is usual at present,the practice is to draw out
using a circular protractor and ruler, usually on squared paper, the various plots and to
calculate the percentage error. A decision has to be reached on how much error can be
tolerated and a working basis might be to pick a closing error of somewhere between five
and ten per cent, which will not result in more than perhaps 15 per cent of the observations
being returned to the field stafffor re-checking. Ifin a particular case these seem too crude a
tolerance limit then this is all to the good and a source of satisfaction.
It is important to keep this stage of the computation fully up to date because rechecks
are usually impossible when the enumerator has moved on to other villages. It does not
follow that access to a computer will necessarily make easier the quick despatch of recheck
instructions. This will only be so if access is obtainable regularly at the time required. When
bearings and distances consistent with a tolerable closing error are complete for each
observation, the closure must be completed and the area calculated. This, too,can be done
on a computer or by hand (see Appendix E); in fact with experience of plotting out
differently shaped fields,a rule ofthumb can be found to shorten this work.For example,in
many instances little if any error is introduced if the first and the last points arejoined and
the failure to close ignored.
When this stage is reached the acreage can be calculated again on the computer or
estimated in one of the indicated ways—for example, by counting the squares or
superimposing a grid and counting from that.
There may be a tendency to strive for precision in such measurements as these to an
extent which diverts attention from the overall complex of errors or discourages work which
might nevertheless provide useful, even if rather rough, guidance. The possibility of
undertaking area estimation should not be abandoned merely because equipment of the
desired standard is not to hand or funds available to buy it. For example,though it is known
that pacing can be an inaccurate method of measuring distances with errors of up to 50
per cent, it is also known that a well-trained field man who has the knack of pacing,and in
conditions which suit it,can get results within plus or minus five to ten per cent without too
much difficulty. This would be a considerable improvement on guessing the area in a
country or even on asking farmers how much they grow. If faced with this sort of position
the way ahead is to put the available staffthrough a careful course of training and to watch
for those who have an aptitude for the work,then to reject the others and put a great deal of
care into bringing the better men up to a high standard. Again, knotted cords as measuring
devices have been referred to most contemptuously by some workers who argue that they
shrink and stretch in changing conditions. Nonetheless, if cord can be obtained, and
sufficient tapes or chains cannot,then it can be made serviceable provided a strict routine of
checking is imposed, using for the purpose the few tapes or chains available. Similarly,.a
fairly simple compass, provided that it is damped and has a reasonable arrangement for
sighting, can give serviceable results. However,if funds are very short it might be better to
use triangulation methods rather than compass-traverse methods. In working up sketches, whether at field level or in the office,it is desirable not to have
too many different scales even if the size of plot varies somewhat. It might be sensible to
concentrate on some measures of the order of 1: 1,000 and stick to this. Training has been mentioned on a number of occasions in these paragraphs and cannot be
over-stressed. The important thing is that the field man should do what he is supposed to do
and do it methodically. If he fails to do this, then there is a grave danger of a systematic
error, which no manipulation later can correct for. The level to which records can sink is
indicated by a test of some 250 measures of fields in which only 44 came within ± 5


