percent;8 percent were outside the range of±50 percent of the true figures.
There is always the possibility that farmers will object to field staff making measurements
of their plots and walking across them. Provided that there is no external source of
distrust—for example anxiety about land alienation—amongst the local people,it seems that
usually they do not raise much objection,even to enumerators crossing their growing crops.
However, the possibility must be kept in mind and action taken if trouble arises. For
instance, one report spoke of farmers being complacent about the measuring but regarded
wheels going over their fields as likely to bring ill luck!
Farmers as informants on area
This possibility might reasonably be considered next since, unlike most of the others
which follow, it allows acreage data to be associated with other farm business information if
desired. It also permits the total area of a farm to be assessed. It seems rare for conditions to
arise in which collecting information on crop area directly from farmers is satisfactory in
developing countries. The local people may have their own very serviceable assessment of
area but, if so, the units are usually not standard over wide areas. If they know the area
because of a land registration or a licensing ofindividual crops the likelihood of bias already
described would make the method suspect. It seems only possible to note the feasibility of
the method, given farmers' cooperation and standardised units of measurement, and to
indicate its relative infrequency of use.
Mapping and Grid Sampling
Where large-scale accurate maps are available, particularly if they show the actual
positions of fields, and where it is not desired to have the area statistics associated with
individual farms, a range of possible techniques is available. The most highly organised,used
in a number of enquiries in India over many years, proceeds by using the grid sampling
method (see Sampling, above). Fairly typically a square area ofabout 2-21 /2acres is located
within each half square mile of the land surface. If the plots on these maps are numbered,
those falling within the grid can be identified and listed. The list of plot numbers is then sent
to the field staff in the areas concerned with instructions to enter against each the crop
occupying the field. It will be noted that,in the conditions assumed for this technique,the
boundaries of the designated fields are quite clear and that they will be well known and
clearly designated in local records. There will,therefore, be no doubt about the boundary of
the fields, and if they are occupied by more than one crop then the proportion of the area,
or the actual acreage under each crop can be indicated, as may be best in the light oflocal
practice. These statistics are then returned to Head Office and the computation of totals for
regional groupings proceeds in an entirely straightforward manner. If the plots are not
numbered and registered in local and national records but are,nevertheless,clearly indicated
in the maps,a variant of this method can be used. A section of the map containing the grid is
sent to the local field worker who enters on the map the portions of the several plots which
are occupied by designated crops. This uses the 'closed segment' approach in which the
recording is confined to the actual boundaries of the sample grid. This need not be so,any
plot which has a specified part of it (say, the S.W. corner,) within the bounds of the grid
might be included and all others excluded without introducing bias. For these techniques a
mapping scale of the general order of about 8 or 10 inches to the mile is desirable but they
could be operated with somewhat smaller scale if necessary. There are reports of a further
variant in which maps of perhaps 2inches to the mile are available with accurate indications
of the location of physical features but incomplete or non-existent records of actual fields.
Enlargements of sample portions of these, with grids inserted, have been sent to the field
staff with instructions to sketch in within the grids the portions ofthe land surface occupied
by the various crops required. Grids bounded by natural features,roads,etc.,have1 also been
used. The field worker identifies the desired portion of the grid by reference to the local
physical features. No doubt this method is capable of being satisfactorily worked.with care.
It seems worth noting,however,that these area type methods have the disadvantage of being


