difficult to link with individual farm records. A variant of these techniques is where the
sample locations for observation take the form of clusters of nine or sixteen points within
areas of the same general order of magnitude as the gridsjust described.The location ofthe
dots is indicated on maps and these are despatched to the field staff who return a statement
of the crop corresponding to the point on which each dot falls. The problems of locating
dots on fairly poor maps must be considerable and it seems at least possible that the risks of
enumerator bias are rather greater with these than with grid area records.
Miscellaneous methods
There are a number of methods in use in various parts of the world for getting an
indication of acreage which should be mentioned before going on to more general
techniques. A very common method of getting a measure of acreage where the amount of
seed used is known, is merely to divide the tonnage ofseed used by the usual seed rate. This
method is found widely and some users—for example,estimators of Irish potato acreage in
the West Indies—claim considerable accuracy for their estimates. In some Middle Eastern
countries the area is arrived at by multiplying the work animals and machines by their daily
work capacity. Possibly where the type of organisation is non-standard and statistical control
is so far slight, such methods are better than nothing. In many countries it has been
customary to collect through the field officers ofthe Departments of Agriculture,reports of
acreage planted each year quite early in the season. These records are usually very difficult
to assess. Sometimes they have continued after other techniques ofobjective estimation have
been developed. In some cases for diverse reasons,the published statistics are the average of
the reported and the objectively estimated figures! There is a special problem,which will be
referred to again below, of obtaining early estimates ofcrop area to combine with estimates
of probable yield for crop forecasting purposes. It may very well be worthwhile to try to
formalise a reporting system if one has been established. The essential feature here is a
systematic programme of reconciliation meetings between the statisticians and the regional
agricultural reporters. It is assumed that the statisticians will, after the season is over, have
reasonably good estimates of the crop either in the form of the results of objective sample
surveys or as records of the quantity ofcrops sold for export or local use. The techniques of
graphical comparison of estimated and reported area,(as outlined in the section below on
forecasting crop yields) can be applied here. The essential point, however, is that all
successful experience of this kind of work shows that reconciliation cannot be done by
correspondence; it is far better to go and sit down with the reporter and take all available
records, match them against his and reach an agreed reconciliation with guidance for future
operations. It is possible,too,that a reporting method of this kind might continue for rather
minor crops even when objective sampling had taken over the handling of the major crop
statistics. These various methods of making an estimate of area are, in principle, applicable at any
period of the crops' life from planting to harvest.(The cases of continuous cropping are
dealt with below.). If there were ample staff resources it would be possible to pick when,in
the cropping season, the record was to be made and, if desired, a series ofrecords could be
made to trace the sequence of planting and of harvesting. In practice, the difficulty is
normally to get completely around the area even once before the crop is harvested. Different
conditions require quite different approaches. A country with reasonably adequate staffing,
and a clear sequence of seasons with different crops, will wish to make a series of passages
through the area in months chosen to catch the various crops. This is a matter of technical
decision in each locality. More generally, it is worth considering how to divide between the
passages the total field work consisting of selecting sample units and making the local
frames, recording crop areas, recording crop yields, compiling financial items relating to the
farm business, etc., etc., depending on the statistical programme. It may well be sensible in
the first passage to complete the local listing to provide frames, and to select the farmers
who will be the primary sampling units and to locate their fields, and to indicate the
designation of fields by a stake with a number painted on it in black paint. Only the field
recording has then to be done on the next occasion when the area is visited and any other


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVLOPING COUNTRIES 53
time available on the visit can be used for collecting information directly from the farmer.
Some statisticians have found this works very well, farmers having no objection to the
presence of the plot designation. In other situations, it has been the practice to include the
main crop area estimate in the first round and to make forecasts of the likely date of
harvesting. Clearly this is easier to do if the crop is nearing maturity when the circuit is
made. It may be necessary, if all areas are to be covered within the constraints of planting
and harvesting dates, to work through a stretch ofcountry,starting at the early planting end
of it and finishing up at the late planting end.
Early estimates of acreage for use in production forecasting give problems to everyone.
One method has been indicated above in passing. It may well be that in most developing
countries the problem must be solved either in that way or by pushing ahead with the
acreage estimating passage through the country as quickly as possible. There are various
sampling techniques in use in developed countries for this work using systems ofvoluntary
reports. For instance a request is sent to a more-or-less random sample offarmers before the
planting time, asking for a statement of the acreage they intend to plant and the area they
planted the previous year. Those who reply will not be a representative sample but,broadly
speaking, the ratio of "intentions" to "last year's area" gives indications. These indications
must be corrected by experience of earlier years, since intentions seem always to be biased to a degree. Further sampling enquiries are made early in the year in which farmers are asked to state
how much they have planted. Similar ratio methods are used to derive early estimates of the crop, stage by stage, as the season advances. It is possible that special occasions will
occur in developing countries where this kind of technique can be used. However,it should
be kept in mind that the results will usually need correction for various kinds of bias—some
from the non-representativeness of the people prepared to reply to voluntary samples, or
some from bias in the individual informants' replies. It may take several years to build up the
necessary experience to provide correction factors to eliminate or reduce these biases.




