IX. ESTIMATION OF CROP PRODUCTION
Introduction
It is desirable in descriptions to keep separate the ideas of 'rate of production', i.e. the
production per unit (such as per hectare or per tree), the total production of a farm or
geographical unit, and the net outgo to use off the farm. Interest in this section centres on
production of national and sub-national areas, but in practice this is usually derived by
combining statistics ofrate of production with those of numbers of production units.
There are several groups of problems in this work. One is a matter of definition. Clearly
between the formation of the grain or root(which,in due course, will be the agricultural
product) and its final preparation for consumption the quantity associated with a given area
ofland will vary. Among the elements of this variation may be
(a) the addition of carbohydrate protein,etc.,and water in the process ofgrowth;
(b) loss of water with ripening of grain crops;
(c) loss of substance by bird and other damage before harvest;
(d) loss by shedding at harvest and in transfer to the store, also bird,monkey and similar
pest loss from the products stacked in the field;
(e) loss in store by pests or rot;
(f) loss by drying or respiration.
It might be noted that products are drawn for consumption from crops at all stages from
soon after the formation of the grain or tuber (well before maturity) right through to the
end of the storage period. For example, green maize cobs may be eaten in the field before
normal harvest while, at the other extreme,a product from the same crop may be used from
store right up until the start of the next harvest. Consequently the sum of the weights of
crop as actually withdrawn for consumption between harvest and the final using up of
supplies may not match the tonnage of crop actually recorded at any point. Even in
developed countries the problems are not handled very rigorously, still less are they in
developing countries where statistical control of production estimation has,usually,not yet
been consolidated. However, a working aim appears to be to deal in statistics of production
as they would be measured when the crop was in store after harvest and before any
appreciable storage losses had occurred.
A second group of items which need definition concerns the timing of the making of
estimates, as distinct from the stage of the crop to which they relate. For practical purposes
in this bulletin, figures prepared before harvest are referred to as "forecasts" and are dealt
with in the following chapter and those made at harvest, or afterwards, are called
"estimates". A confusion appears in some discussions about the usefulness of these several
figures. For instance,references are still to be found to the value of production estimates for
planning crop movement and for dealing with food shortages. In fact, figures must be
available well before harvest if they are to be of use either for movement planning or for
alleviating food shortage. Production estimates are an important,indeed usually an essential,
intermediate stage for production forecasts, but it must not be forgotten that this further
stage must be reached if these two particular aims are to be achieved. However,production
estimates, after harvest, may be valuable as general indicators of levels of production, as
elements in national income estimation, and as contributions to the study of input-output
relationships. If a situation is such that forecasts alone can give the necessary guidance this
will usually require a very special concentration of resources in order to push the work
beyond the stage of production estimation to the one of production forecasting. Otherwise,
where the urgency is less, it seems best to concentrate on estimating crops of which a large


56 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
proportion are sold. These usually provide the opportunity for cross-checking estimates and
for general toning-up of the field techniques. As for subsistence consumption, though it
might be the subject of ten-yearly enquiries for the purpose of contributing to the general
pattern of national product studies, any nutritional interest is probably best studied by direct consumption surveys.
A third group of problems is concerned with the techniques of estimation. The main
methods available are
(a) the weighing of crops if they pass through some natural bottleneck in their utilisation;
(b) eye judgement by experienced reporters;
(c) crop sampling;
(d) utilisation table estimating.
The method of sampling crops, cutting small clearly defined areas in properly selected
samples of the fields, seems now to have become fairly standard in developing countries. In
most developed ones there is a long history ofjudgement reporting. However one cannot say
that the problems of production estimating have been thoroughly solved in either. The
sampling techniques give a quite reasonably low sampling error but they are subject to
certain biases under the conditions normally met in developing countries. They probably
represent the best line to pursue but it is wrong in principle to attempt to depend here(any
more than for any other statistic) on a single source of figures. It should be the practice to
arrange to cross-check each figure obtained by one technique with a figure of similar
definition obtained by another. This seems the only approach that is wise in production estimating at present.
Direct weighing
A few crops, typically the non-edible industrial crop, are sold to processing plants in the
producing neighbourhood. It is usually possible to arrange for a return to be made to some central authority of the tonnages handled at bottle-necks where counting is possible—for
example, marketing boards, ginneries, export points, etc. Since, by their nature, these may
be operated by fairly powerful agencies, it may be necessary to obtain statutory powers in
order to collect this information; on the other hand such agencies may be extremely
cooperative. It is absolutely essential, when using this source of statistics,to check that what
is passing through the recording point corresponds to the desired definition. Examples of
discrepancies include the case where the desired figures relate to total sales but good records
are obtained only for citrus sold for export and little is known of the amount from the same
plantations which moves into local consumption. Again, products may move into or out of
the area being considered. Thus in a number of West African countries there is appreciable
movement across land boundaries. Consequently the quantity marketed through particular
channels may be greater or less than that grown in the country concerned for which
production estimates are sought. Again,where the recording points are not under the control
of a single Organisation,it is possible that the same produce may be counted more than once.
For instance, if the traders making the returns are acting both as primary and secondary
wholesalers, their records may include some produce which has already been included in
other traders'returns or will be so included later on.Stock changes may also spoil the current
throughput statistics as indicators of current production, but usually if a complete harvest
season is used this is not a great problem. The "utilisation table method"noted at the end of
this chapter provides a framework within which data obtained by direct weighing can be
used with the minimum risk of misunderstanding.
Judgement reporting
Many developed countries have built up systems under which voluntary reporters,or the
field staff of the agricultural departments, send in their judgements of the average yields of
selected crops in the localities for which they are responsible.In effect this requires them to
form a judgement both about the absolute level of yield in the individual fields which they
observe, and also about the relative proportions of good and bad fields within their area,so


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 57
that they can develop a correct weighted average for the area. Probably they do not
consciously proceed in these two steps, however.In practice,they depend not only on their
own judgement but also on the results of conversation with farmers and on the actual
out-turn of the fields harvested early. Developing countries have also used this method,
drawing estimates from agricultural officers and village accountants. Agricultural statisticians
customarily condemn the technique as difficult to organise and gravely biassed. There is
some truth in this vie*, particularly when it is remembered that the staff who are in a
position to make the estimates are usually not those of the department charged with
collecting the statistics. Nevertheless, the method may be written off too lightly. If no
system were in use and a new one had to be set up there are good grounds for choosing the
crop sampling method. However,if one is already running,even in a somewhat unorganised
form,it would be better to get the most out ofit than to discard it completely.In particular,
it may be useful in providing cross-checks with crop sampling results and with the records of
disposals through the channels described in the previous section.If'seminars' are arranged so
that those concerned with these various sources of statistics can reconcile discrepancies a
good deal that is of value, in addition to agreeing on a single set offigures,can be obtained.
Sometimes, too, it may be sensible to operate a reporting system for the less important crops, if some indicators for them are desired, while concentrating the field work of crop
sampling on the bigger and more important crop areas. Though no explicit account ofsuch
an arrangement seems available there is no reason why the field staff who are making
estimates by crop sampling should not make more general judgement reports about the
yields of minor crops not included in the sampling programme.
In a system which uses agricultural department staff,reporters should be asked to report
the average yield for the specific areas assigned to them for their ordinary duties. No
correction of the figures from individual reporters should be allowed at regional or similar
level; the original record should be sent as it stands to headquarters, though it should be
open to the regional office to attach comments.Two possible correction devices for bias are
available. If, as occasionally happens, records of this kind have been sent in regularly for
many years as part of a routine reporting programme, diagrams can be drawn up in which
the judgement report is plotted against final out-turn as judged by marketings or exports,
using methods similar to those described under forecasting below. This should indicate the
nature of any biases in reports and provide the means of adjusting for them. It might be
sensible to make such summaries separately for the individual climatic zones or, if this is impracticable, for regions or provincial areas of a country. Since the personnel concerned
are likely to change fairly often, it is pointless to make the summaries for very small areas.
The second possible method can be used, given the necessary cooperation, even if back
records are not available. Each reporter is asked to give hisjudgement of the average yield of
a number of plots (e.g. "master farmer's" fields, experimental station plots,etc.)where the
actual yield will subsequently be known. A comparison of "judgement" and "actual" will
provide a basis for adjusting the judgement report. Of course,this takes account only of any
bias in the appraisal of the individual fields;it does nothing to correct any error in weighting
of the good fields and the bad over the whole area concerned. Typically it is found that
reporters tend to over-estimate crops in poor years and under-estimate them in good years.
Crop sampling
Experience has shown that an estimate of yield per unit area can be obtained with
tolerable accuracy by cutting small plots suitably chosen in a representative sample of the fields in a country.The procedure has become fairly standard in broad outline. A sub-sample
of the fields which are selected for area recording is chosen for the estimation of yield.
Typically perhaps 1/5th or 1/10th ofthe sample for area estimating is so chosen.
The chosen fields are visited at an appropriate time and sample 'quadrats' are sited
within them using a representative method. Usually at least two quadrats are chosen in each
field. At harvest the crop on these quadrats is cut, thrashed, and the produce weighed; a
sample is retained for drying to give a correction for moisture content.The estimated yield
per unit area for the whole territory is derived from these records.


58 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
As to quadrat size, many sizes have been used ranging from one foot square or less up to
1/10th of an acre or more. For reasons discussed below very small plots,i.e. of the order of
1 square metre, are to be avoided in developing countries and a quadrat in the general order
of size between 5x5 to 10x10 metres seems sensible. Numerous instances of-enquiries using
this general order of magnitude are to be found. As to siting within the field, three methods
seem satisfactory. One is the random coordinate method. The staff normally know the size
of the field from previous recordings made to estimate area. They are required to note the
distance across the field and to deduct from it the length of one side of the quadrat,
then to do the same for the width, and within these limits to take two pairs of random
numbers. The first of the pair gives the distance from the corner of origin along the chosen
base side of the field and the second the distance at right angles into the field. By deducting
the length of the side the majority of occasions where a plot might fall outside the field, or
partially outside it, will be eliminated.
The second method is the use of a grid. The field-man is provided with a transparent sheet
of stiff plastic, say about 8 or 10 inches square, marked out in squares by two series of
parallel lines at right angles to each other. Each square is numbered. A plan of the field in
which the quadrats are to be sited is drawn to scale by the field-man.He selects the longest
side of the field as a base and places the base line of the grid over it. He then adjusts the
position of the grid from side to side so that its left hand boundary is over the angle of the
field sketch which is farthest to the left. A random number is then drawn from within the
total number of grid squares. If this numbered square lies over the field sketch then it
designates a sample quadrat. If it lies outside, then successive numbers are drawn until two
fall within the sketch. The size of square on the grid, the desired quadrat size and the scale
of sketch needs to be co-ordinated,but there are several fairly obvious methods of arranging
this. A cross staff (see Appendix C) is likely to be useful for locating sites; it may be
especially useful for siting small quadrats since its use should cut down the opportunity for
personal bias. Quadrats falling near the edges of fields present some problems, too, since
their treatment could also lead to bias. Various procedures for dealing with these quadrats
might be instituted but some may be self-defeating if in practice the field staff are confused
by them or ignore them.It is probably best to accept only quadrats which fall wholly within
the field. If one quadrat does not,another is chosen by the standard procedure.
The third method, used in big enquiries where practice is fairly standard and the level of
cultivation reasonably good, takes two points along the diagonal of the field often in more
or less systematic forms, e.g. 1/3rd and 2/3rds, although two random numbers have also
been used. Tests suggest that no significant bias is introduced by this method and the saving
in time in locating quadrats is valuable.
The quadrats may be either square, triangular or circular but the majority of workers
seem to favour the square plot. When the location has been decided by one or other of
methods described, the first side is laid down using rods pushed into the ground to mark the
corners. A tape is usually used for measuring the length of the side, and various methods
have been used for getting the right angle. The compass is quite serviceable, so is a cross
staff. Some workers have preferred a right angle square, though this might be difficult to
carry. Still another method is to knot a cord to give divisions in multiples of three,four and
five units. When the two ends are held together and the other knots pulled till the strings are
tight a right-angle triangle is set out. The choice between these methods may be a matter of
relative availability of equipment and people. The cord is the simplest and cheapest but it is
difficult to operate with less than three people.It may also be difficult to use in high crops.
A cross-staff really needs two people but,by back siting, and providing a peg to hold the end
of the tape, a compass and tape can be used by one man. The corner points can be either
stakes cut from the bush or, and this is probably better, they can be metal rods which are
carried by the fieldstaff for the purpose. They are not necessarily much more precise than
the bush stake but they tend to contribute to a general air of precision and they may be
easier to get into the ground. One or two departments have made them from tube in sections
which can be dismantled for carrying. They have also been arranged so that the same rods
can form the tripod from which the weighing equipment is suspended.


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 59
When the corners of the quadrat are clearly identified the boundaries are set out with
cords pulled taut and carefully dropped into the crop down to the ground. Care is needed in
this process to make sure that the line is kept straight and that ears originating on stems
which lie outside the boundary are excluded and vice versa.With plots of the size suggested
it is not possible to have rigid boundaries. Rigid frames have been used when designating
quadrats of the order of ten to thirty square feet; it is rather easier to slide the rods which
form these frames under any stems which lean across the quadrat boundary.
The cutting and thrashing of the crop presents the most difficult of the processes in the
crop sampling schemes; if it is a root crop much the same holds true'. The central point is
the labour of separating the product in the form in which it is used or traded from the crop
as it stands in the field. Usually this will mean for grain, thrashing,and for roots,lifting and
separating from soil. The labour of thrashing by hand a number of plots,each ofa tenth of
an acre, is considerable; this may slow down the work to a point which endangers the
programme. Conditions vary but it might be best to think of a team of two handling two to
four such plots a day. It is,in principle,possible to save some of this labour by weighing the
crop as it is cut, taking a known sample of this,thrashing it and weighing the produce,then
calculating the weight of the produce of the quadrat by simple proportion.This is probably
to be avoided since it is a stage which is rather difficult to supervise correctly — at this stage
field staff must be left a good deal of discretion on timing so that they can act quickly when
a farmer intends to harvest, and supervisors cannot readily keep up with them. It seems best
to harvest and thrash the whole quadrat yield;further sub-sampling,as suggested,should be
resorted to only if the organisation would otherwise get out of hand. Some pilot testing of
the time needed in the local conditions for locating and completing the sampling procedure
should be made. For grain, when it has been thrashed, it is customary to mix the crop
thoroughly,to take a sample of, perhaps, two kilos and put in a loose-meshed bag which is
kept under the control of the fieldstaff(or sometimes left with the farmer).If the field staff
keep it, it is exposed in the sun for three days,being taken in at night,and then weighed,to
give a drying-out allowance. Ifit is left with the farmer he is asked to keep it in his hut in an
airy situation, but away from rats, hens, etc., until it is weighed when the field staff call a
few weeks later.
The question of payment to the farmer for this work is a vexed one.If the arrangement
can be made without payment being required,so much the better. One or two enquiries have
operated without difficulty using a quite nominal payment to the farmer for the general
disturbance caused by the particular crop sampling arrangement.
One of the chief problems in this work is getting the field staff to the right point when
the crop is being haryested. Sometimes the headman can help by sending a message to the
field staff when the harvest is likely to start. Otherwise, when the farmer is visited in the
course of the area estimation work the likely date of harvesting can be discussed and a
reasonable forecast made. At least one enquiry arranged to cut half of each quadrat or half
the quadrats when the field staff visited the farm a few days before harvest and the rest at
harvest if they were successful in getting there at the right time. This gave an insurance,and
a comparison of the two,where they were available,gave a means of correcting yield records
for the plots which had not been visited at the right time. No doubt a farmer could be
persuaded to leave the sample quadrats untouched when he harvested,if the boundaries were
marked beforehand, but this would usually be of little advantage since such scraps of crop
would be the goal of every bird in the neighbourhood.
The square plots, which have been assumed in the above account, seem largely to have
taken over from other shapes. In principle, triangular plots are rather easier to define since
three cords and one base line position are sufficient. However, since they carry a higher
edge-to-area ratio than square plots, they are probably best avoided (see below). Circular
plots have been reported occasionally, and it is not entirely clear why they have not been
