Notwithstanding the wide use ofcrop sampling schemes they are not entirely the answer.
However, probably the correct way ahead is to operate this method as rigorously as possible
in the field and to direct attention to elucidating the nature of any biases to which it is
subject. As reported, these biases are usually upwards (i.e. the production in a region as
estimated by this method is higher than that which can be accounted for by the commercial
harvest or disposals). There appear to be a number of possible sources of this. One is that the
field operator may be inclined to include as within his quadrat boundary portions of crop
which, strictly speaking,lie outsideit. This is a kind of personal bias which has often been
noted in field experiments. Again, under field conditions it is very difficult to lay down the
procedure for siting quadrats so precisely that it leaves the field man no room for choice. He
may be tempted to move a small quadrat somewhat towards a better part ofthe crop than
the correct selection procedure would designate,especially if he avoids having to cut prickly
weeds in doing so. If the quadrat is large this is less easy to do. Again, there may be some
confusion arising from the fact that the crop sampling procedure does not usually allow any
major harvesting losses whereas the ordinary farm harvest may lead to quite substantial
losses, either by shedding or by bird attack in the process of getting to store. Studies have
suggested that perhaps one half of an upward bias of ten to fifteen percent was attributable
to this difference in damage. Gleaning of sample areas of farm harvested crop may give an
idea of shedding losses but not of bird and monkey damage. Finally,in practice there may
be a bias arising from the field man sampling from a different superficial area from the one
which he measured as the area of the crop earlier in the season. This will be so if the crop
suffered damage in the course of growth after the area measurement was made. If the plot
for cutting could be laid down at the same time as the area is measured no problem would
arise but, since this is often impracticable, the area from which it is sampled at harvest may
exclude this failed area. In some enquiries where the failure was of a fairly clear-cut nature,
on fields which had distinct boundaries, an arrangement has been operated where an
estimate is made of the amount of the field which has failed, either at a passage made for
this and other enquiries,(including forecasting the likely date of harvest) a little before
harvest, or at the time of harvesting. This will not, however, deal with the typical case in
shifting cultivation where bush has invaded and swamped the crop at the edges.
Though there are reports of complete fields being harvested in test enquiries
particularly test enquiries for comparing sample-based estimates with commercial
out-turn — there seems little evidence of their use as part of a regular sampling study for
crop estimation. Clearly it would be difficult for field staff themselves to harvest complete
fields but occasions may be emerging where it is possible to get a quite reasonably good
estimate of the produce of a field by noting what is sold,or by noting the number ofsheaves
carried off and weighing test samples of sheaves. Similarly, as farm record studies expand,
more and more records of this kind may emerge. There seems some advantage in giving
attention to the possibility ofrecording production per field,or production per farm, partly
as indicating commercial out-turn and partly as indicating the frequency distribution of scale
of production — a matter not by any means irrelevant in commodity planning.
Utilisation table
This is used for various purposes and is, strictly speaking, a formal,and basically wholly
simple, technique for reconciling statistics of various elements in a commodity supply
picture. Typically for a commodity such as a grain
opening stocks + production + imports = use for food + use for animal feed + exports
+ waste and drying out + closing stocks.
If all these,except one,could be rigorously estimated then this final one could be calculated.
It is rarely possible to achive such rigorous calculations even in developed countries,but by
piecing together firm estimates from international trade figures,by assuming that stock will


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 61
not differ much from one end of crop year to another, and by inserting estimates based on
small-scale enquiries for consumption, wastage, and so on, a reasonable reconciliation of
figures from numerous sources can be built up. It is extremely important that cross-checks
of this kind should be made whenever possible and a reasonable amount of time be spent on
them. One incidental advantage of this kind of tabulation is that it provides an indication of
food usage which is not available from any other source (Food Balance Sheet tabulations
by F.A.O. see publications listed). Associated with this calculation in the office should be a
programme of reconciling figures obtained by direct statistical estimation using the methods
suggested with those reported by field staff and the trading figures of central organisations.
A good deal of tact is required, both to get serious consideration of such work and to carry
it through without mutual irritation, but it is well worth doing, partly because when well
done it improves cooperation and partly because new light on the actual working situation
for a commodity is often thrown in the process.
In the main, these paragraphs have been written in terms of grain. The basic approach is
very similar for groundnuts or roots, though the detailed process may have to be varied
because of the different harvesting processes.
