X. CROP FORECASTING
Introduction
A forecast is a statement of the most likely yield or production of a crop on the basis of
known facts at the time of making the forecast,and assuming certain conditions of weather,
damage by pests and diseases, and other factors which may affect yield between the date of
the forecast and final harvesting of the crop. For working purposes it is usually assumed that
these factors will be about the same as the average for previous years when the state of the
crop at that time of year was similar to that in the current year. When considering crop production forecasting four broad stages are recognisable;
a)just before harvest when all the fruits(or other commercially used parts of a plant)
are present and are the same size as they will be at harvest;
b) a late season stage when all the fruits, etc., have set or, if they have not, their
probability of contributing to the final yield is virtually zero;
c) early season, when leaves and stems are formed but the fruits are not completely set; and d)before planting.
The first and the last of these can be set aside for the purpose ofthe present discussion.
The first deals with the stage at which the crop sampling scheme, described in the previous
chapter, applies. It may technically be regarded as a forecast in that the estimates are
actually made before harvest, but in working practice in most cases in developing countries
the results are not available until the crop has moved to market.The last situation — namely
the forecasting of production prior to planting — is usually in a different category from the
rest as being based either on the economic relationships between prices of crops and other
related variables and the area planted as estimated from statistics for a run of years, or on
returns of farmers' "intentions". The two approaches are equally applicable to breeding
plans for livestock or any other consequence offarmers' production decisions.
For the rest — namely the late and the early season stages —three methods of approach
have been explored in various agricultural statistics departments. Of these methods, one is
based on reports from observers in the field who make appraisals of the general state of the
crop in their localities, the second uses historical relationships between weather and yield as
a basis for deriving a forecast of yield from the weather in the course of a current season,
and the third is based on objective measurements made on the crop. All three need to be
combined with early estimates of the area for harvest in order to derive a total production
for the geographical area. Various corrections and adjustments may be necessary to all the
basic figures designed to indicate likely rates of production. It is possible, though the
situation seems rare, for judgements to be arrived at not by an area estimate multiplied by a
yield estimate but directly in terms of overall tonnage for a particular locality.
The possible lines along which crop forecasting might develop are indicated in the
paragraphs which follow. However,some ofthem must be viewed as highly tentative because
the amount of well-consolidated forecasting work of good precision which is going on in
developing countries is rather small. Where a method has found commercial acceptance the
situation is usually one where a cash crop attracts an outstanding amount of interest and in
consequence it has been possible to assemble enough resources to build up a system of
judgement reports. Even so, it is not improbable that a wholly rigorous assessment of the
precision of early season forecasts would show only modest success. The impression
conveyed is often better than this because the actual manner of operation results in regular
revision of the results as the season proceeds so that,by the close,the forecasts have tapered


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 63
into estimates and thence into records of actual achievement. Where this happens the
identification of the degree of forecasting success is possible only by rather disruptive
postmortem examinations of successive forecasts compared with the final estimate. Some of
the methods which are suggested below are based on techniques in use in developing
countries, others on those from temperate countries modified by adjustment for conditions
in developing areas.
Early estimate of productive area
The possible need of early estimates of the productive area has been mentioned from time
to time in the course of the discussion on the techniques of crop area assessment. If the
forecasting organisation is based on plant counts and similar records which are made on
sub-samples of the sample used for crop area estimation it is likely that adequate early area
estimates will be obtainable. Where suitable area estimates are lacking it is usually either
because a significant proportion of the crop fails between the time of the 'passage'for area recording and the time of harvest or because the crop area suffers no change during growth
but there are too few field staff to complete the area recording in time.
There is no easy solution, the more promising of the lines of attack require substantial resources in cash and skilled manpower, and it is essential to look at the whole matter critically to decide whether the need for forecasts justifies the foregoing of so much other
work.Ifit does,then the resouces must be allocated. It may then be possible to speed up the
regular recording work to give the area records in time.If this is not possible,the next most
promising line for securing pointers to the extent of crop failure is to collect judgement reports on the subject from the field staff,from agricultural extension staff,or from village accountants. If the main problem is slowness in completing the area records it is possible
that reports from local observers giving their judgement of the current year's area as a
proportion of that of the previous year might be useful.
Historical comparisons of this kind are liable to suffer from various distortions — as the
U.S. Department of Agriculture Crop and Livestock Reporting Service experience
showed — but with the experience of several years for comparison the method might be
made serviceable. It is not an attractive prospect, however, except perhaps when some
agency — e.g. a marketing board or cooperative — has staff in close continuous touch with farmers.
Condition reports
In the world as a whole this method seems to be the most widely used though all
countries recognise that it is capable of giving dubious results on occasion. However, the
condition of a crop during growth seems to be something which observers are able to assess
with some measure of precision. Naturally, however, they cannot at a given point in time
forecast what will be the effect of weather and other influences between that point and the
final harvest. Generally speaking, there are two bases for such reports,the commonest being
an assessment of crop condition and the other a forecast of prospective yield in terms of
weight per unit area. It is found in practice that experienced observers ofcrops have an idea
in their minds of a 'full' or 'normal' crop rather than an average crop.In this context a full
crop is one that is healthy and undamaged by drought, insects, disease or accidents, and
which shows the state of growth which is reasonable under such favourable conditions.The
fact that crops do suffer damage from drought,pests,accidents,and so on,accounts for the
not uncommon remark that crops never come up to average these days! Condition reporting
is usually done in terms of percentages of the full or normal crop. However,it seems unlikely
that observers can identify more than a small number of sub-categories between crop failure
and crop perfection. It might well be enough to report on a scale of 1-10.There have been
in use from time to time reporting systems based on the number offingerstin a hand(a five
finger crop is perfect, a one finger crop is bad) or coins making up some coin of larger
denomination. If permanent reporters are being used there seems no particular advantage in
choosing this kind of device but it might be sensible to use a scale running from 0 to 10 to


64 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
narrow somewhat the range of choice which they feel is effectively offered them. Each
reporter needs to be told the precise geographical region for which he is responsible for reporting, the crops for which reports are required, and the dates on which they must be
despatched. It is probably particularly worthwhile to visit reporters engaged on this kind of
work and to discuss with them what is in the organiser's mind and what problems they are
themselves meeting.
The reports from the individual reporters should be passed to some form of local
scrutiny, but no amendment should be permitted at this stage. The regional officer
concerned may make a comment before passing on the document, or he may pass it back
and ask the field staff to look at it again. In the central office these indicators will be
weighted by area of crop in the respective localities and the consolidated estimate for each
main geographical region derived.It may be enough to leave these in this form,indeed unless
there are historical data which will provide a guide to the usual relationship between such
estimates and the final outturn,little else can be done. If such historical series are available
then a graph of condition against final outturn may indicate a relationship which will allow
the probable yield corresponding to the condition figure for the current year to be read off.
Obviously, all such relationships may change with changing technical conditions and this
must be watched for. Though in principle it may be possible to derive mathematical
relationships between condition, other variables(such as pest damage or the weather) and
final yield, it seems usually to have been found convenient to deal in graphical terms. Where
a major deviation between the condition and the final outturn is experienced a second
influencing variable may be available — for example, reports of pest damage or rainfall
records for a critical stage in the growth. A useful correction may be obtained by drawing in
on the first graph the line — linear or curved — which most closely fits the plotted points.
Lines drawn by eye are likely to be quite serviceable for this purpose. A note is now made
each year of the difference between the actual value of the final yield and the value on the
line of best fit corresponding to the condition estimate for that year.These values are then
graphed against the pest or weather variable and a line of best fit for that graph drawn in,
too. As the condition and pest, etc., data come in each year the corresponding yield values
on the lines of best fit of the two graphs are noted. The sum of these two values gives the forecast yield.
A rough and ready indication of the likely harvest level should be obtainable from this
kind of procedure. It might be that one of the techniques based on objective measurement
described below, may be more suited to important crops but if a condition reporting system
is already in use it would be wise to continue it in addition. To push judgement reporting to
much higher levels of precision would probably require some individual person to take a
continuing interest in the forecasting methods for a particular crop. It might be well worth
arranging this if a field organisation is available through whichjudgement reports of this kind
can be obtained. The objective method described later offers prospects of methodical
improvement but there is likely to be a long way to go before success is achieved with it;a
more straightforward approach in terms which look sensible to the ordinary trader and farmer is not to be despised.
Crop-weather relationships
Since the weather noticeably affects yield, there has been for many years an interest all
over the world in relating crop yields to weather factors. It seems natural to believe that if
the weather in the growing season is so influential on the crop growth then from historical
relationships, combined with current observations of meteorological data a good forecasting
system should be derivable. In spite of the very large amount of work that has gone into this
procedure, its representation in the world's forecasting schemes is slight. For a few years
certain spring-sown wheat and other grain areas in North America used weather conditions
and subsoil moisture conditions alone as indicators of prospective yield but this has been
abandoned as a sole method. Early attempts in any particular situation are usually
encouraging but beyond a certain point discouragement sets in. Part of the problem is the
sensitivity of the crop to conditions in specific short periods of its growing phases and


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 65
meteorological records for these periods may not be easy to assemble. Again, important
weather factors may come late in the season. For instance, root crops are very sensitive to
growing conditions in the last few weeks before harvest and all grain crops are very sensitive
to conditions at harvest. Any number of weather relationships and yield can be explored
using regression analysis if so desired. For exploratory purposes the procedure of plotting
the yield against the apparently most important variable and drawing in a line of best fit,
then plotting the deviations from this line against the second factor, and so on, may be revealing.
Generally speaking, it seems best to look to weather to provide a modifying factor for a
crop reporting or an objective measurement system rather than the main indicator.
Crop measurements
For many years attempts have been made to find single measurements of a crop which
will provide useful indicators of the final outturn. For instance,the height of cinchona trees
and the diameter of their branches seem to give a useful indication of their yield of bark.
Not many useful results of this work seem to have appeared. Oflate the approach has been
by way of analysing the progress of particular fruits through the season in relation to the
stage of maturity which they have reached at the time of observation.
In the opening paragraphs of this chapter two situations were noted as applicable to
forecasting, one of these relates to the later part of the season after the fruit or other
commercial product is set. Here the important feature is the probability of survival of these
fruits up to the time at which they are harvested. Earlier in the season,before the individual
fruits are set,reliable indicators of outturn are much harder to obtain.
For the later stages,basically there are three elements in the picture,
a)how many fruits there are,
b)how many will survive to be harvested,
c)what will they weigh.
The way ahead in this work appears to be to pick an important crop and to aim at
quantifying these various elements by concentrating on this crop all the available staff in an
effort to get as many relevant observations as possible.
The approach has usually been to classify the fruits observed at any particular time of
observation into maturity classes. Then the number of fruits surviving at harvest from the ith
maturity class can be regarded as given by Pi Ni, where Ni is the number of fruits in the
maturity class i at the time of observation and Pi is the probability that fruits in that class
will survive to harvest. The total number of fruits harvested will be given by summing this
product for all the maturity classes.
To quantify these relationships two sets of observations are needed. One is a count ofthe
number of fruits in the various maturity classes; this requires a definition of maturity class.
This matter of definition has produced some difficulties and some short cuts. As noted
below, they are fairly easy to define for cotton; they have proved largely irrelevant for
oranges; but for maize they have been very difficult to define in a form which is easy for
field staff to operate. For a particular crop in a particular locality the consultation between
agricultural botanists,husbandry staff, and statistical staff is highly desirable. The estimation
of survival probability is made by noting the survival oftagged fruit on sample plots.
There remains the question of weight of fruit per unit at harvest. This may be obtainable
from historical averages — average weight of a field crop, ear of grain, cob, etc., may have
been recorded for various purposes. If this source cannot be relied on it may be necessary to
work out growth relationships from actual observations and sampling of fruits with a view to
defining the final weight of fruits which have a known weight at a given stage in the season.
Obviously this particular element could be the subject of elaborate enquiries, but-in the
season or two while exploratory work is going on, there should be little difficulty in
arranging to weigh some commercially harvested crops and get a working estimate of average
fruit weight. Refinements could then be pushed ahead as opportunity offered.
It is likely that any department undertaking objective plant measurements as the basis of


66 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
forecasting will already be engaged on fairly large scale sampling enquiries directed to crop production estimation.1 Given such field enquiries, the sensible approach would be to add
measurements of the crop in the course of growth to the routine observations for acreage
and final yield. This extra work can, initially, best be put on teams with slightly easier
working conditions or somewhat more competent staff. Using these facilities the basic
relationships, probabilities, and mean weights can be explored. Further, some attention
should be given to the best way of using available time. Where the work has been done most
extensively (in North America) much smaller plots have been used for crop sampling than
are recommended here for conditions in developing countries. Consequently it is likely to be
better to count the plants per quadrat of the size recommended (plant counts tend to be
easy to do)and then to take a small representative sample from the total quadrat,perhaps
20 stems or 2square metres,for a count offruits per plant.
The way ahead towards results of substantial interest seems clear. However,it would be
rash to be over-optimistic; there are many variables and each technical change will make a
change in the relationships. It seems highly important that the need for forecast should be
weighed with great thoroughness and if at all possible, the work concentrated on no more
than two crops until some light is seen into the best working techniques.
