XI STATISTICS OF PERENNIAL CROPS
Introduction
Perennial crops set rather different problems from the annual crops which have been the
subject of the preceding chapters. In some ways the situation is simpler since the crops are
relatively stable in productive area, so that rapid or frequent surveys for the purpose of
estimating area are not required. On the other hand, a number of them present special
problems either of identification, or recording of produce — for example some yield fairly
steadily all around the year which means that there is never a single occasion when the
season's products can be assessed.
For convenience this chapter is concerned with all kinds of crops which occupy the
ground for, say, two years or more and with the estimation both of plant numbers(or area) and production.
Perennial crops can be divided for statistical purposes into about four categories.
Estate production
A number of perennial crops,— rubber and coffee are examples in certain countries — are
grown in solid blocks on a regular plan under highly organised management systems. The
same species may be grown by quite different methods in the same areas,also. Statistics of
acreage, production or forecasts of production, of the estate element in a country present
no basic difficulty since the office records of the controlling agencies customarily contain
such information. The information may not be forthcoming unless statutory requirements
are imposed but from the statistician's standpoint the problem is straightforward.It is highly
desirable when collecting statistics from this type of producer to make sure that the scope of
the enquiries matches the prevailing range of interest in the changes occurring in the sector.
For instance it may be less interesting to know details of the production of,say, rubber,
than to know whether the enterprise is changing over from rubber to other products, or
whether it is ceasing to expand in rubber, mainly because it has begun to channel its
expansion on newly cleared land into some other product. This is not, of course, likely to
happen with an alert statistical service but there is some risk that, out of custom, the
statistics will have concentrated so entirely on the former key crop that the initial indicators
ofa changed investment policy may pass not,indeed,unnoticed,but unquantified.
Commercial tree crops owned by smallholders.
These may be of the same varieties as those to be found on plantations or they may be of varieties which customarily are not used in estate production. Many coconut groves are
operated by small family operators rather than by joint stock companies. There is no basic
difficulty in extending the type of sampling programme outlined for crop area and
production estimation for annual crops to embrace permanent crops. For example,if the
scheme provides for farmers as primary sampling units,then the area of trees ofthe required
crop operated by the farmer can be recorded as if they were annual crops. If the trees are
irregularly planted it may make better sense to abandon attempts at area estimation and to
count the number ofindividual trees.
There is a certain range of marginal problems attaching to tree crop estimation by
sampling techniques, however. For example the custom in ceitain countries may provide for
tree crops to be held under different systems of tenure from that applyipg to other uses of
the land on which they grow. Consequently a farmer may be chosen as a primary sample


68 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
unit and indicate to the enumerator his farm land, but an entirely different set of enquiries might be needed in order to elucidate which trees he has rights in. Moreover, it is at least possible that he may have moved his home but retained rights in trees which he planted in his original home area, or if he may have gone to some distance to find land on which to plant permanent crops (as has been the case of cocoa in some countries in West Africa). These circumstances may result in the field staff having to travel much farther than was expected when the programme was worked out. Perhaps more dangerous to the success of the enquiry is the probability that farmers will be reluctant to admit to having trees at a
distance if this would mean a trek across country at a time of year when they do not want to
leave home. It is quite possible under those conditions for the crop to be under-estimated in
the sampling studies.
Wild economic trees.
In some parts of the world it is customary for the local people to harvest the produce of
trees without having any personal and continuing rights in them. In these circumstances it
may be of interest to have an estimate of the total number of trees in a locality as a general
indication of the production potential.It is likely under these conditions that the proportion
of the total fruit which is harvested will vary from season to season(as for instance 'wild' oil
palm production in West Africa). Some approach to estimating total numbers in restricted
localities can be achieved by walking along bush paths, treating them,in effect,as transects
and counting the trees within,say,fifty feet of the path on either side,(the width estimated
by eye but checked from time to time by measurement). Other approaches have used linear
transects of a chain in width at regular intervals across the area. Typically,a road is used as
the baseline, and from points chosen along it transects are traced on a compass bearing.The
trees are counted, either completely or in sections at intervals, depending on the resources available and the size of the country to be covered. This approach is really better treated as
part of a general economic survey of the commodity concerned than as an element in
statistical work. Unless trees are firmly within the economic sector is is probably not worth
spending many statistical resources on them.
Any perennial crop lends itself rather better than annual crops to obtaining information
from aerial photographs. Solid blocks of planted trees show up clearly on aerial photographs,
unless they have been planted under taller shade trees, and estimates can be taken by
outlining the block concerned on the prints in chinagraph pencil, and then measuring the
area covered by a grid or planimeter. Counts of individual trees scattered through mixed
vegetation, if they can be identified without difficulty,are probably best made on a random
selection of prints from the whole mosaic. If the trees are close together so that a standard
print would involve a very large amount of counting, then randomly sited quadrats or
transects seem equally applicable though no very ample literature on the subject appears to
have been published for agricultural crops(though there is an extensive literature on forest estimation,of course).
In all records of area or numbers of economic trees,it is essential to distinguish significant
age categories. The particular ones relevant to the crop and the locality can be determined in
consultation with the technical staff, but, broadly, it is important to distinguish trees that
are different in respect of their bearing capacity rather than to distiguish age for age's sake.
Those too young to bear should be distinguished from the others,trees which have begun to
bear but which are not yet in full bearing,and full bearing trees. It may also be necessary to
distinguish those that are, in fact, broken down and scarcely fruiting. These distinctions are essential if reasonable production estimating is to be obtained.
Production estimation
Basic principles of estimating production do not differ from those described for annual
crops. In some situations judgement reports are reasonably satisfactory and various techniques of crop sampling have been tested. It is only fair to state that it has proved very difficult to handle some tree crop estimating and forecasting procedures satisfactorily.


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 69
A basic system would proceed somewhat as follows. Starting with the farmer as primary
sampling unit, his plantation would be surveyed and two quadrats,each of an area which on
average would contain about ten trees,sited. The trees are then recorded one by one with an
assessment of age, a strip of bark or palm frond being tied round each as it is counted to
avoid double counting. Two trees in each quadrat might then be selected at random and the
primary branches of each counted and recorded. One branch from each tree is then selected
at random and the fruit on that branch picked and weighed. The successive sampling
fractions are then applied to the records offruit weight for the individual branches to derive
an estimate ofthe total production for the geographical unit desired.
A very similar process can be used in the early part of the year after the fruit has formed,
to obtain a count of the number of fruits on the branch. If they are small (for example,
coffee) a feasible procedure is to strip the fruits into a container and weigh them;a portion
of two or three kilos is weighed and the fruits counted. The ratio from the weighed sample
provides a conversion factor from fruit weight to fruit numbers which can be applied to the
tree yield. These records might need to be made separately for fruits of each maturity stage
as described in the previous chapter.If it can be assumed that they will all survive to harvest
then all that is required is an estimate of the mean fruit weight at harvest which might be
obtained from tests made for experimental purposes in earlier years.Such results should be
re-checked by observations on fruit from sample farms as soon as possible.
It may not always be feasible to destroy the produce of whole branches of a producer's
fruit harvest in the early part ofthe season without loss of goodwill,even if he is paid for his
loss. If an attempt is made to avoid this by counting fruits per branch without picking them
a very rigorous programme of testing should be built into the enquiry.Even with quite good
working conditions and large fruits it has been found very difficult to get consistency
between observers in counting the same.fruit on the same branch;this appears to be a line of
work where there is much still to be discovered.
Suggestions for useful approaches to the handling of crop estimation in smallholdings
may be obtainable from a group of enquiries made in recent years in U.A.R. The details
there varied with the crop, and they will no doubt vary from place to place for the same
crop, so great detail of description seems unnecessary. Broadly, the enquiries started with
records of plantations containing the economic trees concerned with an indication of the
number of trees, the varieties and the ages. However, the circumstances in which these
details were compiled meant that they could not be regarded as accurate. Hence this group
of studies also sought to provide an improved estimate of the number of trees as well as of
the yield per tree.The location ofthe plantations was ascertained from local records or with
local help and they were listed in sequence according to the lie of the land.They were then
grouped to give primary sampling units of rather similar size—the sizes of the individual
plantations varied considerably. In one study such units included 300 date palms,in another
about 2,000 citrus, and in another about 800 banana hills. (If there is any degree of
standardisation in planting it might be reasonable to use a more-or-less standard area oftrees,
in place of numbers of trees, for some kinds of basic record.)Five to seven percent of these
primary sampling units were then chosen at random for detailed recording.The trees on each
were recorded on a map ofthe unit concerned,the position of each tree being indicated,and
the characteristics, such as variety, age, and status(in the case oftrees thrown up as suckers
from other trees) were indicated. This information provided the basis for an accurate
assessment of the numbers of each variety and age group, using the sampling fraction for
raising. The numbers of trees in the several selected units were then totalled cumulatively
and 5 trees were chosen at random within each primary sampling unit. Associated with each
of these were the two adjacent trees, making a total of 15 trees for the unit(i.e.5 groups of
3). These groups were then visited at harvest and the crop weighed.Variants of this method
were necessary if, as in the case of bananas, the product is not harvested at a specific time
but may be cropped more or less evenly through the year. In this case sufficient time was
spent at the sites concerned on a sufficiency of occasions to note the total number of
bunches which had already reached maturity in the course of that season(asjudged by the
cut-off stalks), as well as those which were in course of growth or were being harvested at


