the time of the recorder's visit. Where possible the field observers tried to visit the plantation
on the days when the merchants arrived to harvest the crop.
The fact that the field records consisted of sketch maps showing individual trees made
such sequenced recording feasible. However, the system may be difficult to operate
successfully without fairly ample labour for field work.
In studies of coconuts various approaches have been used,depending on the data available
to form a frame and the size ofthe holdings. When units offairly small size can be identified
for use as primary sampling units (with necessary data for raising) all the trees on the units
(say, smallholdings) can be numbered serially and a random sample taken from the whole
list. About four trees per smallholding has seemed useful for yield observations.Some gain in
convenience without appreciable loss in precision may come from choosing only one tree per
holding and associating with it the next three in serial order.
For large blocks of planting a similar system ofsampling from a serially numbered list of
trees might be developed provided an intermediate stage were inserted in which units of
reasonably manageable size were selected from within the total block. If the scale of
production precluded this then a means of estimating the density of trees per acre would be
needed to allow records of the areas under trees to be converted to numbers oftrees.Sample
quadrats of40 yds x 40 yds appear to be suitable for this.
If planting is in regular rows,a tree can be chosen by reference to a random choice ofrow
and of tree within the row. Such a tree can then be made a defined corner(e.g.the S.West)
of the 40 yds x 40 yds quadrat. For yield records this designated tree and the three others
next nearest within the plot may be used.
For cocoa, techniques seem to have been worked out in the main areas but, broadly
speaking, the practices already outlined above are applicable under such conditions.The plot
size used appears to be about 18 trees per farmer. Complete pod count is usually made of all
the pods at intervals through the season—in September, November, April and May, for
instance, under categories of "fermentable" and "unfermentable". Later, the pods actually
harvested are recorded. Which techniques will be most suitable and what sampling fraction to use at the various
stages depend so greatly on local conditions and the availability of background data that
each design must be matched to the particular case. Arrangements must usually be made to
provide for recording yields over a period of several months since the harvest may not be
concentrated at one point in the year.It is usually necessary also to arrange for records to be
made of annual crop planting amongst tree crops, and also of grubbing and re-planting
activities on the trees. These are probably best handled by pursuing the enquiries for a
sub-sample of the chosen primary sampling units. Perhaps the greatest comfort to the
statistician in dealing with tree crops is that the productive area—except following boom
planting times—tends to change rather slowly,so his records,once made,stay up to date for
some while. A possible technique for forecasting tree crop production before harvest where the
outturn is ultimately known depends on this rather slow change in the productive area.
Where there is a body of observant and intelligent field staff(normally,men with the basic
training and experience with views of their own)they can be asked to state when the crop is
set what proportion of a normal crop they think is standing that year.Some systems refer to
a 'par' crop and ask the reporters for an assessment based on a 'par' crop as 100. If the
consensus is 120 compared with "par = 100" and if it later becomes known that 6000 tons
was marketed one might reasonably conclude that a par crop was 6000 +120 x 100= 5000
tons. If the reporting is well founded and no abrupt changes in planting or grubbing occur,
then the estimates of a 'par' crop from a series of years should show some
consistency—possibly tending upwards by a few percentage points each year in reflection of
tree growth. If such consistency is found it provides the basis for a forecasting system; if it is not
found then it should clearly indicate to the field staff that their judgement is of doubtful
value and clear the ground for an attack on the problem using objective methods.
