XII STATISTICS OF MIXED CROPS & OTHER SPECIAL CASES
Introduction
So far, various problems of defining boundaries of crop areas have been referred to but it
has been tacitly assumed that there is never any doubt about the definition of the crops
which lie within the defined boundaries. However,in many countries this is far from being
the case; local practices in the growing of food crops and even of cash crops may include
mixing them on the same land surface. This may be found in a variety of forms,some of
them representing crops coincidental in time,some successional.
The simplest situation is that where a parcel of land is planted in a number of portions,
each under a different pure crop. Thus, one field might be divided into four—one part
planted with maize, one with millet, one with groundnuts, and one with cassava. Often,
however,the mixing is done by interplanting the various crops either by alternating rows or
by a scattering of the plants of the one crop among the plants ofanother.Sometimes these
mixtures consist of one crop which is quite clearly dominant with a few scattered plants of
another. For example, in some places women have the established right of planting a thin
scatter of cotton plants in the main cropped area,but the density per unit area is by custom
kept very low. Other examples are fairly simple mixtures of two or three crops which are
part of the regular and established pattern of cropping in the neighbourhood.The individual
plants are scattered but the relative importance of the various crops making up the mixture
is more or less constant from farmer to farmer and field to field. There are situations,too,
where there is a great number of mixtures but experts can yet recognise them as having some
individual identity. Finally there are cases where the whole pattern of cropping has broken
down and odd attempts at planting various crops are scattered throughout the cropped land
in more or less complete confusion.
One might add to this the various kinds of successional cropping which are discussed
below. These may bring in not only mixtures of annual crops but mixtures of annuals and
perennials.
Statistics of mixed crops is probably the most discussed subject in this whole field of
agricultural statistics in developing countries,but no satisfactory solution has emerged that is
both technically satisfactory and practically feasible.
The existence of the problem indicates that there is uncertainty as to exactly what is to
be measured by statistics of crop area. Tacitly it is assumed that in the various respects in
which users are interested one hectare ofcrop A in one locality is sufficiently like one hectare
of crop A in another locality for comparisons of the two or aggregates of figures from the
two places to have some meaning. Clearly in the present context there is a question whether
meaningful results can be obtained by aggregating area statistics from pure stands and from
mixed crops.
Approach to measurement
The various types of crops briefly noted above present different problems to the
agricultural statistician. First, the situation where a parcel of land is divided into portions,
each of which is planted to pure stand, presents very little difficulty beyond the extra time
needed in the field to complete the more detailed records. There is some possibility of doubt
about the correct way of drawing the boundary between crops of rather different growth
and cropping patterns, but this is not usually a matter of very great significance. For
example, grains might be planted broadcast at intervals of a few inches, whereas maize might
be planted in rows at distances of two or three feet. It is usually possible to regard the part


72 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
plot under maize, to take this example, as ending at a distance outside the last row equal to
half the inter-row distance. If it is not planted in regular rows then half the interplanting
distance beyond the last plants might be a reasonable limit at which to draw the assumed
sub-plot boundary.
The cases where there is a single main crop with a very small scattering ofsecondary crop
can probably,for practical purposes,be regarded as a pure stand ofthe principal crop and be
entered accordingly.
The rest, namely those where the individual plants of the several species concerned are
scattered between each other, admit no such straightforward solution. One possible
approach might be to regard each component of the crop as occupying the whole area. In
effect this is saying that a mixture of maize, groundnuts, and cassava is not fundamentally
different from a mixture of maize and weeds from the standpoint of statistical reporting.
The maize and weed crop would be regarded without compunction for statistical purposes as
being "maize", notwithstanding the competition of the weeds with the crop. Groundnuts
and cassava could be regarded as merely weed-like competitors for maize, and similarly for
the others. The total area under crop, if the statistics are handled in this way, would
naturally be treble the total superficial area of the land, but this effect must always be
expected where more than one crop is grown in the course of a season,whether they follow
one after another, whether they partially overlap each other, or whether they are mixed,
planted and harvested more or less at the same time. Again,an attempt might be made,to
divide the area under the mixed crop between the individual crops concerned on some
concept of the "proportion of the area occupied" by the respective crops. This demands
rather complex perception on the part of the observer. What he can assess on the ground is
likely to be either the numbers of plants of the respective crops per unit area, or the
proportion of the ground surface covered by the plants of the respective crops. In some
instances, the proportionate coverage is something that might, with practice, be judged
without too much difficulty. However, the plants in some mixtures differ considerably in
height and the same piece of ground surface may be covered by two or more sets offoliage.
If we remember also that the observer may well try to take into account the appearance of
pure stands of the respective crops in making his mental calculations for sub-dividing the
superficial area between the various components of the mixed crop, it is clear that the
principles on which such a division can be made are likely to be very difficult to standardise.
Another approach might be to convert the plants on any given superficial area of the
mixed crop to their equivalent in pure crop. This again reguires an appraisal of density of
plants in a reference pure stand and in the mixed stand. Moreover,it requires some tacit
allowance for the possibility of competition between the various components.For instance,
if the same number of plants ofcrop A appear in a mixture as in a pure crop,the plants of A
presumably are no more competitive with each other in the mixture than in the pure stand
and the same would appear to hold for B.But to what extent does A compete with B in the
mixture and hence to what extent must correction be applied to the acreage ofthe mixture
in order to convert it to the "equivalent area" ofcrop A?Some reporting services have met
this problem by not probing too deeply into the conceptual difficulties noted here,leaving
their field staff with instructions to sub-divide the land by judgement. Others have
subdivided the mixed acreage between the components,or calculated pure stand equivalent,
on the basis of seed rates.
All these methods appear to be building a reporting service with dubious bases of
assessment. This is so even if in each locality there is a pure stand of each crop which can
form a basis of comparison — in many areas,one would need to travel a long distance before
finding anyone planting certain crops in pure stand, so local reporters could have no
experience of this. It is arguable that it should possible to standardise some ofthe elements
ofjudgement criticised here by using sampling techniques to form estimates of plant density.
This is probably true,but assessment of area and of yield per unit area places a heavier strain
on field services than they can bear in most countries, and the addition of a density
measuring stage would increase the strain still further.
The most satisfactory way ahead for these mixtures where the individual plants are


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 73
scattered would seem to be to record the areas concerned as being under the various
specified crop mixtures. It may be possible to describe these in terms of principal crops and
secondary crops or it may be possible merely to refer to them as including the several crops.
This should give some indication of the cropped land-use which has bearing on the
description of agricultural practice and,for this purpose,is likely to be more revealing than
any of the derived estimates of "area equivalent" which are referred to above. The
straightforward cases where a crop is in virtually pure stand,or where the plot is broken into
sub-plots with pure crops,would be handled in the manner suggested above.
It would then be at the production stage that an attempt would be made to supply, on
the one hand,estimates ofthe area ofthe crop gown in mixtures which could be associated
with the part grown in pure stand where this occurred, and on the other estimates of yield
per unit of superficial area. The estimation of yield per unit area for mixed crops is, in
principle, practicable using the crops sampling techniques described here, but there appears
to be no experience of continued successful handling byjudgement methods.The approach
using sampling requires the normal procedure of selection in one or more stages to give a
final list of primary sampling units, such as farmers. The area of each field operated by
selected farmers is recorded at a suitable point in the growing season and these field records
stratified on the basis ofthe crop mixtures indicated therein.Sampling for crop cutting then
proceeds within these strata, but otherwise in the normal way. At harvest the plots are cut
either crop by crop or the ground is cleared and the material then sorted,the economic parts
separated and weighed, and provision made for drying samples as required. It has to be
recognised, however, that the presence of a number of crops on the same piece of land
greatly complicates the already awkward step of getting the enumerator on the spot at the
right time. It may well be that enumerators must be brought back to the field as many times
as there are crops. If we bear in mind that some crops (e.g. cotton) must individually be
harvested on more than one occasion, the burden ofthis kind of work is clear. Nonetheless,
there appears to be no short cut and where the need is pressing appropriate resources will
have to be made available.
This seems to be a case, as for livestock, where considerable care should be applied in
assessing the exact needs of statistical material.Some of this work would be easier to handle
by resident reporters conducting farm record enquiries. It might be best to arrange for a
country-wide sampling scheme to get a pattern ofland use for mixed crops and to depend on
records from a much smaller, and perhaps scarcely representative set of closely recorded
farms to provide interpretative yield data to amplify the area record.
In conclusion, it might be noted that ifit is desired to have available some area equivalent
for the crops grown in mixed stand, a rational one could be derived from estimates of yield
of pure stand and by dividing the production of a crop in all kinds of mixtures by the
average yield,in pure stand,a "pure stand equivalent" ofthe mixture can be derived.
Successional Cropping.
It is common practice where the climate permits rapid growth for more than one crop to
be produced within twelve months. If the practice is simply to plant one crop, wait for its
maturity, harvest it,then plant another crop and waits for its maturity,no specific statistical
problem arises. It is necessary to arrange sufficient visits to the chosen sample areas to allow
the necessary records to be made but,this apart,the work is not particularly difficult. Other
examples of successional cropping may present their own problems. For instance, where a
piece ofland is cropped with annual crops but has,for example,bananas and citrus plantings
added,it is likely that it will remain as an area ofinterest for the farmer in respect of these
"tree" crops even if the area has reverted more or less to bush as far as annual cropping is
concerned. Here, too, the principle may cause no trouble although the cost in time of
keeping track of this kind of marginal production activity may be heavy.. This problem may
reach its gravest form in some tropical areas without very much variation in growing season,
where the agricultural practice provides for a more or less amoeboid growth of cropping.
Thus Ugandan practice in some parts might begin with a planting of bananas which was split,


74 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
one part of the plot a few weeks later being planted to beans,maize,potatoes,cassava,and
the rest left in bananas only. The banana portion might then be combined with a portion
cleared somewhat later and interplanted with a mixture similar to the first. A little later an
additional plot of land might be cleared adjacent to another of these parcels,And a further
crop planted extending across both of them As the months pass, crops will be harvested
when they come to maturity and the land replanted with an annual if the spacing of the
bananas permits it,or it might be left as a pure banana garden.
One can only say that under conditions such as these it is extremely difficult even with
intensive concentration of field staff to keep a track of the progress of cropping and the
extent of cropped area over a twelve-months season.It is inconceivable that any economical
method of handling the problem could be devised, and the best approach appears to be to
seek from larger scale enquiries an estimate ofthe total cropped area in use by the farmers of
the localities concerned and then, using intensive surveys of small samples, to build up a
picture ofthe detailed cropped land use and the consequences in terms of output.
Incompletely harvested crops.
Crops in all types of agriculture may remain unharvested,sometimes for technical reasons
such as the land being too wet to allow access, sometimes for economic reasons such as
failure of the market for the produce and the consequent lack of incentive to harvest it.
What might be regarded as a special case of this "economic" cause ofincomplete harvesting
is found when more of a reserve crop is planted than farmers find they need for subsistance
use. The classic case of this is cassava in West Africa.
Cassava is grown in some situations as a cash crop and the ground is completely cleared
when it reaches maturity, which may be from nine months after planting onwards.However,
it may also be planted as a reserve crop — it is under shifting cultivation often the last crop
in a cycle before the land is allowed to go back to bush. Under these conditions the usual
practice is to harvest what is required, choosing the younger plants which are likely to be
more palatable,and allow the rest to go to waste.Somewhat the same situation may hold for
some tree crops — for example,plantains.
There are, of course, two different possible definitions of production in such
circumstances. Actual production, as represented by the product harvested and used, and
potential production as represented by the total crop whether harvested or not.It should be
noted that, whereas the former is a resonably clear-cut concept the latter is much less so
since the weight of produce which is regarded as being available will depend on the date at
which the assessment is made. As one passes from the early days ofthe growth ofthe tuber
the weight available will increase until a stage is reached where parts must be regarded as
inedible even under conditions of grave food shortage. After a certain point these must be
written off for food purposes and the quantity effectively available will begin, perhaps, to
fall unless new growth has taken over by then.
This "cassava problem" is another which has attracted a great deal of discussion and
cannot be said to have been solved. Some progress in any particular situation would be
represented by agreement on which of these definitions it is desirable to use.Production for
market for which the crop is completely cleared presents no more difficulty than the
production of any other cash crop. If potential production is required then no special
difficulty, other than the work involved, is presented by a sampling scheme undertaken at a
suitable period after the planting of the crop. This might be towards the end of the twelve
month period in the case of cassava in many situations but varieties vary in the periods in
which they come to maturity. If actual production is required the problem may be of a
different order. Usually it is not possible to estimate harvested area because users pick a root
here and a root there from amongst the growing crop as seems best to them. Under these
conditions the only reasonable approach is to include the production of the cassava within
the recording programme for a farm output survey of the kind outlined in the following
chapter. Though in the context of developing countries cassava provides the commonest example
of incomplete harvesting it may also be found in cash-crops when market conditions are too


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 75
discouraging to justify further harvesting towards the end ofthe season.The remaining crop
may be ploughed in, fed to livestock or otherwise disposed of. If this kind of incomplete
harvesting is a significant feature estimates can probably be provided by reporters who are in
touch with growers. Reports are usually made in terms of "proportion of the crop
unharvested" or "proportion of the area unharvested" as may seem appropriate in view of
harvesting custom. In practice, only a rather broad indicator of abandonment may be
possible. One set of problems arises from uncertainties about definitions when growers pick
over the best from their crop and leave some crop on all plants. It must be expected, too,
that there will be a tendency to exaggerate abandonment of crop in the atmosphere of
disappointment of a bad season.
