XIII LIVESTOCK NUMBERS AND PRODUCTION
Introduction
Collection of livestock statistics in developing countries is usually either a reasonably
straightforward process or an intensely difficult one. Among the few reasonably
straightforward situations are first, those where there is a body offairly large farmers,with
well organised and managed herds, who can supply information as required from their own
records, and second, those cases where livestock are an integrated part of small-farm
production systems where a sample survey of farms can be designed to provide the required
material. However,in many countries the majority oflivestock is to be found in the nomadic
sector, often where the economic use is secondary to the social value of the stock, or in
small herds associated to some degree with farms but often grazed at a distance from the
farming headquarters. This being so,it seems necessary to proceed with livestock statistical
work with a good deal of circumspection; probably the following three stages provide the
best way ahead.
The various requests for statistics of livestock should be scrutinized with great care to
assess the possibility of meeting the threshhold level of completeness and precision necessary
to meet the need. This may require long and patient consultation with potential users;
veterinary, agricultural and statistical departments may all be concerned.This process should
include the drafting of the specific questions which the statistics are expected to answer and
preparing an explicit statement on whether the data in prospect would,in fact,supply this
guidance if they were available. For example, if their value for assessing overstocking
problems was the basis of a request for data on livestock numbers, would a knowledge of
current livestock numbers alone advance the understanding of evolving conditions? Is the
need perhaps,for an indication of the trend in livestock numbers,rather than for an estimate
of their absolute numbers? Again, would statistics of population be much guide to potential
market supply if the annual output of stock for sale is no more than 5-10 per cent ofthe
population? Or again, is it possible to get any guidance about the significance of the
contribution of livestock products to human nutrition without a good indication of the
distribution of the product between sectors of the population,as well as an idea of the total
supply? It would seem that only a nutritional survey would throw much light on the
nutritional aspects of meat supply in most instances. If the answers to questions like these
are specifically 'yes', then some attempt along the lines indicated below should be made to
operate a sample enquiry to obtain the necessary information.
Low cost methods
If is found that the information is either not urgently needed or if available,it is unlikely
to provide the kind of guidance which is sought, then it seems best to get together the best
guidance which is to be obtained from available statistical material supplemented by
relatively cheap exploratory enquiries which give qualitative guidance. There is usually a
certain body of data already to hand in most countries including regular reports from the
Veterinary Departments which are compiled in the course of field veterinary control work;
data from taxation records where livestock taxes are in use: the results of various ad hoc
enquiries: the statistics of imports and exports of stock across land boundaries or through
ports, or their movement over recognised routes; etc., etc. Attempts should be made to list
all the markets through which livestock are sold, and,for as many as possible, to estimate
the numbers passing through at different seasons of the year. If numbers and quantitative
data are impossible to obtain the main pattern of seasonal variations should be sought


78 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
together with reports of the origin ofthe stock,their destinations,some idea oftheir weight,
and of the prices at which they change hands.Some idea is also desirable ofthe organisation
of the trade, the number of traders involved,their sizes,relationships and so on.In the large
towns there are usually slaughterhouses whose operations are closely controlled on hygenic
grounds, and numbers slaughtered here may be available. It may also be found that in the
regional offices of the civil administration, the agricultural department, or the veterinary
department, there are appreciably larger numbers of useful statistics than are to be found at
head office. However,it must be said that many ofthe available livestock statistics appear in
great detail but, when checked are incomplete to a substantial degree or conflict with each
other. No doubt in every country there are exceptions to this but it is a situation which it
would be wise to expect. Consequently, such staff as can be spared for specific enquiries
under these conditions might well be put on to operating check surveys in a selection of
situations which are planned to throw light on the degree of deviation ofthe departmental
statistics from the true situation. The villages or herds which might be covered in such
enquiries might well not be a proper representative sample of the whole but the results
should be a guide to the likely errors. Local opinions about such statistics should be treated
with reserve until objective comparisons can be made. Usually, too,information which will
allow a reasonably accurate indication of livestock output to be derived from the outline
statistics available is also lacking. Typically, there is a tendency to impute to the herds and
flocks of an undeveloped system a level of production a good deal higher than is likely to be
found in practice. Consequently it is desirable to get an indication of the main features of
herd structure(for example the proportion of cows to stock of various ages,etc.)and also of
the number of births per hundred head of breeding stock and the death rate in the first year
of life. Cattle are mainly in the main in mind here, but comparable information is relevant
for sheep and pigs also. It may not be necessary to undertake a time-consuming enquiry to
elucidate the main levels of these several items if available experience is tapped and the
indications cross checked. When assembled, this material should add up to a serviceable indication of the main
features of the livestock industry and may be sufficient for many purposes. To improve
greatly upon it is likely to demand very heavy expenditure of statistical resources. It is at
least possible and, some would say likely, that when the country's livestock owners who
have previously had little contact with an economic system move into the market,then the
prospect of collecting statistics will improve. This improvement may come in part from
greater interest on the part of the producers and in part from greater preparedness of
governments to vote funds for the study of a sector which is currently ofincreasing interest.
Sampling enquiries
So much for low cost assembly of statistical material. Where it is decided that some direct
and positive collection process should be undertaken, the way ahead which promises to be
most likely to be successful is outlined below.The history of work in this field suggests that
in any but straightforward cases, where the herds are part of the regular system ofa settled
agriculture,the propsects of initial success are small.
The frames and sampling units available for formal sampling enquiries depend on the
administrative and special enquiry records already available. For livestock which are part ofa
mixed farming system any of the frames already discussed as serviceable for locating farms
should be suitable for livestock enquiries. In this connection, it seems necessary to say that
while there is no reason in principle why livestock statistics should not be collected as part
of a multi-purpose subject enquiry, it is highly probable that this will not be successful. The
sort of attention which is required to obtain good livestock statistics is somewhat different
from that required for crops. For example, the livestock,although associated with the farm
quite closely, may be grazed away from the farm and the enumerator must be prepared to
persist until he actually sees them. If they are grazed at a distance of several miles it is
unlikely that his persistance will reach that far. Possibly, too, if he has had no personal
acquaintance with livestock he may look on this part of his job with little enthusiasm
anyway. Consequently though the frame may be the same as the one used for such crop


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 79
enquiries as have been described already,it is preferable to mount a separate study(possibly
with selected enumerators) — or at least a separate passage — for the purpose of collecting
livestock statistics. Since there is a very tempting argument for using the same travelling time
to collect as much information as possible on each visit, these problems of livestock
recording must be stressed if they are to be given the right weight when enquiries are being
planned. In this kind of enquiry rather special care is needed to watch for "landless holdings".This
may have different specific meanings in different places,but quite often there are producers
who are holders oflivestock but do not crop land and the need to include them in the frame
either together with the crop producers or in a separate section of it must be stressed.
Frames for specifically livestock enquiries are often derived from one. or other of the
veterinary records. These may list villages with the livestock in each, kraals or their
equivalent, or individual stock holders. A typical enquiry might proceed by stratifying the
villages by size as indicated by recorded numbers of stock; within villages herds might be
stratified by size. Some enquiries have then included about ten percent of the stock in the
final tabulation. It is possible that numerous variants on the most common pattern of
livestock holder will be found on enquiry. Thus there may be regular stock owners located in villages, all with more or less modest herds, possibly other stockholders scattered over the
countryside with similar sized herds, and still others with massive herds.In the latter case,it
might be desirable to make as complete an enumeration as possible of this large herd sector
while sampling for the rest.
It has been found on many occasions that initial enquiries by enumerators collecting
livestock statistics are only partially successful, less so even than for crop statistics.
Consequently, there is much to be said for so organising the enquiry that the stock owners
have a number of opportunities of contact with the enumerator so that, gradually, the full
total connected with their holdings may become apparent. It might be possible to arrange
this either by including the livestock work amongst the responsibilities of a resident
enumerator engaged on a range of statistical work — though being careful to guard against
the risks already noted in multi-subject enquiries which have already been noted — or by
alloting enumerators to livestock work for a period and getting them to collect a fair range
of inter-related information. This latter method is probably preferable if the circumstances
and staff make it feasible.In this case it might be arranged that they visit each holder once a
month, recording the livestock he holds at each date. The enumerators would move on
to other villages in the interval. They should be instructed to see any livestock that are to be
reported, though this may be,initially, a council of perfection. Additions to the herd,births, purchases, amalgamations of herds, etc.and also numbers leaving the herd by slaughter,sale,
or death should be recorded.Particular attention should be given to the death rate for young stock.
It must be recognised that, while a monthly visit may be as frequent as available staffing, and the patience of the livestock owner, permits it probably is too infrequent for reliable
working. If a two week cycle could be arranged it would be preferable in many instances;
circumstances differ but it is usually found that a month is rather a long time for accurate
memory. Even in developed countries where farmers may be vividly conscious of the
identity of individual days and dates in the month,it has been found that enquiries which
require them to remember the dates on which young stock were born or sows farrowed up to a month previously are subject to substantial "recall" error.
An intelligent enumerator with capacity to get on with livestock owners is likely to be
able to progressively bring his records up to improved states ofcompleteness. By noting any
discrepancies between the totals he had on the previous note and the reported ingoings and
outgoings, it should be possible to frame a line of conversation (rather than specific
cross-questioning) which will elucidate where the explanation lies. Each type ofinformation
then acts as a check on the other and both together promote completeness. Of course it
must be remembered that livestock owners tend to be independent people,even if they are
not superstituous about counting their stock,and may be reluctant cooperatotrs in statistical
studies.


