The categorisation of stock for these enquiries may need to be adjusted somewhat to
match the standard statistical returns required by national agencies under their commitments
to international statistical services, and the local needs. Generally, it is desirable to
distinguish between unimproved stock and improved stock, and amongst the improved,
between pure stock and graded-up stock. Usually in any particular situation the difference
between improved and unimproved is well recognised locally.It will also usually be desirable
to provide for separate figures to be collected about elements in the total population which
are directly indicative of output. For example, where milk is the main product the number
of lactating cows at different times of the year should be separately indicated. For meat
stock, which are fairly completely within the economic sector, the age classification is
usually as sound an indicator as is likely to be obtained. Given the age categories,the survival
rate of young born, and the numbers of stock sold or otherwise disposed of, a population
flow chart can be derived which gives valuable insight into the overall working ofthe system,
and provides cross-checks statistics one against another,and provides serviceable pointers to
the level of output.
Nomadic stock
The above enquiries point the way to compiling serviceable statistics, which one may
hope will progressively improve with experience of this work on the part of both the
producers and the statisticians. However, these methods have no practical application to
truly nomadic livestock. In principle, it might be possible to deal with them by a sampling
procedure, or an enumeration procedure, based on water holes, dipping gates, etc., but in
practice schemes of this kind that have extended to more than a small fraction of the
country, have not provided reliable results. Usually the veterinary department has certain
broad indications of the numbers ofstock concerned,and for most purposes this.is probably
all that it is worthwhile seeking to handle. There is the possibility of obtaining estimates of
livestock by aerial counts, either by photographing or by personal counting.This technique
has been used fairly extensively by ecologists interested in the large herbivores. For instance,
a slow aircraft operating at, perhaps, 80 knots can be used to count livestock in selected
areas, or in transects across larger portions of the country. It appears that the actual
counting does not present great problems unless very large herds are concerned and here
they can be photographed and counted at leisure. In principle, such photographs could be
used for deriving some age categorisation given the necessary conversion factors and from
linear dimensions on the photograph to age of beast. Clearly this would be a great deal easier
to do if the stock were distributed more or less evenly across the country,but any substantial
concentration at water holes, or under solid blocks of trees, might make reliable counting
much more difficult. It would seem from reports of ecological studies using this technique
that the method is applicable;the question is whether in any particular situation the interest
in the statistics is acute enough; ifit is,then it may be possible to secure the funds necessary
to contract with aircraft operators for the work.
The above discussion concerns livestock numbers with only incidental references to
production of live animals ready for slaughter, or milk, eggs, etc., for market. It must be
recognised that even in highly organised agricultures in developed countries, the estimation
of livestock and livestock product production is the least elegantly organised ofagriculture
statistics. Usually, the information cannot be obtained by methods parallel to those used for
crop production estimation, and where satisfactory data are available, they are almost
invariably obtained by returns from the purchasers of stock at bottlenecks such as markets,
slaughterhouses, or milk processing plants. Since the importance of centralised services of
this kind for livestock in developing countries is usually slight, the proportion of the total
output which can be counted in this way is small. Typically,statistics of numbers ofstocks
slaughtered in urban slaughter-houses are available under hygiene and sanitary regulations.
The milk handled in milk processing plants can usually be estimated from the records of
plants concerned; because of the cost of the equipment they tend to be reasonably
organised. With the arrival of "industrial" systems of poultry meat and egg production,
using hybrid stock, it might be possible to obtain indications of the usage of birds for this


