rejected in, say, European practice. On the other hand,some parts may be rejected for local
reasons which are regularly used elsewhere in the world. Presumably the interest in most
cases is in the supply of edible material for the local population, although on occasion the
possible supply offlesh as defined internationally may be the main interest.
These are not very satisfying proposals for handling livestock statistics but to judge from
reports of statistical work in this field in various developing countries, satisfaction is rare.
One point might be made in conclusion. Where specific development plans are underway for
livestock production, it is very desirable that the provision of adequate records should be
built into the development programme — and for this purpose the European style statistics
would often be a useful pattern.




