XIV FARM OPERATION AND BUDGETARY RECORDING
Introduction
In recent years it has come to be recognised that certain data which are essential for
general economic planning,for evaluation of projects,as well as for the work ofadvisers and
instructors seeking to help farmers, can only be obtained by rather intensive recording of
operations on as large a sample offarms as can be accommodated.In particular,information
is needed about the relationship between resource inputs(for example,inputs oflabour and
fertiliser) and product outputs. Involved in these assessments are clear and reasonably
quantitative descriptions of farm operations in terms of the work actually done,limitations
which farmers meet in their management, the results which they obtain, etc. There is no
reason, in principle, why this kind of information cannot be obtained using a rigorously
selected sample of farms,though,in practice, the cost in enumerator time may prevent the
sample being a large one and the need for some consistancy of cooperation may make some
selection other than of a representative nature unavoidable. It is probable that rather
different information will be needed for national planning,regional planning,and individual
farm management purposes, but the following notes take a general-purpose line embracing
something of all ofthem.
Frames
The normal frame such as is used for enquiries into crop acreage,or livestock numbers,is
suitable for this work provided that farms can be identified. Typically,farms selected for
intensive enquiries of this kind might be a subsample of the sample drawn for acreage
estimation purposes. Where a major cash crop is part of the farming system, it has been
found useful to stratify the farms which are included in the sample by reference to their area
of cash crops, and to draw the sample for the farm records from within these strata.If cash
crop area were not available for a large sample of farms (e.g. if a sample were drawn
specifically for a "management type" enquiry) the necessary data might be obtained from a
pre-enumeration survey oflimited scope.
Scope
The detailed content of the enquiry in a particular case may reflect the active interests of
the potential users, but in many instances the information sought will include the
following, — basic areas of the main crops both cash and subsistence; numbers oflivestock,
both on the farm and held elsewhere;land held but not cropped and land to which access for
grazing is permitted; inputs of farm resources, including labour, fertilisers, sprays and any
other inputs that are relevant in the situation; output of farm products, not only for cash
but for household use in the farm family and to provide food and drink for labour;receipts
in cash from the sale of farm products and for activities outside the farm and remittances
from members of the family at a distance; expenditures on farm and household purchases;
savings; equipment owned and capital formation generally. It is to be expected that for a
number of these items records may be scanty but workers in a number of situations have
found that with patience useful guidance can be obtained on all ofthem.
Approach to recording and farmer contact.
Labour is used on farms on most days ofthe year,or at least over a substantial part ofthe
season, and there may be numerous occasions when products are sold and purchases are


84 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
made. Consequently reliable records of this kind ofinformation cannot usually be obtained
by enquiry on a single occasion in the course of the year. Frequent recording is needed,
possibly more or less continuously. It cannot be said that there is clear agreement between
workers on the most effective approach to this work. There are reports of enquirers who
have sought to collect data relating to the whole of a year's use of labour on the farm by
questioning the farmer on a single occasion; others have visited the farm once a month;
others once a week;still others insist that only by detailed day-by-day,diary-type,recording
is it possible to be sure that the data are reliable. In part,these differences no doubt reflect
differences in the operating situation, for example if a farmer sells only on a single occasion in the year and then to a cooperative society, it is quite possible that he has documentary evidence to show how much his receipts were for that year. Even if he has no record,with
his permission the society is likely to be able to provide one. No doubt there is a certain
amount of difference between farmers in different localities in their ability to remember or in their possession of records which will provide the basic information needed by the
enquirer. Nonetheless, those who are coming to this kind of work for the first time should
give great weight to the view of those who say that only by careful recording will true data
emerge. For example, in obtaining information about how much time was spent by workers,
paid or family,in producing a particular crop,a complete record of what everyone was doing
who had any concern with that farm, each working day,is desirable. It has been found by
some workers that it is not sufficient to record what time the farmer or his helpers said they
spent on work on a particular field. If the enquiry is pushed on to ask how else they spent their day, the figures which would otherwise have been entered for the farm operations are likely to be changed. Only when the farm data are available as part of a complete record of the use of time will a reasonably true figure for labour input in the farming operations be obtained. Having said this it must be recognised that the survey director is likely to find
himself forced away from this ideal by the objection of the farmers to this degree of
cross-questioning. Nonetheless, taking one country's experience with another, it seems
highly desirable that at least a selection offarmers on a selection of days should be treated in
this way so that the enumerators and the enquiry directors learn the kind of uncertainties
about labour recording that are peculiar to their particular area.
Given that the approach and documentation has been settled,before starting in each area
a formal visit should be made to the chosen villages to explain the purpose ofthe enquiry to
ensure that there is no misunderstanding about the use to be made of the information
collected — so far as it is ever possible to ensure that there is no misunderstanding — and
about what the cooperating farmers are going to get out of it. Generally speaking, there
should be no promise of any kind made to them except, perhaps, that they will be given a
certificate saying that they have cooperated. It is a good idea to arrange a village or
community meeting for these explanations and for a senior officer in the unit directing the
enquiry to be present to give it status and to explain authoritatively what is intended.
The demands of this kind of work put very special responsibilities on the field staff
compared with the demands on those in area estimation and similar 'passage' work.They are
going to be in contact with the community,even if not with the same individuals, over a
period of perhaps a year or more and there is a very high premium on their tact and innate
decency. The degree of personal contact between the enumerator and the village people
could be considerable but it is desirable that contact should be confined to the actual
working sessions and that he should keep away at other times. The problems have been
noted already under Staff Control. Staff of dubious reliability under these various headings
are a grave risk, particularly if they are recruited locally. The ability is useful too, of promoting the kind of general conversation which will bring
out the required formation for the record without the use of rigorous questioning.
Staffing.
The amount of staff required for farm recording obviously varies somewhat with the
amount of work to be done and the amount of travelling. A rough indication might be given
rather as follows. If fairly continuous daily or alternate day recording of inputs, farm


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 85
operations, receipts, and expenditure, is required it is unlikely that one enumerator can
handle more than four or five farm families. If this work is to be kept under close
supervision and the data moved forward for tabulation on a strict timetable about one
supervisor per five enumerators will be needed. In a big enquiry one can reckon on needing probably one senior economist-statistician in charge of a survey which involves fifteen or twenty field staff. Obviously this may not be possible on occasion but,unless the work has
already been established as a routine, there are grave risks if some such concentration of
expert guidance is not available at least for some months after the start of an enquiry.The
statistician would, however, be concerned also with the office tabulation ofthe data and for
this purpose it might be wise to reckon on about one clerical officer on office tabulation for
every five enumerators.
Obviously there will be variation from these sorts of figures in the light of local
circumstances, but some such pattern as this is likely to emerge. Where it is not possible to
attain this concentration then it is likely to be safer to concentrate on getting a small
number of properly organised and controlled detailed farm records, and to collect more
broadly from the rest of the sample some indication of available resources,inputs,and so on.
This is likely to be more successful than attempting to cover detailed recording on a wider
range of farms and fail through incompleteness of supervision and lateness oftabulation.If
some rigorously supervised data on, for example, input/output relationships are available
from a small sample of farms it might be possible to cut costs and yet extend awareness of
the working conditions on the farms concerned by collecting data from a rather wider
sample by questioning farmers as intensively as the goodwill permits.
It is possible that where area estimation and similar enquiries are in progress the call-back
checks and general post-enumeration control for that enquiry might be carried out by staff
on intensive farm enquiries.
Recording techniques and problems
It is impossible to foresee all the problems that are likely to arise in work of this kind.
They run the full gamut of technical and human origins. However,there are a number of
points which it might be useful to note as pointers to the kind of things needing attention in particular cases.
If one of the objects of the enquiry is the determination of input/output relationships
from crops it is necessary, or at least highly desirable, to be able to associate the input of
each item with the field or crop on which it was used. Consequently,field records must have
a place for the individual plot identification to be entered and this must be readily available
in the field. A considerable aid to accuracy in recording is a good sketch map of the farm
concerned. If enumerators are well trained in this they can provide their own,but if they are
hesitant it would be preferable to pick the best of them at the work and get him to prepare
the maps for all his colleagues within reasonable range. To avoid errors in recording details
for a specific field, a stake with the number of the field is a considerable help and often
survey organisers have found that farmers have no objection to this.
Sales off the farm usually present relatively little difficulty if they go to centralised
buying points. Even if they go in small quantities locally, there may not be much of a problem provided that the recording programme provides for entries every couple of days,or
so. Consumption on the farm is likely to be less straightforward than might be hoped
initially. For instance, some crops are eaten on the farm before they are fit for harvest. This
is always an extremely difficult feature to deal with and the best that sethns possible is to ask
the farmer or worker who is being recorded to drop the remains of the food he has eaten
somewhere within sight and for the enumerator to go along and note them at intervals.
Obviously this is not a possibility for regular reporting but some insight into the amount of
green maize, for instance, eaten on the farms, might come from counting thc cobs thrown
away. Crops brought into the farm house for consumption also present problems because
only a part of them may come straight from the field to consumption without staying for a
while in store. The harvest of one day may be mixed with products brought in on other days
before the food is prepared thus losing their identity. If weighing is possible, well and good,


86 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
otherwise measuring may be possible with subsequent conversion, or the enumerator must
note the approximate size of the items concerned, match them with other material and
weigh these. The enumerator should be instructed to be present when produce is being
prepared or, if there is a daily pattern, when it is being brought in from the fields. If they
miss, they should ask the farm family to show them about the size of basket or size of roots concerned.
It may be difficult to transfer quantities into values for subsistence items:Provision may
need to be made for the purchase of samples of similar products in the markets of
neighbouring villages in order to get a price. This may be better done by the supervisor than by the enumerator.
It is risky to make any assumptions about the available labour on farms,or to ask farmers
what labour they have "available". It has been noted on many occasions that farmers are
operating on a scale which they could not possibly handle with their own family labour and
thus, therefore, must be hiring or otherwise controlling outside labour which they do not
mention in reply to an enquiry. In handling labour records it is important to keep a note of
the names of workers associated with a farm,if these are at all stable,and check the stated
labour use against these for consistancy. One must remember that time may mean very little
to people in some situations. "Minutes" and "hours" as units of time may have no
equivalent either in local language or local thinking. A hasty enumerator will get a figure
which reflects much more the kind of question he put to the informant than the actual
amount oftime that the man spent on the particularjob.
Whether recording labour or receipts or expenditure at short intervals,but not daily(for
instance weekly or twice a week), the record sheet should provide a space for an entry on
each day back to the one on which the last record was taken.It has been shown repeatedly
that people do not remember precisely what has occurred during a stated recent period.
They are inclined either to omit a day or to include with the total requested for the period
"since I was here last" an item for at least one day included in the previous records. It may be necessary to make fairly detailed records of what people do in their
non-farming time and to reach some decisions ori which of these activities,if any,ought to
be associated with the farm task. For example, if a worker goes to the dispensary in the
middle of a working day, should that be included as part of the labour use on that day?
Probably it should. Again, it has been found that in certain resettlement schemes relatives, who previously could have made ready contact with each other in their old community,now
find themselves separated and the women take time off deliberately to visit each other for social contact. This may not be thrown up without reasonably detailed time records. Is it
part of the productive time or not? Probably it is, if those procedures are going to be
customary. The cost of labour occasionally introduces a difficulty. If there is a daily
payment settled regularly this may not be a particular problem. However,on occasion there
seems to be a considerable interval between the time that the work is done and the time that
the payment is made and this both confuses the record and leaves some doubt as to whether
this alleged circumstance is concealing some other transaction. Again,the payment for group
labour may not be in cash but in food or drink.Some care may be needed to make sure that
purchases of material for group entertainment, or withdrawals from the home crop for this
purpose,are entered as a labour cost and not as family consumption.
The amount of recording of cash receipts and expenditure and the number of people who
are concerned may vary greatly with the situation. Sometimes the number of sales is very
small and it is easy to locate what happened to the funds. At other times,sales are going on
all the time and by more than one member of the family, perhaps there may be non-farm
activities bringing in money, and migrant workers from the family may be sending
remittances. It is at least possible that some of these will not be reported to the enumerator
in a proportion of the families. It may be possible to cross-check some of the transactions by
asking the farmer what he has sold, how much he got for it,and then what he did with the
receipts. If he then mentions purchases which add up to less than the stated receipts he is
questioned on the discrepancy. If he mentions items totalling more than the alleged sales
brought in, this may throw light on other sales which he had forgotten to mention. This sort


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 87
of cross-checking may be impracticable either because the necessary goodwill is lacking, or
simply because of the number of items concerned. Moreover the receipts may be coming to
the farmer and the expenditure may be largely the concern of the farmer's wife or wives and
two or more different people have to be questioned — and each may have certain
transactions he wishes to keep secret from the other.
A net excess of receipts over expenditure might, naively, be regarded as representing
savings. It is highly unlikely that at least in the earlier stages of an enquiry, any useful
information on savings will be obtained by direct questioning or even by comparisons ofthis
kind, The most that can generally be hoped for is that a few farmers will be cooperative
enough to give fairly complete information and thus give an insight into the way the
farm/family finances are likely to be run. More than one enquiry has suggested heavy drains
on savings throughout the period of the study but this probably merely represents
under-recording of receipts.Some of the cross-checking is spoilt by very long delays between
the transfer of goods and the subsequent settlement of the cash account. This may mean
that long after a farmer has purchased something he in fact pays out the money for it,or it
may mean that long after he has supplied someone with some farm product he receives
payment for it. In many instances, in the course of an enquiry lasting some weeks or months, new and
better information may be gathered as farmers gain confidence.It is desirable to have on the
daily record sheet spaces in which corrected information of this kind can be entered with a
note of explanation so that the main tabulations can be adjusted accordingly.
Studies for assessing fertiliser response on a field scale under normal agricultural practice
can follow exactly the same pattern as those for estimating yield per unit area in general
statistical enquiries, with one exception. Normally a design for this purpose would provide
for two sample units per crop within each sample village (or possibly within each sample
farm) and no particular attention would be given to their agricultural treatment.In order to
be able to assess efficiently the consequence of applying fertiliser(or any other identifiable
treatment) it is necessary that one of each pair be treated and the other untreated. If a
pre-enumeration survey can be arranged, then the fields can be stratified according to their
treatment and selection carried out within strata. Otherwise, extra fields will have to be
drawn for the fertiliser assessment study until "with" and "without" plots are available. The
results of such studies are usually very valuable modifiers of the guidance obtained from
field experiments.Two comments might be made,however.In the assessment of response on
farms it is usually only possible to compare "with" and "without"; it is not practicable to
distinguish different levels of"with".In many instances this covers the main area ofinterest.
Secondly, it may be necessary to draw the samples for comparison from different farmers,
within villages, because the farmers either use fertiliser on all their fields or they use it on
none. If this is the situation, the difference between the "with" and the "without" will
reflect a combination of fertiliser effect and "farmer competence" since some types of
farmer are more likely to use fertilisers than others.Something of the same effect may occur
even if both samples are drawn from within one farm because farmers are unlikely to choose
at random the fields on which they will use fertiliser.
Useful guidance on the state of an agricultural sector and its course of adjustment can
sometimes be obtained from the answers to questions seeking qualitative information.
Comparable guidance by way of analytical statistical studies might require very considerable
bodies of data. Such questions might concern— whether improved varieties or practices are in use.
— presence or absence ofspecific diseases.
— rotation length(in shifting rotations).
— how many months the family can live on their own staple food (indicating degree or
self-sufficiency) — what they would buy if they had a windfall profit or bonus. — what they can buy now that they could not 10 years ago. No doubt some of the records proposed here should ideally be compiled daily round the
year. Very few normal individuals will stand such intrusion for more than a few weeks.The


88 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
overall design must, therefore, provide for relaxing the questioning or ending it before crisis
tension is reached. As some guide it might be well to continue daily questioning on labour
use, budgets, etc., and assessment of quantities of produce brought into the household for
no more than one month, moving then to other households in the sample. Twice weekly
visits without interference with household practice might be possible through a- year. Of
course it may be possible to get away with heavier loads on the informants than this, but it is
important when approaching overload levels to give some thought to the prospects for the
next piece of statistical work attempted in the neighbourhood.


