XV PRICE STATISTICS
Introduction
Statistics of prices connected with the agricultural industry are extremely important to an
understanding of the working of the economy. It is more than likely that more insight into
the state of an agricultural industry could be obtained per unit ofexpenditure by enquiries
into prices than enquiries into many of the subjects discussed so far. Some degree of price
reporting is to be found in most countries, but it is often oddly superficial. Among the
deficiencies and elements of confusion are the following:- commodities are not fully defined;
the quantity units are uncertain; farmers may sell at the farm and the local market and in
some distant locality to which they have transported their produce;they may sell effectively
at retail, at wholesale and at a mixed-stage in between; there may be statutory fixed prices
which are not, in fact, adhered to. The quantities passing through particular markets
fluctuate sharply from one time of the year to another, which upsets reporting procedure.
Prices as quoted may be largely nominal because growers are tied to buyers by debts
incurred for goods supplied and consequently they do not get the market price. Amidst this
confustion it may not be clear for what purpose the statistics are wanted and hence what
criteria should be used in their choice. Furthermore, it is easy to get together a body of
material in the price field and unless some external agency demands critical scrutiny,these
price statistics will pass into the record with little comment.It is in the face of these kinds of
problems that improvement in price reporting has to be carried on.
There are usually a number of possible points along a distribution chain at which prices
might be assessed, always a large number of farm products which might be included and
usually a number of production requisites(such as fertilisers) which should be included,too.
For the purpose of this chapter, emphasis is placed on farm product prices at the first stage
where a reasonably stable and broad market is to be found. The matter of prices along a
distribution chain is dealt with in the following chapter on marketing. In other words,this
chapter is concerned with a regular routine of price reporting. However,though many of the
suggestions for reporting apply equally to retail prices these are not specifically dealt with in
either chapter because interest in retail prices in developing countries tends to centre on
labour relations and wage fixing in urban areas which is outside the main agricultural area of
interest. Index numbers derived from wholesale or retail prices are not included either,
though the price series prepared from the reports outlined in this chapter would be suitable
for the preparation of agricultural price index numbers if desired. With these many sources
of uncertainty and error attaching to price reporting it seems better to concentrate on
getting one series or group of series in a reasonably reliable form than to try to bring a whole
range of possible series up to a modest level of reliability. Suggestions are made below for
getting a general indication of relationships between different series for use where such
amplification of the basic series is essential.
A list of the main uses of price series seems a useful background to further discussion of
this subject. Among the uses for which statistics of prices are compiled might be included
a) To provide comparisons over time, place and, occasionally, form. Governments are
usually very much more concerned with trends in prices and relative trends in prices than
they are with the absolute levels.
b) To provide the basis for converting physical quantities produced or marketed to
valuation in money terms for the purposes of regional or national accounts. Occasionally
they may also be needed to supplement farm records in deriving estimates offarm income.
For these purposes prices of both farm products and farm requisites are required.


90 AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
c) For market guidance in current trading operations. Thus traders are usually interested
in price reports to give some general indication of-the state ofthe market in places to which
they have no direct access.
d) To give an indication whether the price which a farmer obtained from a dealer in his
recent transaction was in fact a fair one in the light of general market conditions at the time.
e) As the basis for contracts where it is customary for the price of a commodity sold
under contract to be linked to current market prices.
These various needs represent very different demands on the statistical service. Thus a)
requires great consistency in definition and other details of reporting if the comparisons are
to be valid. On the other hand, the general level of the prices reported need not be precise
nor need the quotations be frequent.Even monthly prices are often quite suitable for giving
inter-country comparisons or time series for general assessment purposes. Often the same
applies to b)though in principle it might be desirable to have the figures at the right level.
However, there are usually so many conventions in accounting work of this kind that
adjustments may be incorporated without too much difficulty. c),d)and e)all demand that
the prices as quoted shall correspond to the actual commercial transactions in the markets
and be recognisable as realistic by those trading as buyers or sellers. Moreover,where trading
is continuous, and consequently prices may be of interest at any time,at least daily'reports
are usually necessary. These two groups of figures are orientated in such different directions
that a good case can be made for organising their collection by different staff. This is not to
say that the two are not handled in many countries by the same agencies but,nonetheless,
the outlook required in order to be thoroughly successful in the one is rather different from
that required for success in the other.Those collecting statistics for a)and b)are senfing the
statistical department; those collecting statistics for c)and d)are serving those operating in a
marketing field. In big centres,where it is possible to separate these functions,there is much
to be said for doing so, even if both sets of staff are employed by a statistics department.In
some ways there might be little loss if those providing daily reports were members of a
marketing department. However, if there is this dichotomy, a regular practice should be
instituted of checking the indications from a selection of the daily reports against the
indications of the reports for a) and b) and generally holding a postmortem on the comparison.
The main ways of obtaining statistics of prices of farm products and/or farm requisites
a) from farm recording studies usually by dividing the values involved by the quantities
rather than by direct noting of prices at the time;
b) from reports obtained from farmers about prevailing prices in the course of general
statistical passages for physical recording work;
c)by observation and discussion at markets or with traders;
d)from schedules offixed buying or selling prices ofsome kind of statutory organisation;
e)from private trading price lists;
f) from marketing boards and other semi-statutory organisations which keep their own statistics.
The bulk of such work uses methods c) to f) and consequently usually requires staff in
quite different places from those where the main recording of physical statistics takes place.
Market reporting tends to be concentrated in the big towns.It may be possible to mix price
reporting with other kinds of statistical work but it is usually better to assign staff to specialising in price work,perhaps combining it with office work on general statistics.
Reporting procedure
Proceeding now to the outline of a reporting procedure,it is probably best to review the
main characteristics of the commodity field which has to be covered and the markets
through which the produce is disposed of. The first need is to select first-hand selling points
which are representative of a substantial proportion of the trade. Where it is customary for
farmers to take their produce to a market centre and there sell in fair sized quantities to
first-hand buyers, there may be little difficulty in making a choice. Where it is customary for
are


AGRICULTURAL STATISTICS FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES 91
producers to sell to travelling buyers who act as collectors for larger buyers in market
centres, a difficulty is introduced, since the small itinerant buyers are probably not good
sources of price reports. Under these conditions the best approach may be to split the
reporting. One group of staffshould concentrate on the normal reporting at the main market
centres, while the other does as good ajob as is feasible in collecting reports ofthe prices at
which they have sold by direct enquiry from farmers. Reports of this kind should also
indicate the kind of trader or other buyer to whom the sale is made.
A standard day for the report should also be chosen. Thisis usually better as a day ofthe
week rather than as dates in the month.The activity of many markets varies quite markedly
with the day ofthe week and consequently there is some risk that if the chosen date falls on
a different day of the week, fluctuations in reports will appear which have no useful indicative value.
Amongst the markets which correspond to the stage of first-hand selling required, a choice should be made of those most suited for the purpose in hand. This choice should
depend on such features as the commodities on offer and the variety,and also on the size of the market and its consistency over time. It is also important to define precisely what is to be regarded as the boundaries of the market for reporting purposes. Sometimes in a city there are a number of markets with slightly different characteristics,and the ones to be used for reporting should be clearly defined. Even within a market of this kind, however, and
even where a physical market area is provided,there may be firms on the fringe who handle
a substantial quantity of the produce. If these are to be included then the fact should be
built-in to the reporting system.
A practical approach to this may be to determine, in the light of local information,
whether the trader, or traders, concerned handles the same kind of produce from the same
kinds of producer as the main market place. If there is no clear separation of trade then it
might be better to confine reporting to the market place unless the firm is notably
cooperative. It is usually easier to maintain consistency in reports from a big market than
from un-cooperative traders,treated more-or-less as individuals.
Markets customarily vary in their activity and price level over the day. Some can be
regarded as opening at a price related to the previous day's trading and closing at a price
relating to the supply and demand situation for that day. However, in many up-country
markets business may trail off during the day so that no clearly defined closing price can be
discerned. In other markets,trading may go on virtually round-the-clock.In still others,each
transaction is a separate transaction and its level depends very considerably on the haggling skill of the parties. Generally speaking,it seems best to review current practice and to decide
on the time of the day when the peak trading occurs. This may vary from season to season,
and should be locally known.Reports are best quoted as the mostfrequent price for trading
within the peak trading period.
Since the aim is to secure comparability of price reporting, the need to report price of
"the same thing" on each occasion is clear. Unfortunately, it is not by any means obvious
what is to be regarded as "the same thing". The same variety of a crop may not be available
all the year around; or even if the same variety is available all year around it may represent a
very different position in the hierarchy of users' preference scale at one season than at
another. Though real enough,this latter aspect is difficult to reflect and the best approach is one directed to using as nearly as possible the same variety throughout the year. For this
purpose it is desirable to consult the technical experts before reporting is begun to find out
in which periods of the year different varieties can be expected to be on offer. One that is
available for most of the year should be chosen as the basis for reporting, but one or more
other varieties should be selected to cover the periods when the first is not available. The
reports should be collected for the standard variety for each day that reasonable trade is
carried on, but for periods overlapping the end and beginning ofthe main season by two to
four weeks reports should also be obtained for the secondary varieties. In all instances the
quotation should refer to "fair average quality". Some care may be needed to get this idea
into the minds of the reporters and it may be necessary to bring together a selection of
produce from various points and indicate the type of goods corresponding to the desired standard of quality.